// C/P Practice Test flashcards: original stand-alone cards that teach what the Chem/Phys questions of AAMC Practice
// Exams 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 need (the pe1-cp … pe6-cp cards of data/qb-aamc-fl.js). TODAY's C/P flashcard step uses only
// these cards (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js).
// Every card names, in `from`, the Practice Exam card(s) whose question needs it; `aamc` and `disc` come from that card.
// Original wording, numbers and compounds only: no AAMC passage, question, number or answer choice is reproduced.
// Format: {id, s: topic tag, disc, aamc, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, from: [src, …], how: {ask, know, steps, trap}, teach}
// One card per method: a card lists in `from` every spec item (and so every question) it prepares for, across the
// exams; the whole deck stays at 250 cards or fewer (tools/test-cp-test-cards.cjs).
const CP_TEST_CARDS = [
  // ── Practice Exam 1 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-03a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:3#1","pe1-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:46#1"],"q":"A TLC baseline is 1.5 cm above the plate's lower edge. Measured from that edge, the solvent front is at 9.5 cm and a spot's center is at 5.5 cm. What is the retention factor (Rf)?","choices":["0.42","0.50","0.58","2.0"],"ans":1,"exp":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance, both from the baseline.** Spot: 5.5 − 1.5 = 4.0 cm. Front: 9.5 − 1.5 = 8.0 cm. Rf = 4.0 ÷ 8.0 = **0.50**.","how":{"ask":"Measure both distances from the baseline, then divide.","know":"**Rf = distance moved by the compound ÷ distance moved by the solvent front**, both measured from the **baseline** where the sample was spotted. Rf is between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"The distances are given from the edge, but the sample started at the baseline.","hl":"baseline is 1.5 cm above the plate's lower edge","fig":"cpt-tlc-plate"},{"text":"Subtract the baseline from both readings.","calc":["spot: 5.5 − 1.5 = **4.0 cm**","front: 9.5 − 1.5 = **8.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["Rf = 4.0 ÷ 8.0 = **0.50**"]},{"text":"0.58 skips the baseline (5.5 ÷ 9.5), 0.42 subtracts it from only the spot, and 2.0 is inverted.","strike":["0.58","0.42","2.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.50."}],"trap":"Dividing the raw readings (5.5 ÷ 9.5 = 0.58): both distances must start at the baseline."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 cm","note":"where the sample started"},{"t":"9.5 cm","note":"solvent front, from the edge"},{"t":"5.5 cm","note":"spot, from the edge"}]},{"text":"**Start at the baseline.** The race starts where the sample was spotted, not at the plate's edge.","calc":["spot: 5.5 − 1.5 = 4.0 cm","front: 9.5 − 1.5 = 8.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Divide spot by front.**","calc":["Rf = 4.0 ÷ 8.0 = 0.50"]},{"text":"**Also:** on silica, the compound with the strongest polar groups (O–H or N–H first) has the smallest Rf."},{"text":"**Also:** a low Rf means strong binding to the stationary phase; a high Rf means the compound prefers the mobile phase."},{"text":"**Also:** only N–H, O–H and F–H donate hydrogen bonds; C=O and ether oxygens can only accept them."},{"text":"**Also:** in chromatography, Rf is characteristic of the compound under fixed conditions; spot size only reflects how much was applied."},{"text":"**Also:** every Rf lies between 0 and 1; a larger value means the ratio is inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** sugar-based column materials (polysaccharides such as dextran, agarose and cellulose) are covered in –OH groups, so they interact with proteins mainly by hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline), and it always falls between 0 and 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-02a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:2#1"],"q":"A team concludes that amino acids arose on the primitive Earth, since passing sparks through methane and ammonia gave them in the lab. The argument requires that:","choices":["those gases were present then.","the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."],"ans":0,"exp":"A lab synthesis shows what **can** happen. To conclude it **did** happen on the early Earth, the **starting materials and conditions** (here methane, ammonia and electrical discharge) must have existed then. Yield, purity and a claim about all reactions do not bear on that.","how":{"ask":"Find the unstated premise that must be true for the lab result to support the historical claim.","know":"• **Assumption**: a premise the conclusion needs but does not state; if it were false, the conclusion would fail.\n• A lab model of a past event supports the claim only if its **inputs and conditions** existed at that time.","steps":[{"text":"The conclusion is about the early Earth; the evidence is from a lab.","hl":"amino acids arose on the primitive Earth"},{"text":"Test each choice: if it were false, would the argument fail?","calc":["gases absent then → the lab result says nothing about then → fails","low yield → some still formed → stands"]},{"text":"Yield, optical purity and all reactions are not needed for the conclusion.","strike":["the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."]},{"text":"Answer: the gases must have been present on the early Earth."}],"trap":"Choosing a detail about the experiment's quality; the gap is between the lab and the early Earth."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"on the primitive Earth","note":"the conclusion"},{"t":"in the lab","note":"the evidence"},{"t":"requires","note":"find the assumption"}]},{"text":"**Split conclusion from evidence.** The evidence is a lab result; the conclusion is about the past."},{"text":"**Bridge the gap.** For the lab to stand in for the early Earth, the same ingredients had to be there."},{"text":"**Negate to test.** If the gases were absent, the argument collapses, so that is the assumption.","calc":["negate the choice → does the conclusion fail?"]},{"text":"**Also:** an assumption is the unstated premise whose denial would break the conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lab model supports a claim about the past only if its starting materials and conditions existed then."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-04e","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:4#1","pe1-cp:4#3","pe1-cp:4#4","pe1-cp:4#2"],"q":"Which statement about the acid-catalyzed dehydration of 1-methylcyclopentanol is true?","choices":["One water is lost, via a carbocation","Two waters are lost in one step","The alkene geometry is set by the alcohol","The C–H bond breaks before the C–O bond"],"ans":0,"exp":"The OH is protonated and leaves as **one water molecule**, giving a **planar carbocation**; then H⁺ is lost from a neighboring carbon. Because the flat cation forgets the alcohol's 3D arrangement, the reaction is **not stereospecific**, and the C–O bond breaks first (it is E1, not a concerted E2).","how":{"ask":"Judge each statement against the E1 mechanism: what leaves, in what order, through what intermediate.","know":"• **E1 dehydration**: ROH + H⁺ → ROH₂⁺ → **R⁺ + H₂O** (one water) → alkene + H⁺.\n• The C–O bond breaks **first**; the C–H bond breaks last.\n• The carbocation is planar, so the alcohol's geometry is lost: **not stereospecific**.","steps":[{"text":"Write the E1 steps for this tertiary alcohol.","hl":"acid-catalyzed dehydration","calc":["R–OH + H⁺ → R–OH₂⁺","R–OH₂⁺ → **R⁺ + H₂O**","R⁺ → alkene + H⁺"]},{"text":"Only one water molecule leaves, and the steps are separate, not one concerted step.","strike":["Two waters are lost in one step"]},{"text":"The planar cation does not remember the alcohol's arrangement, so the alcohol does not set the geometry.","strike":["The alkene geometry is set by the alcohol"]},{"text":"Water leaves before H⁺ is removed, so the C–O bond breaks first.","strike":["The C–H bond breaks before the C–O bond"]},{"text":"Answer: one water is lost, via a carbocation."}],"trap":"Treating dehydration like E2 (one step, stereospecific): with acid and heat, alcohols go through a carbocation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acid-catalyzed dehydration","note":"E1"},{"t":"1-methylcyclopentanol","note":"tertiary alcohol"}]},{"text":"**Count the waters.** One OH leaves as one H₂O."},{"text":"**Order the bonds.** C–O breaks to make C⁺; the C–H breaks after.","calc":["C–O first → C⁺ → C–H last"]},{"text":"**Flat cation, no memory.** The planar C⁺ can lose an H from either side, so the starting geometry does not carry over."},{"text":"**Also:** in E1, the C=C always forms between the cationic carbon and a carbon directly bonded to it."},{"text":"**Also:** a direct E1 alkene always has the former C–OH carbon in its C=C."},{"text":"**Also:** E1 through a planar carbocation gives both E and Z alkenes, with the more stable E usually major."},{"text":"**Also:** tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily because they form the most stable carbocation; primary alcohols dehydrate slowest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid-catalyzed dehydration loses one water through a carbocation, so it is stepwise and not stereospecific."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-05a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:27#3"],"q":"The activated carboxyl group of glycine reacts with the amino group of valine, releasing one water molecule. Which linkage forms?","choices":["Amide","Ester","Anhydride","Ether"],"ans":0,"exp":"Carboxyl + **amine** → **amide** (a peptide bond), with loss of H₂O. Carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester; carboxyl + carboxyl gives an anhydride; an ether needs two alcohols.","how":{"ask":"Name the bond made when a carboxyl group condenses with an amine.","know":"• **Condensation** joins two groups and releases H₂O.\n• RCOOH + R′NH₂ → **amide** (RCONHR′); RCOOH + R′OH → ester; RCOOH + RCOOH → anhydride.\n• Between amino acids the amide is the **peptide bond**.","steps":[{"text":"The partner is the amino group, an amine.","hl":"amino group of valine"},{"text":"Ester and ether need an alcohol O; an anhydride needs a second carboxyl group.","strike":["Ester","Ether","Anhydride"]},{"text":"Answer: an amide (peptide) bond."}],"trap":"Choosing ester because water is lost; esters also lose water, but only when the partner is an alcohol."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"carboxyl group","note":"acyl donor"},{"t":"amino group","note":"N nucleophile"},{"t":"releasing one water","note":"condensation"}]},{"text":"**Match the partner.** The nucleophile here is nitrogen, from the amine."},{"text":"**Build the bond.** The carboxyl's carbonyl carbon bonds to the other amino acid's N (C–N).","calc":["R–COOH + H₂N–R′ → R–CO–NH–R′ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** carboxyl + amine → amide (peptide bond); carboxyl + alcohol → ester."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-06a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:6#1"],"q":"Which statement gives the order of SN2 reaction rates for alkyl halides with the same leaving group?","choices":["Methyl fastest, tertiary slowest","Tertiary fastest, methyl slowest","Secondary fastest, methyl slowest","Primary fastest, tertiary slowest"],"ans":0,"exp":"SN2 needs **backside attack** on the carbon bearing the leaving group. Each added alkyl group crowds that side: **methyl > 1° > 2° ≫ 3°** (tertiary essentially does not react by SN2).","how":{"ask":"Rank substrates by how crowded the backside of the reacting carbon is.","know":"• **SN2**: one step, the nucleophile attacks opposite the leaving group.\n• Steric order: **methyl > primary > secondary ≫ tertiary**.\n• The reverse order (3° fastest) belongs to SN1, which goes through a carbocation.","steps":[{"text":"SN2 rate depends on access to the carbon's backside.","hl":"order of SN2 reaction rates","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Tertiary-first is the SN1 order; secondary-first fits neither.","strike":["Tertiary fastest, methyl slowest","Secondary fastest, methyl slowest"]},{"text":"Primary is fast but methyl, with no alkyl groups, is faster still.","strike":["Primary fastest, tertiary slowest"]},{"text":"Answer: methyl fastest, tertiary slowest."}],"trap":"Using carbocation stability (3° > 2° > 1°); that ranks SN1 rates, not SN2."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"SN2","note":"backside attack, one step"},{"t":"same leaving group","note":"only sterics differ"}]},{"text":"**Picture the attack.** The nucleophile must reach the carbon from behind the leaving group."},{"text":"**Count the blockers.** Each alkyl group on that carbon gets in the way.","calc":["CH₃–X: 0 → fastest","1°: 1","2°: 2","3°: 3 → no SN2"]},{"text":"**Also:** SN2 slows with crowding at the carbon being attacked (methyl > 1° > 2°, no 3°), not at the leaving group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 rates run methyl > 1° > 2° ≫ 3°, the reverse of SN1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-07b","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:7#2","pe1-cp:7#1","pe1-cp:7#3"],"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol reacts with HBr. Which product can form without a methyl shift in the carbocation?","choices":["2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane","2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene","2,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene"],"ans":0,"exp":"Loss of water from C2 leaves a **secondary cation on C2**. Br⁻ adding there gives **2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane** directly. C3 is quaternary (no H), so a **1,2-methyl shift** from C3 makes a 3° cation at C3; that cation gives 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane, 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene and 2,3-dimethylbut-1-ene.","how":{"ask":"Find the product whose new group sits on the carbon that held the OH, with the carbon skeleton unchanged.","know":"• C⁺ forms where the OH was; Br⁻ there = direct product.\n• A methyl shift moves C⁺ to the next carbon and rearranges the skeleton (the methyl changes carbons).\n• Products with Br on a carbon that never held the OH, a C=C that does not include that carbon, or a changed branching pattern come from the shifted cation.","steps":[{"text":"The OH is on C2; C3 carries two methyls and no H.","hl":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"Direct path: Br⁻ adds to C2, keeping both methyls on C3.","calc":["CH₃–CH(Br)–C(CH₃)₂–CH₃ = **2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane**"]},{"text":"A 2,3-dimethyl skeleton means one methyl moved from C3 to C2.","strike":["2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene","2,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane."}],"trap":"Choosing an alkene because elimination seems \"direct\"; here every listed alkene has the 2,3-dimethyl skeleton of the shifted cation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol","note":"OH on C2, quaternary C3"},{"t":"HBr","note":"cation path"},{"t":"without a methyl shift","note":"direct product"}]},{"text":"**Place the cation.** C⁺ forms on C2, where the OH was."},{"text":"**Compare skeletons.** Before the shift: two methyls on C3. After: one on C2 and one on C3.","calc":["3,3-dimethyl → unshifted","2,3-dimethyl → shifted"]},{"text":"**Pick the unshifted one.** Br on C2 with the 3,3-dimethyl skeleton."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbocation shifts H or CH₃ from a neighboring carbon whenever that makes a more stable cation."},{"text":"**Also:** a new group on a carbon that never held the leaving group is the fingerprint of a carbocation rearrangement."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a product whose groups or branches moved to a carbon that never held the OH came from a rearranged cation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-08d","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:8#3","pe1-cp:8#1","pe1-cp:8#2","pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"q":"A mixture of pent-1-ene, 1-chloropentane and pentan-1-ol is run on a nonpolar gas chromatography column. In what order do they reach the detector?","choices":["Alkene, then chloride, then alcohol","Alcohol, then chloride, then alkene","Chloride, then alkene, then alcohol","Alkene, then alcohol, then chloride"],"ans":0,"exp":"Lowest boiling point first. Pent-1-ene has only London forces (bp ≈ 30 °C). 1-Chloropentane adds a C–Cl dipole and more mass (bp ≈ 108 °C). Pentan-1-ol **hydrogen-bonds** (bp ≈ 138 °C), so it comes out **last**.","how":{"ask":"Rank the three by intermolecular forces, which sets their boiling points and so their GC order.","know":"• GC elutes in order of increasing boiling point.\n• Alkene: London only → most volatile.\n• Alkyl halide: dipole–dipole plus London → middle.\n• Alcohol: hydrogen bonding → least volatile, last.","steps":[{"text":"All three have five carbons, so the functional group decides.","hl":"pent-1-ene, 1-chloropentane and pentan-1-ol","calc":["pent-1-ene: London → bp ≈ 30 °C","1-chloropentane: dipole → bp ≈ 108 °C","pentan-1-ol: H-bonding → bp ≈ 138 °C"]},{"text":"The alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it cannot come out first or second.","strike":["Alcohol, then chloride, then alkene","Alkene, then alcohol, then chloride"]},{"text":"The alkene is more volatile than the chloride, so it comes out before it.","strike":["Chloride, then alkene, then alcohol"]},{"text":"Answer: alkene, then chloride, then alcohol."}],"trap":"Ordering by molar mass alone: the alcohol is lighter than the chloride but comes out after it, because it hydrogen-bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"nonpolar gas chromatography column","note":"order by boiling point"},{"t":"reach the detector","note":"elution order"}]},{"text":"**Name each force.** C=C: London only. C–Cl: a dipole. O–H: hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Weakest forces leave first.**","calc":["alkene < alkyl halide < alcohol"]},{"text":"**Also:** in GC, compounds come off in order of increasing boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** with similar sizes, an alkane boils lowest and an alcohol highest, because hydrogen bonding is the strongest force."},{"text":"**Also:** an O–H or N–H group raises the boiling point far more than added mass does, because of hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** fractional distillation separates liquids by the same property, boiling point. An alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it boils higher than an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, and the lower-boiling liquid distills over first.","calc":["dipropyl ether or 1-chlorohexane vs hexan-1-ol","ether / chloride: lower bp → collects first","alcohol: H-bonding, higher bp → stays behind"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in GC, an alkene beats an alkyl halide of the same chain, and an alcohol, which hydrogen-bonds, comes out last."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-09b","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:9#1","pe1-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:9#3"],"q":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane reacts by SN2 with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa. What configuration does the product have?","choices":["(S) only","(R) only","Racemic mixture","Meso compound"],"ans":0,"exp":"SN2 gives **inversion**. The –SCH₃ group (first atom S, 16) now ranks **first**, just as Cl did, and the order of the others (ethyl > methyl > H) is unchanged. Inverting the 3D arrangement with the same priority order flips the label: **R → S**.","how":{"ask":"Apply inversion, then check whether the priorities changed.","know":"• **SN2 → inversion** of the 3D arrangement; no carbocation, so no racemization.\n• **CIP priority** by atomic number of the first atom: S (16) and Cl (17) both outrank C (6) and H (1).\n• If the new group has the same rank as the old one, inversion switches R ↔ S.","steps":[{"text":"The reaction is SN2, so the configuration inverts.","hl":"reacts by SN2","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Priorities before and after.","calc":["before: Cl > ethyl > methyl > H","after: **SCH₃** > ethyl > methyl > H"]},{"text":"Same order, inverted geometry, so the label changes; SN2 does not racemize, and the product has one stereocenter.","strike":["(R) only","Racemic mixture","Meso compound"]},{"text":"Answer: (S) only."}],"trap":"Keeping the label R: inversion with unchanged priorities always switches the descriptor."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane","note":"starting label"},{"t":"SN2","note":"inversion"},{"t":"CH₃SNa","note":"S nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Flip the umbrella.** The nucleophile enters opposite Cl; the other three groups turn inside out."},{"text":"**Re-rank.** S still outranks every carbon, just as Cl did.","calc":["rank order unchanged → R becomes S"]},{"text":"**Also:** CIP priority is set by the first atom's atomic number; go farther out only to break a tie."},{"text":"**Also:** after a substitution, the product keeps the substrate's carbon chain and the new group's position; only the group's name changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 inverts the center; if the entering group takes the leaving group's priority rank, R and S swap."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:10#1","pe1-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:10#3","pe3-cp:10#1"],"q":"In the acid-catalyzed esterification of acetic acid with ethanol, the oxygen joining the acyl carbon to the ethyl group comes from:","choices":["the ethanol","the acetic acid","the acid catalyst","the water"],"ans":0,"exp":"In Fischer esterification the **alcohol's oxygen is the nucleophile**: it attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon. Later an –OH from the **acid** leaves as water. So the bridging –O– of ethyl acetate is the **ethanol** oxygen.","how":{"ask":"Identify the nucleophile, whose oxygen is kept in the product.","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O (acid catalyst).\n• The **alcohol O attacks** the carbonyl carbon; **water leaves from the acid** side.\n• So the ester's C–O–R′ oxygen comes from the alcohol.","steps":[{"text":"The bridging oxygen links the acyl carbon to the ethyl group.","hl":"the oxygen joining the acyl carbon to the ethyl group"},{"text":"The acid loses one of its oxygens as water; the catalyst only adds and removes H⁺.","strike":["the acetic acid","the acid catalyst","the water"]},{"text":"Answer: the ethanol."}],"trap":"Assuming the acid keeps both oxygens; one of them leaves in the water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acid-catalyzed esterification","note":"Fischer"},{"t":"ethanol","note":"the nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Activate.** H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic."},{"text":"**Attack and leave.**","calc":["EtO–H attacks C → tetrahedral intermediate","acid's –OH → –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in Fischer esterification the ester's bridging oxygen comes from the alcohol, its C=O oxygen from the acid, and the water from the acid."},{"text":"**Also:** an H on O or N exchanges rapidly with solvent water (faster with acid or base), so it cannot keep a label, so it cannot show which bond broke."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in Fischer esterification the alcohol keeps its oxygen as the ester's –O–, and the acid loses an oxygen as water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-11a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:11#1","pe1-cp:11#2","pe1-cp:11#3","pe1-cp:45#1","pe1-cp:45#2"],"q":"A camera lens forms a sharp image of a flower 5.0 cm behind the lens when the flower is 20 cm in front. What is the focal length in metres?","choices":["0.040 m","4.0 m","0.067 m","25 m"],"ans":0,"exp":"Convert first: o = 0.20 m and i = 0.050 m. The **thin-lens equation** adds the reciprocals: 1/f = 5 m⁻¹ + 20 m⁻¹ = 25 m⁻¹, so **f = 0.040 m** (4.0 cm).","how":{"ask":"Use the thin-lens equation with both distances converted to metres, then invert to get f.","know":"• **Thin-lens equation: 1/f = 1/o + 1/i** (o = object distance, i = image distance, f = focal length).\n• Work in **metres**: 1 cm = 0.01 m.\n• The equation gives 1/f; flip the result to get f.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two distances and convert them to metres.","hl":"5.0 cm behind the lens","calc":["i = 5.0 cm = **0.050 m**","o = 20 cm = **0.20 m**"]},{"text":"Add the reciprocals.","hl":"20 cm in front","calc":["1/f = 1/o + 1/i","1/f = 1/0.20 m + 1/0.050 m","1/f = 5 m⁻¹ + 20 m⁻¹ = **25 m⁻¹**"]},{"text":"Invert to get the focal length.","calc":["f = 1 ÷ 25 m⁻¹ = **0.040 m**"]},{"text":"25 m forgets to invert 1/f. 0.067 m subtracts the reciprocals (20 − 5 = 15 m⁻¹). 4.0 m does the arithmetic in centimetres (f = 4.0 cm) and then labels it metres.","strike":["25 m","0.067 m","4.0 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.040 m."}],"trap":"Doing the sum in centimetres gives f = 4.0 cm; that is right in cm, but 4.0 m is a hundred times too large. Convert to metres before you start."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it measures.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 cm behind the lens","note":"image distance i"},{"t":"20 cm in front","note":"object distance o"},{"t":"in metres","note":"unit of the answer"}]},{"text":"**The thin-lens equation** links the three distances, each measured from the lens.","calc":["1/f = 1/o + 1/i"]},{"text":"**Convert to metres first,** so 1/f comes out in m⁻¹.","calc":["o = 0.20 m → 1/o = 5 m⁻¹","i = 0.050 m → 1/i = 20 m⁻¹"]},{"text":"**Add, then flip.**","calc":["1/f = 25 m⁻¹","f = 0.040 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** power in diopters = 1 ÷ (focal length in metres); converging lenses are +, diverging lenses are −."},{"text":"**Also:** in any eye problem, the image distance is the lens-to-retina distance; the object distance is lens-to-object."},{"text":"**Also:** solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| scales the height and a negative m means inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** for an object beyond 2f, find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i and use m = −i/o: the real image is inverted and smaller, shrinking as the object moves away."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert o and i to metres, add their reciprocals to get 1/f, and remember to invert at the end if the question asks for f."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#1","pe1-cp:12#3","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"q":"A protease binds the tetrahedral intermediate of peptide hydrolysis much more tightly than it binds the intact peptide. How does this binding speed the reaction?","choices":["Lowers activation energy","Makes the reaction exergonic","Shifts equilibrium to products","Raises the temperature"],"ans":0,"exp":"The tetrahedral intermediate resembles the **transition state**. Binding it tightly **stabilizes the transition state**, so the top of the energy hill drops and the **activation energy (Ea)** falls. Reactant and product energies are untouched, so ΔG and the equilibrium stay the same, and an enzyme does not heat the solution.","how":{"ask":"Decide which part of the energy diagram the enzyme changes when it grips the transition-state-like species.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy**; a lower Ea means a faster reaction.\n• An enzyme binds the **transition state** more tightly than the substrate, lowering the peak of the curve.\n• **ΔH (or ΔG) = products − reactants**; Ea = transition state − reactants. The enzyme does not move either end, so ΔG and K are unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme grips the species that looks like the transition state, not the starting peptide.","hl":"binds the tetrahedral intermediate of peptide hydrolysis much more tightly than it binds the intact peptide"},{"text":"Stabilizing the transition state lowers the peak; read both barriers from the curves.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide hydrolysis with and without the protease","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]},"calc":["Ea without enzyme = 90 − 0 = **90 kJ/mol**","Ea with protease = 50 − 0 = **50 kJ/mol**","ΔG = −30 − 0 = **−30 kJ/mol** on both curves"]},{"text":"Both curves start and end at the same heights, so ΔG and the position of equilibrium are unchanged.","strike":["Makes the reaction exergonic","Shifts equilibrium to products"]},{"text":"An enzyme works at the temperature of its surroundings; it does not heat them.","strike":["Raises the temperature"]},{"text":"Answer: it lowers the activation energy."}],"trap":"Thinking a faster reaction must also be a more favorable one: speed depends on Ea, favorability on ΔG, and an enzyme changes only Ea."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"tetrahedral intermediate","note":"looks like the transition state"},{"t":"much more tightly","note":"preferential binding"},{"t":"speed the reaction","note":"a rate question"}]},{"text":"**Read the energy diagram.** The left plateau is the reactants, the right plateau the products, the peak the transition state. The enzyme's curve has a lower peak but the same ends.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same reaction, two paths","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]}},{"text":"**Measure the hill.** Ea is peak minus start.","calc":["no enzyme: 90 − 0 = 90 kJ/mol","with protease: 50 − 0 = 50 kJ/mol","barrier is 40 kJ/mol lower → faster"]},{"text":"**Why tight binding of the transition state works:** the binding energy is released at the peak, which pulls the peak down."},{"text":"**Also:** catalysis needs the transition state stabilized more than the substrate; extra substrate binding alone raises the barrier."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme speeds a reaction by stabilizing the transition state, which lowers Ea; it never changes the start or end energies."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#2","pe1-cp:50#1"],"q":"An uncatalyzed isomerization has equilibrium constant K at 30 °C. A catalyst is added and the temperature is held constant. Which property of the reaction changes?","choices":["Equilibrium constant","Standard free energy","Time to equilibrium","Equilibrium yield"],"ans":2,"exp":"A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward **and** reverse reactions by the same amount, so equilibrium is reached **sooner**. It does not change the energies of reactants or products, so **ΔG°**, **K** (still 6) and the amount of product at equilibrium all stay the same.","how":{"ask":"Separate the properties set by the start and end energies from the one set by the height of the barrier.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K**: both depend only on reactant and product energies.\n• A catalyst lowers Ea for **both directions** equally, so forward and reverse rates rise by the same factor.\n• Same K → same equilibrium mixture; only the **speed of getting there** changes.","steps":[{"text":"The temperature is fixed, so K can change only if ΔG° changes.","hl":"the temperature is held constant"},{"text":"Compare the two paths: the ends sit at the same heights; only the peak is lower.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Isomerization with and without the catalyst","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,35],[5,70],[7,20],[9,-20],[10,-20]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,15],[5,40],[7,0],[9,-20],[10,-20]]}]},"calc":["ΔG° = −20 − 0 = **−20 kJ/mol** on both curves","Ea forward: 70 → 40 kJ/mol","Ea reverse: 70 − (−20) = 90 → 40 − (−20) = 60 kJ/mol","both barriers drop by **30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Same ΔG° means the same K and the same mixture at equilibrium.","strike":["Equilibrium constant","Standard free energy","Equilibrium yield"]},{"text":"Answer: the time to equilibrium, which becomes shorter."}],"trap":"Thinking a catalyst pushes the reaction toward products: it speeds the reverse reaction just as much, so the final mixture is the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"equilibrium constant K","note":"set by ΔG°"},{"t":"A catalyst is added","note":"lowers Ea"},{"t":"held constant","note":"no temperature effect"}]},{"text":"**Two different questions.** \"How far?\" is answered by ΔG° and K. \"How fast?\" is answered by Ea."},{"text":"**Link ΔG° and K.** At a fixed temperature they move together, and both depend only on the ends of the diagram.","calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K","same ends → same ΔG° → same K"]},{"text":"**Both directions speed up.** The catalyst lowers the forward and reverse barriers equally, so the ratio of the forward and reverse rate constants (k_f/k_r = K) is unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** an inseparable homogeneous catalyst contaminates the product; a catalyst never shifts the equilibrium."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst changes only how fast equilibrium is reached, never ΔG°, K or the equilibrium mixture."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-13a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:13#1","pe1-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:13#3","pe1-cp:13#4"],"q":"A 0.20 M solution of formic acid is prepared. Adding which substance would lower the percent ionization of the formic acid?","choices":["Nitric acid","Pure water","Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"],"ans":0,"exp":"HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻. Nitric acid is a **strong acid**, so it floods the solution with **H⁺, an ion already in the equilibrium**. By **Le Châtelier** the equilibrium shifts **left** and a smaller fraction of formic acid ionizes (the **common-ion effect**). Water dilutes the acid, and the two bases remove H⁺; all three raise ionization.","how":{"ask":"For each added substance, decide whether the formic acid equilibrium shifts left (less ionized) or right (more ionized).","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] at equilibrium ÷ starting [HA] × 100.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product pushes the equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product pulls it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: a strong acid adds H⁺, a product of HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, so HA ionizes less.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium for the weak acid in the stem.","hl":"0.20 M solution of formic acid","calc":["HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻   (Ka ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴)","alone: x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁵) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ M","% = 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **3.0%**"]},{"text":"Nitric acid is a strong acid: it adds H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts left. With 0.050 M HNO₃ present:","calc":["x ≈ Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 ÷ 0.050","x = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","% = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **0.36%**  (down from 3.0%)"]},{"text":"Water dilutes the acid, and a diluted weak acid ionizes to a larger fraction.","strike":["Pure water"]},{"text":"Both bases remove H⁺ (OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O; HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃), so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"]},{"text":"Answer: nitric acid."}],"trap":"Choosing a base because it \"neutralizes\" the acid: removing H⁺ pulls the equilibrium to the right, so the weak acid ionizes more, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid","note":"weak acid: HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻"},{"t":"lower the percent ionization","note":"shift the equilibrium left"}]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** is the fraction of the acid molecules that have given up their H⁺.","calc":["% ionization = [A⁻] ÷ [HA]₀ × 100"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a product and the equilibrium moves back toward the reactant. H⁺ is a product, so an extra source of H⁺ makes less HA ionize.","calc":["add H⁺ → shift left → % ionization down","remove H⁺ → shift right → % ionization up"]},{"text":"**Also:** diluting a weak acid lowers [H⁺] but raises its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Also:** an electron-withdrawing substituent (such as Cl) near the acidic group makes the acid stronger, so it ionizes more."},{"text":"**Also:** a drop of indicator is too small an amount to change an acid's equilibrium or its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a strong acid (a common ion, H⁺) lowers a weak acid's percent ionization; adding a base raises it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:56#1","pe1-cp:14#1"],"q":"A heating element with a resistance of 250 mΩ is connected across a 4.5-V supply. Assuming constant resistance, how much current does it draw?","choices":["18 A","18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"],"ans":0,"exp":"Convert first: **250 mΩ = 0.250 Ω**. Then **Ohm's law, I = V/R** = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω = **18 A**. A resistance under 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number.","how":{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide the voltage by it.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (A = V ÷ Ω).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mΩ = 0.001 Ω. Capital **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milliohms to ohms.","hl":"a resistance of 250 mΩ","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.250 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"4.5-V supply","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω","I = **18 A**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 4.5-V supply versus resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"I = V ÷ R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,18],[0.5,9],[0.75,6],[1,4.5],[1.5,3],[2,2.25],[3,1.5]]}]}},{"text":"18 mA divides by 250 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 56 A divides 250 by 4.5, which is R ÷ V and unconverted. 0.056 A is R ÷ V in ohms, the ratio upside down.","strike":["18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 A."}],"trap":"Treating 250 mΩ as 250 Ω gives 18 mA, a thousand times too small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"250 mΩ","note":"resistance R (milliohms)"},{"t":"4.5-V supply","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"how much current","note":"I in amps"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before dividing.","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 ÷ 1000 Ω = 0.250 Ω"]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** current = voltage ÷ resistance.","calc":["I = V ÷ R = 4.5 ÷ 0.250 = 18 A"]},{"text":"**Also:** at constant resistance, V = IR makes voltage directly proportional to current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** I = V/R with R in ohms; convert mΩ by dividing by 1000 first, and expect more amps than volts when R is under 1 Ω."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:15#1"],"q":"In vacuum, light travels at 300,000 km/s; inside a plastic lens it travels at 200,000 km/s. What is the plastic's index of refraction?","choices":["1.5","0.67","0.50","0.33"],"ans":0,"exp":"**n = c/v** = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**. Light is never faster in a material than in vacuum, so every real index is at least 1; the three values below 1 are impossible.","how":{"ask":"Divide the speed of light in vacuum by its speed in the plastic, then check that n ≥ 1.","know":"• **Index of refraction: n = c/v** (c = speed in vacuum, v = speed in the material).\n• Since v ≤ c, **n ≥ 1 always** (n = 1 only for vacuum).","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two speeds.","hl":"300,000 km/s","calc":["c = 300,000 km/s","v = 200,000 km/s"]},{"text":"Apply n = c/v; the units cancel.","hl":"200,000 km/s","calc":["n = c ÷ v","n = 300,000 ÷ 200,000","n = **1.5**"]},{"text":"0.67 is v/c, the ratio upside down. 0.50 and 0.33 compare the speed difference with v or c. All are below 1, which would mean light moves faster than in vacuum.","strike":["0.67","0.50","0.33"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.5."}],"trap":"Writing v/c instead of c/v. Any index below 1 is a red flag that the ratio is flipped."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two speeds**.","marks":[{"t":"300,000 km/s","note":"c, in vacuum"},{"t":"200,000 km/s","note":"v, in plastic"},{"t":"index of refraction","note":"n = c/v"}]},{"text":"**The index says how many times slower light is** in the material than in vacuum.","calc":["n = c ÷ v"]},{"text":"**Fast over slow.** The bigger speed goes on top, so n comes out at least 1.","calc":["300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c/v, and n ≥ 1 for every material; a value below 1 means the ratio was flipped."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:16#1"],"q":"Two lamps emit the same 590-nm yellow light, but lamp X is three times as intense as lamp Y. What is tripled for lamp X?","choices":["Photon arrival rate","Energy of each photon","Speed of the photons","Degree of polarization"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Intensity = (photons per second × energy per photon) ÷ area.** One wavelength means one photon energy (E = hc/λ), so the only way to triple the intensity is to deliver **three times as many photons each second**. Light speed and polarization do not set intensity.","how":{"ask":"Split intensity into its two factors. Which factor can change while the wavelength stays fixed?","know":"• **Intensity** = power ÷ area = (photons per second) × (energy per photon) ÷ area.\n• **E = hf = hc/λ**: the energy of one photon depends only on its color.\n• Light speed in air is fixed at about c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s, whatever the brightness.\n• **Polarization** is the direction the electric field oscillates in; it describes orientation, not amount.","steps":[{"text":"Same wavelength for both lamps, so every photon carries the same energy.","hl":"the same 590-nm yellow light","calc":["E = hc ÷ λ","E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s)(3.00 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (590 × 10⁻⁹ m)","E ≈ **3.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J** per photon, for both lamps"],"strike":["Energy of each photon"]},{"text":"Light from both lamps travels at c, and polarization only describes the direction of the field's oscillation, not how much light arrives.","strike":["Speed of the photons","Degree of polarization"]},{"text":"Write intensity as a product and triple it: the only factor left to change is the photon rate.","hl":"three times as intense","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A","3I = (**3** × photons/s) × (same E) ÷ A"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Intensity of 590-nm light versus photon arrival rate","x":{"label":"Photons per second (relative)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Intensity (relative)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Intensity","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[4,4]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: photon arrival rate."}],"trap":"Picking 'Energy of each photon' because intensity is an energy flow. If each photon carried more energy, the wavelength, and so the color, would have changed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same and what differs**.","marks":[{"t":"the same 590-nm yellow light","note":"same color: same λ"},{"t":"three times as intense","note":"brighter by 3×"},{"t":"What is tripled","note":"find the factor that grew"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in photons.** Each photon's energy is set by its color alone.","calc":["E = hf = hc/λ","same λ → same E"]},{"text":"**Intensity counts energy arriving per second on each square metre.** It is photons per second times energy per photon.","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A"]},{"text":"**See it:** at one color, intensity rises in a straight line with the photon rate.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fixed color: intensity is proportional to photons per second","x":{"label":"Photons per second (relative)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Intensity (relative)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Intensity","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,1],[2,2],[3,3],[4,4]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for light of one color, intensity is proportional to the number of photons per second; speed, wavelength and polarization do not set it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-17a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:17#1"],"q":"A green LED emits light at a frequency of 580 THz. Using Planck's constant in eV·s, how much energy does each emitted photon carry?","choices":["2.4 eV","0.0024 eV","0.24 eV","2400 eV"],"ans":0,"exp":"**E = hf** with **tera = 10¹²**: f = 5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz, and E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(5.8 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) = 24 × 10⁻¹ eV = **2.4 eV**, a typical visible-light photon energy.","how":{"ask":"Convert THz to Hz, multiply by Planck's constant in eV·s, and combine the powers of ten.","know":"• **E = hf**: E = photon energy, h = Planck's constant, f = frequency (Hz = s⁻¹).\n• **h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s** (1 eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J).\n• Prefixes: giga 10⁹, **tera 10¹²**, peta 10¹⁵.\n• Visible photons carry roughly 1.7–3.1 eV.","steps":[{"text":"The frequency is given, so use E = hf.","hl":"a frequency of 580 THz","calc":["E = h × f"]},{"text":"Convert the prefix: tera = 10¹².","calc":["580 THz = 580 × 10¹² Hz","= **5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Multiply the numbers and add the exponents separately.","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(5.8 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹)","4.14 × 5.8 = 24.0","10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E = 24.0 × 10⁻¹ eV = **2.4 eV**"]},{"text":"Check in joules: (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.8 × 10¹⁴) = 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, and 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 2.4 eV.","calc":["3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ **2.4 eV**"]},{"text":"0.0024 eV treats tera as giga (10⁹). 2400 eV treats it as peta (10¹⁵). 0.24 eV writes 580 THz as 5.8 × 10¹³ Hz, one power of ten short.","strike":["0.0024 eV","2400 eV","0.24 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 eV."}],"trap":"Slipping one prefix step (tera as giga) shifts the answer by a factor of 1000. A visible photon always carries a few eV, so 0.0024 eV or 2400 eV is a unit slip."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the number and its unit**.","marks":[{"t":"580 THz","note":"frequency f"},{"t":"eV·s","note":"use h in eV·s"},{"t":"each emitted photon","note":"energy of a single photon"}]},{"text":"**One photon's energy is Planck's constant times frequency.**","calc":["E = hf","h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s"]},{"text":"**Tera means 10¹².** Write the frequency in scientific notation before multiplying.","calc":["580 THz = 5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Numbers and powers of ten separately.** Multiply 4.14 × 5.8, then add −15 and +14.","calc":["4.14 × 5.8 = 24.0","10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E = 2.4 eV"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf with f in hertz (THz × 10¹²); multiply the leading numbers and add the exponents, then check that a visible photon comes out near 2–3 eV."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-18a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:57#1"],"q":"A fluid's gauge pressure at depth h is P = ρgh, and its density is ρ = m/V. Which expression gives the depth h?","choices":["PV/(mg)","mg/(PV)","Pm/(Vg)","P/(mVg)"],"ans":0,"exp":"Substitute the density definition: P = (m/V)gh. Multiply both sides by V to clear the fraction: PV = mgh. Divide by mg: **h = PV/(mg)**.","how":{"ask":"Put the density definition into the pressure equation, clear the fraction, then isolate h.","know":"• **Substitution**: replace ρ with m/V.\n• **Clear a fraction** by multiplying both sides by its denominator (here V).\n• Then divide both sides by everything still multiplying h.","steps":[{"text":"Substitute ρ = m/V into P = ρgh.","hl":"ρ = m/V","calc":["P = (m/V) g h"]},{"text":"Multiply through by the denominator V.","hl":"P = ρgh","calc":["P × V = m g h","PV = mgh"]},{"text":"Divide both sides by mg.","calc":["h = **PV/(mg)**"]},{"text":"mg/(PV) is the result flipped. Pm/(Vg) comes from substituting ρ = V/m, the density definition upside down. P/(mVg) treats density as m × V instead of m ÷ V.","strike":["mg/(PV)","Pm/(Vg)","P/(mVg)"]},{"text":"Answer: PV/(mg)."}],"trap":"Putting V in the denominator: since ρ = m/V, the V sits under m, and moving it across the equals sign puts it on top with P."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two definitions** and the unknown.","marks":[{"t":"P = ρgh","note":"pressure equation"},{"t":"ρ = m/V","note":"density definition"},{"t":"depth h","note":"isolate this"}]},{"text":"**Substitute.** Wherever ρ appears, write m/V.","calc":["P = (m/V)gh"]},{"text":"**Clear the fraction.** Multiply every term by V.","calc":["PV = mgh"]},{"text":"**Isolate h.** Divide by what multiplies it, then check units: (Pa·m³)/(kg·m/s²) = m ✓.","calc":["h = PV/(mg)"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a fluid column, h = P/(ρg): atmospheric pressure can push water up an evacuated tube about 10 m.","calc":["h = P/(ρg)","1 atm ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; water: 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) ≈ 10 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a question gives a formula, name each variable and plug the values in. In a blood pressure written systolic/diastolic, the diastolic is the lower number.","calc":["given pulse pressure = P_s − P_d","120/76 mmHg: P_s = 120, P_d = 76","pulse pressure = 120 − 76 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** substitute one definition into the other, multiply through by the fraction's denominator, then divide to isolate the variable."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-19d","s":"Fluids","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:19#4","pe1-cp:19#1","pe1-cp:19#2","pe1-cp:19#3"],"q":"A patient's systolic pressure is 95 mmHg. Expressed as a percentage of atmospheric pressure, about how large is this value?","choices":["12.5%","8.0%","94%","0.13%"],"ans":0,"exp":"Write both pressures in **the same unit**, mmHg: 95 ÷ 760 = 0.125, and 0.125 × 100 = **12.5%**. 8.0 inverts the ratio, 94% divides mmHg by kPa, and 0.13% forgets to multiply by 100.","how":{"ask":"Divide the patient's pressure by atmospheric pressure in matching units, then convert to a percentage.","know":"• **1 atm = 760 mmHg**.\n• **% = value ÷ 760 mmHg × 100** when the value is in mmHg.\n• Never divide mmHg by kPa.","steps":[{"text":"Note the value and its unit.","hl":"95 mmHg","calc":["value = 95 mmHg","1 atm = 760 mmHg"]},{"text":"Divide, then multiply by 100.","calc":["95 ÷ 760 = 0.125","0.125 × 100 = **12.5%**"]},{"text":"Eliminate the slips: 8.0 = 760 ÷ 95 (inverted); 94% ≈ 95 ÷ 101 (mmHg over kPa); 0.13% ≈ 0.125 written as a percent without the × 100.","strike":["8.0%","94%","0.13%"]},{"text":"Answer: 12.5%."}],"trap":"Dividing by 101: that is atmospheric pressure in kPa, and dividing mmHg by kPa gives a meaningless number near 94%."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the number and what to compare it with**.","marks":[{"t":"95 mmHg","note":"the value"},{"t":"atmospheric pressure","note":"760 mmHg"}]},{"text":"**Same units first.** The value is in mmHg, so use 1 atm = 760 mmHg."},{"text":"**Percentage = fraction × 100.**","calc":["% = value ÷ 760 × 100"]},{"text":"**Also:** a typical healthy adult's blood pressure is about 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."},{"text":"**Also:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic."},{"text":"**Also:** 1 atm = 760 mmHg; multiply atm by 760 to get mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** percent of 1 atm = (pressure in mmHg ÷ 760) × 100."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-20b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:20#2","pe1-cp:20#1","pe1-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2"],"q":"Strontium-90 has a half-life of 29 years. How much of a 40 mg sample of it remains after 87 years?","choices":["5 mg","10 mg","20 mg","35 mg"],"ans":0,"exp":"n = 87 ÷ 29 = **3** half-lives. **Remaining = starting amount × (½)ⁿ** = 40 mg × ⅛ = **5 mg**. 35 mg is the amount that decayed, not the amount left.","how":{"ask":"Count the half-lives, then halve the starting amount that many times.","know":"• n = t ÷ t½.\n• **Remaining = N₀ × (½)ⁿ**.\n• Decayed = N₀ − remaining.","steps":[{"text":"Note the starting mass, the half-life and the time.","hl":"40 mg","calc":["N₀ = 40 mg","t½ = 29 y, t = 87 y"]},{"text":"Count half-lives.","hl":"87 years","calc":["n = 87 ÷ 29 = **3**"]},{"text":"Halve three times.","calc":["(½)³ = ⅛","40 mg × ⅛ = **5 mg**","40 → 20 → 10 → 5 mg"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Strontium-90 remaining in a 40 mg sample","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":87,"step":29},"y":{"label":"Mass remaining (mg)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Sr-90 left","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[29,20],[58,10],[87,5]]}]}},{"text":"20 mg is after one half-life and 10 mg after two; 35 mg (40 − 5) is what decayed.","strike":["20 mg","10 mg","35 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 5 mg."}],"trap":"Answering 35 mg: that is the decayed mass. The question asks what remains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 29 years","note":"t½"},{"t":"40 mg","note":"starting amount"},{"t":"87 years","note":"elapsed time"}]},{"text":"**Count half-lives:** n = t ÷ t½.","calc":["n = 87 ÷ 29 = 3"]},{"text":"**Halve n times**, which is the same as multiplying by (½)ⁿ.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Each half-life halves the mass","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":87,"step":29},"y":{"label":"Mass remaining (mg)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Sr-90 left","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,40],[29,20],[58,10],[87,5]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** fraction decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ, where n = time ÷ half-life."},{"text":"**Also:** to find the starting amount, run it backward: double once per half-life, so original = remaining × 2ⁿ.","calc":["n = 20 days ÷ 5 days = 4","original = 7 mg × 2⁴ = 7 × 16 = 112 mg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** amount remaining = starting amount × (½)ⁿ, with n = time ÷ half-life."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-21b","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:21#2","pe1-cp:21#1","pe1-cp:21#3"],"q":"A pump moves 24 L in 4.0 min. Its power curve passes through 4 W at 0 L/min, 10 W at 2 L/min, 16 W at 4 L/min and 22 W at 6 L/min. What power does the pump draw?","choices":["22 W","16 W","4 W","88 W"],"ans":0,"exp":"First find the operating flow: 24 L ÷ 4.0 min = **6 L/min**. Then read the curve at that flow: **22 W**. Reading at 4 confuses the time with the flow.","how":{"ask":"Work out the flow rate in L/min, then read the power at that flow on the curve.","know":"• **Flow rate = volume ÷ time**, in the axis units (L/min).\n• To read a graph: find the x value, go up to the curve, read across to y.","steps":[{"text":"Compute the flow rate in the graph's units.","hl":"24 L in 4.0 min","calc":["Q = 24 L ÷ 4.0 min = **6 L/min**"]},{"text":"Read the curve at 6 L/min.","hl":"22 W at 6 L/min","calc":["P(6 L/min) = **22 W**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pump power versus flow rate","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,4],[2,10],[4,16],[6,22],[8,28]]}]}},{"text":"16 W is read at x = 4, the number of minutes. 4 W is the curve at almost zero flow, what you get by using the per-second rate (0.1 L/s) on a per-minute axis. 88 W multiplies the power by the time, which gives work, not power.","strike":["16 W","4 W","88 W"]},{"text":"Answer: 22 W."}],"trap":"Reading the graph at a number from the stem (4.0 min) instead of at the computed flow rate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"24 L","note":"volume"},{"t":"4.0 min","note":"time"},{"t":"power curve","note":"P read from flow"}]},{"text":"**Get the x value.** The curve's x-axis is flow in L/min.","calc":["24 L ÷ 4.0 min = 6 L/min"]},{"text":"**Go up to the curve, then across.** At 6 L/min the line sits at 22 W.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Read power at the operating flow","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,4],[2,10],[4,16],[6,22],[8,28]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** compute a rate in exactly the units of the graph's axis before reading the graph."},{"text":"**Also:** work = power × time, with power in watts and time in seconds, giving joules."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute the x value in the axis's units, then read the curve at that x; never read at a raw number from the question."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-22a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:22#1","pe1-cp:22#2","pe1-cp:22#3"],"q":"A purified enzyme left in air slowly forms a link between two of its cysteine side chains. What kind of interaction is this link?","choices":["Covalent bond","Hydrogen bond","Salt bridge","London dispersion"],"ans":0,"exp":"O₂ from the air **oxidizes** the side chains: each cysteine **thiol (–SH)** loses its H and the two sulfurs bond, giving a **disulfide (–S–S–)**. Electrons are shared between the sulfurs, so it is a **covalent bond**, far stronger than hydrogen bonds, salt bridges or dispersion forces.","how":{"ask":"Name the link that forms when cysteine side chains are oxidized, then classify it.","know":"• Cysteine side chain: –CH₂–**SH** (a thiol).\n• **Oxidation**: 2 R–SH → R–S–S–R + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ (a **disulfide**).\n• The S–S bond is a shared electron pair: **covalent**.\n• Hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and dispersion forces are noncovalent and do not need oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"Air is the oxidant and cysteine is the residue.","hl":"left in air slowly forms a link between two of its cysteine side chains"},{"text":"Write the reaction.","calc":["2 Cys–SH + ½ O₂ → Cys–S–S–Cys + H₂O","S–S = shared electron pair → **covalent**"]},{"text":"These are noncovalent attractions, and none needs oxidation to form.","strike":["Hydrogen bond","Salt bridge","London dispersion"]},{"text":"Answer: a covalent bond (a disulfide)."}],"trap":"Calling the disulfide a weak side-chain interaction like the others in tertiary structure: it is the one covalent cross-link among them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"left in air","note":"O₂ oxidizes"},{"t":"cysteine side chains","note":"thiols, –SH"}]},{"text":"**Cysteine carries a thiol.** Its side chain ends in S–H.","fig":"cysteine"},{"text":"**Oxidation joins two thiols.** Both H's leave and the sulfurs bond to each other.","calc":["R–SH + HS–R → R–S–S–R"]},{"text":"**Classify the bond.** The sulfurs share electrons: a covalent bond, unlike the noncovalent forces that also shape proteins."},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide is always covalent; it is intermolecular when its two cysteines belong to different molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** reducing agents such as β-mercaptoethanol or DTT break disulfides back into two thiols."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** oxidizing two cysteine thiols makes a disulfide, a covalent S–S bond."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-23c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:23#3","pe1-cp:23#1","pe1-cp:23#2"],"q":"A designed peptide has a cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) and a cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp). Which residue gives cells a charged group to bind?","choices":["Arg in region B","Cys in region A","Gly in region A","Cys in region B"],"ans":0,"exp":"Match each residue to its region first. Region A holds **Gly** (no side chain) and **Cys** (a thiol for disulfide cross-links), so it offers no charge. Region B holds Lys, Glu, Arg and Asp, all **charged** at body pH; **Arg** is one of them. There is no Cys in region B.","how":{"ask":"List the residues in each region, then find a charged one in the region where it actually sits.","know":"• Charged at body pH: Asp −, Glu −, Lys +, Arg +.\n• **Cys** = thiol for **disulfide cross-links**, not a charge.\n• **Gly** = no side chain.\n• A choice must name a residue that is really in the region it names.","steps":[{"text":"Write out the two regions.","hl":"Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys","calc":["region A: Gly, Cys → cross-linking","region B: Lys, Glu, Arg, Asp → all charged"]},{"text":"Region A has no charged side chain: Gly has none, and Cys forms disulfides.","strike":["Cys in region A","Gly in region A"]},{"text":"Cys does not appear in region B, so that pairing is wrong.","hl":"Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp","strike":["Cys in region B"]},{"text":"Answer: Arg in region B."}],"trap":"Choosing cysteine because its sulfur looks reactive: it belongs to the cross-linking region and gives a covalent link between strands, not a charge for cells."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cross-linking region A","note":"Cys for disulfides"},{"t":"cell-binding region B","note":"charged residues"},{"t":"charged group","note":"Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg"}]},{"text":"**Sort by job.** Cys makes S–S cross-links; Gly is a spacer; charged residues bind cells.","fig":["cysteine","glycine"]},{"text":"**Check the charged ones.**","calc":["Lys +, Arg +","Glu −, Asp −"],"fig":["arginine","glutamate"]},{"text":"**Check the location too.** A right residue in the wrong region is a wrong answer."},{"text":"**Also:** for an electrostatic contact, choose a side chain charged opposite to the partner: Lys or Arg for a − site, Asp or Glu for a + site."},{"text":"**Also:** glycine has no side chain (R = H), so it adds no charge or functional group to a binding site."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each residue to its labeled region and its job: Cys cross-links, Gly adds nothing, and Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg give charges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-24c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:24#3","pe1-cp:24#1","pe1-cp:24#2"],"q":"A report states that a regulatory phosphate is attached to a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol. Which residue carries the phosphate?","choices":["Serine","Threonine","Tyrosine","Cysteine"],"ans":0,"exp":"A **primary alcohol** has its –OH on a carbon bonded to only one other carbon: **–CH₂–OH**. That is serine's side chain. Threonine's –OH is on a carbon with two carbon neighbors (secondary), tyrosine's is on a ring (a phenol), and cysteine has –SH, not –OH.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain matches the description the report gives: an –OH on a carbon with one carbon neighbor?","know":"• **Primary alcohol**: –CH₂–OH (1 C on the carbinol carbon).\n• Ser –CH₂OH (1°); Thr –CH(OH)CH₃ (2°); Tyr ring–OH (phenol); Cys –CH₂SH (thiol).\n• Use the description given, not the residue you expect to see phosphorylated.","steps":[{"text":"The report's clue is the class of alcohol.","hl":"side chain is a primary alcohol"},{"text":"Find the side chain that ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":["serine-sc","t1-phosphoserine"],"calc":["Ser: –CH₂–OH → C bearing OH has 1 C neighbor → **1°** ✓"]},{"text":"Threonine's OH carbon has two carbon neighbors (secondary), and tyrosine's OH sits on a ring (phenol).","strike":["Threonine","Tyrosine"]},{"text":"Cysteine ends in –CH₂–SH: a thiol, not an alcohol.","strike":["Cysteine"]},{"text":"Answer: serine."}],"trap":"Picking tyrosine or threonine because they are also phosphorylated: the report names a primary alcohol, and only serine fits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"primary alcohol","note":"–CH₂–OH"},{"t":"regulatory phosphate","note":"on an –OH"}]},{"text":"**Translate the description into a structure.** Primary means the –OH carbon has one carbon neighbor.","calc":["primary: R–CH₂–OH","secondary: R–CH(OH)–R′","phenol: ring–OH"]},{"text":"**Match it to a side chain.** Only serine ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** serine is a primary alcohol, threonine a secondary alcohol, tyrosine a phenol, and alanine only a methyl group."},{"text":"**Also:** phosphorylation goes on a side-chain –OH, so look for Ser, Thr or Tyr and reject side chains with no hydroxyl."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** turn the description into a structure first (primary alcohol → –CH₂OH → serine), then pick the residue that matches it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-25c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:25#3","pe1-cp:25#1","pe1-cp:25#2","pe3-cp:43#1"],"q":"A peptide's only ionizable side chains are histidines, with pKa 6.0. At pH 8.0, what is the main form of these side chains?","choices":["Mostly neutral","Mostly positive","Half positive","Mostly negative"],"ans":0,"exp":"pH 8.0 is **two units above** the pKa, so by Henderson–Hasselbalch the neutral (base) form outnumbers the protonated (+1) form **100 : 1**. About 99% of the histidines are neutral. They would be half positive only at pH 6.0 and mostly positive below it; a histidine side chain never turns negative.","how":{"ask":"How far is the pH from the pKa, and which side of it is the pH on?","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**.\n• Histidine: acid form = imidazolium (+1); base form = neutral imidazole (0).\n• Each pH unit away from the pKa is a 10-fold change in the ratio.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the pKa and the pH.","hl":"pKa 6.0"},{"text":"Apply Henderson–Hasselbalch.","calc":["log([base]/[acid]) = pH − pKa","= 8.0 − 6.0 = 2.0","[base]/[acid] = 10² = **100 : 1**","fraction neutral = 100/101 ≈ **99%**"]},{"text":"See it on the titration curve: at pH 8 almost none is protonated.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Histidine side chain protonated (pKa 6.0)","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Protonated, +1 (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Protonated","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,99.9],[4,99],[5,91],[5.5,76],[6,50],[6.5,24],[7,9],[8,1],[9,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Half positive happens only at pH = pKa; mostly positive needs pH below 6.0.","strike":["Half positive","Mostly positive"]},{"text":"Losing its proton leaves histidine neutral, not negative.","strike":["Mostly negative"]},{"text":"Answer: mostly neutral."}],"trap":"Thinking of histidine as a 'basic, positive' residue regardless of pH: above its pKa of 6.0 it is mostly uncharged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 6.0","note":"histidine side chain"},{"t":"pH 8.0","note":"two units above"}]},{"text":"**Compare pH with pKa.** pH above pKa → mostly deprotonated; below → mostly protonated.","calc":["8.0 − 6.0 = +2"]},{"text":"**Each unit is a factor of 10.** Two units above the pKa → 100 base : 1 acid.","fig":"histidine-sc","calc":["10² = 100 → ≈ 99% neutral"]},{"text":"**Also:** a side chain's charge changes only when the pH moves past its pKa; acids go 0 → −1, bases go +1 → 0 as pH rises."},{"text":"**Also:** charged side chains repel and keep peptides apart; when the pH neutralizes them, the strands can pack together."},{"text":"**Also:** at a fixed pH, lowering a group's pKa means less of it is protonated, so a structure held together by the protonated form (such as C·C⁺ pairs in an i-motif) is less stable; compare pK values to see which change lowered it.","calc":["pKa 6.5 → 6.0 at pH 6.5: 50% → 24% protonated → less stable"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare pH with each side chain's pKa; one unit past it is about 90% converted, two units about 99%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-26a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2"],"q":"In a muscle bed, blood moves at 2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm². The capillaries they feed total 800 cm². What is the capillary blood speed?","choices":["0.10 cm/s","2.0 cm/s","40 cm/s","0.0025 cm/s"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Continuity: Q = A·v is the same at every level.** Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = 80 cm³/s, so v = 80 ÷ 800 = **0.10 cm/s**. Blood crawls through capillaries because their **total** area is twenty times larger, not because each one is thin.","how":{"ask":"The same volume per second passes the arterioles and the capillaries. How fast must it move through twenty times the total area?","know":"• **Continuity: Q = A·v** (Q = flow rate in cm³/s, A = cross-sectional area, v = mean speed). For blood, Q is the same at every level of the circulation.\n• Vessels in parallel: use their **combined** area.\n• Larger total A → smaller v. Capillaries have the largest total area, so blood is slowest there.","steps":[{"text":"Find the flow rate in the arterioles.","hl":"2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm²","calc":["Q = A × v","Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = **80 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"The same Q passes the capillaries; divide by their total area.","hl":"total 800 cm²","calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 80 cm³/s ÷ 800 cm²","v = **0.10 cm/s**","area × 20 → speed ÷ 20"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same flow (80 cm³/s): speed falls as total area grows","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"2.0 cm/s assumes equal flow means equal speed. 40 cm/s treats the thin capillaries like a nozzle and multiplies by 20. 0.0025 cm/s divides the arteriole speed by the capillary area and skips Q.","strike":["2.0 cm/s","40 cm/s","0.0025 cm/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.10 cm/s."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow vessels speed blood up. One narrow tube would, but the blood is split among countless capillaries whose combined area is far larger than the arterioles'."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm/s","note":"arteriole speed"},{"t":"40 cm²","note":"arteriole total area"},{"t":"800 cm²","note":"capillary total area"}]},{"text":"**Continuity:** blood is not lost between levels, so the volume per second is the same everywhere.","calc":["Q = A·v = constant"]},{"text":"**Combined area decides the speed.** More total area for the same Q means slower blood.","calc":["v = Q ÷ A"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed versus total area at a fixed flow of 80 cm³/s","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** given n vessels each of area a, total area = n·a, so u = Av/(na).","calc":["n = 1.6 × 10⁹ capillaries, a = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ cm² each","n·a = 1.6 × 10⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 800 cm²","u = Av/(na) = (40 × 2.0) ÷ 800 = 0.10 cm/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = A·v is the same at every level, so speed is smallest where the total cross-sectional area is largest: the capillaries."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-27a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:27#1","pe1-cp:27#2","pe1-cp:27#3"],"q":"On a potential–pH diagram, the boundary between Cu²⁺ and copper metal stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5. Which species is absent from its half-reaction?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"],"ans":0,"exp":"A **horizontal** boundary keeps the same potential whatever the pH, so the equilibrium **does not depend on pH**: its half-reaction, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu, contains **no H⁺ or OH⁻**. It still transfers electrons, and both copper forms appear in it.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a boundary at one fixed potential across a range of pH says about the half-reaction.","know":"• Pourbaix diagram: potential E (y) versus pH (x); each line is an equilibrium between two forms.\n• **Horizontal line**: E does not change with pH → **pH-independent** → no H⁺ or OH⁻ in the half-reaction, but electrons are transferred.","steps":[{"text":"The boundary is flat: one potential across several pH values.","hl":"stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5","calc":["E constant while pH changes → pH-independent"]},{"text":"Write the half-reaction it represents.","calc":["Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu   (E° = +0.34 V)","no H⁺, no OH⁻"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"A line set at a potential is a redox couple, so electrons must be in it; Cu²⁺ and Cu are the two forms the line separates.","strike":["The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ and OH⁻."}],"trap":"Thinking a flat line means \"no electrons\": that describes a vertical line. A flat line is a redox couple with no H⁺ or OH⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"potential–pH diagram","note":"E on y, pH on x"},{"t":"stays at +0.34 V","note":"horizontal line"},{"t":"from pH 0 to 5","note":"pH changes, E does not"}]},{"text":"**Read the slope of each boundary.** A flat line sits at one potential, whatever the pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"**Flat = pH has no say.** If changing pH does not move the line, H⁺ and OH⁻ are not in the equilibrium.","calc":["horizontal → electrons yes, H⁺/OH⁻ no"]},{"text":"**Also:** a vertical Pourbaix boundary means the equilibrium depends on pH but transfers no electrons."},{"text":"**Also:** the higher a region lies on a potential–pH diagram, the more oxidized the species stable there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a horizontal Pourbaix boundary is a redox equilibrium that does not depend on pH (no H⁺ or OH⁻ involved)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:28#1"],"q":"Sulfur hexafluoride, SF₆, has six fluorine atoms bonded to a central sulfur atom that carries no lone pairs. Which hybridization describes the sulfur?","choices":["sp³d²","sp³d³","dsp³","sp²"],"ans":0,"exp":"Six bonded atoms and no lone pairs = **6 electron domains**, so sulfur needs **6 hybrid orbitals**: sp³d² (1 s + 3 p + 2 d = 6), which point to the corners of an octahedron. d²sp³ is the same count, written when inner d orbitals mix in a metal complex. sp³d³ makes 7, dsp³ (another name for sp³d) makes 5, and sp² makes 3.","how":{"ask":"Count sulfur's electron domains, then pick the hybridization label that makes exactly that many orbitals.","know":"• Hybrid orbitals needed = electron domains (bonded atoms + lone pairs).\n• **2 → sp; 3 → sp²; 4 → sp³ (tetrahedral); 5 → sp³d (trigonal bipyramidal); 6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (octahedral)**.\n• Count the orbitals in a label: s = 1, then read each superscript for p and d (no superscript = 1).","steps":[{"text":"Count sulfur's domains: six bonds and no lone pairs.","hl":"six fluorine atoms bonded to a central sulfur atom that carries no lone pairs","calc":["domains = 6 bonds + 0 lone pairs = **6**","6 domains → **6** hybrid orbitals → octahedral"]},{"text":"Count the orbitals each label makes.","calc":["sp³d²: 1 + 3 + 2 = **6** → octahedral","sp³d³: 1 + 3 + 3 = 7 → pentagonal bipyramidal","dsp³: 1 + 1 + 3 = 5 → trigonal bipyramidal","sp²: 1 + 2 = 3 → trigonal planar"]},{"text":"Seven orbitals is one too many, and five or three cannot hold six bonds.","strike":["sp³d³","dsp³","sp²"]},{"text":"Answer: sp³d²."}],"trap":"dsp³ also uses a d orbital, so it looks right for an expanded octet, but it makes only 5 hybrid orbitals: the trigonal bipyramidal set of PCl₅, not the octahedral set of SF₆."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"six fluorine atoms","note":"6 bonded atoms"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"0 extra domains"},{"t":"hybridization","note":"how many orbitals mix"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** = atoms bonded + lone pairs. Each domain needs one hybrid orbital.","calc":["SF₆: 6 + 0 = 6 domains → 6 orbitals"]},{"text":"**Match the count to a label.**","calc":["4 → sp³ (CH₄, tetrahedral)","5 → sp³d (PCl₅, trigonal bipyramidal)","6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (SF₆, octahedral)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 6 domains → octahedral → sp³d² (d²sp³ in metal complexes); 5 → sp³d; 4 → sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-29b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:29#2","pe1-cp:29#1"],"q":"A sealed flask holds 2.00 mol of nitrogen gas at STP. Assuming ideal behavior, what volume does the gas occupy?","choices":["44.8 L","48.9 L","11.2 L","89.6 L"],"ans":0,"exp":"At **STP (0 °C = 273 K, 1 atm)** one mole of ideal gas fills **22.4 L**, so V = 2.00 mol × 22.4 L/mol = **44.8 L**. 48.9 L is what the sample would fill at 298 K (room temperature), not at STP; 11.2 L divides by 2 instead of multiplying; 89.6 L uses 4 mol, counting the two N atoms in each N₂ as separate moles.","how":{"ask":"What is the molar volume at STP, and how many moles are in the flask?","know":"• **STP = 0 °C (273 K) and 1 atm**. At STP, 1 mol of ideal gas occupies **22.4 L**.\n• 22.4 L/mol holds only at 0 °C; at 25 °C (298 K) a mole fills about 24.5 L.\n• Check with PV = nRT: V = nRT/P.","steps":[{"text":"Note the amount and the conditions.","hl":"2.00 mol of nitrogen gas at STP","calc":["n = 2.00 mol","STP: T = 273 K, P = 1 atm"]},{"text":"Use the molar volume at STP.","calc":["V = n × 22.4 L/mol","V = 2.00 mol × 22.4 L/mol","V = **44.8 L**"]},{"text":"Check with PV = nRT.","calc":["V = nRT ÷ P","V = (2.00 mol)(0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹)(273 K) ÷ 1 atm","V = **44.8 L** ✓"]},{"text":"48.9 L uses 298 K (25 °C), room temperature, which is not STP.","calc":["(2.00)(0.0821)(298) ÷ 1 = 48.9 L"],"strike":["48.9 L"]},{"text":"11.2 L divides 22.4 by 2 instead of multiplying.","calc":["22.4 ÷ 2 = 11.2 L"],"strike":["11.2 L"]},{"text":"89.6 L uses 4 mol, counting the two N atoms in each N₂ as separate moles.","calc":["(2.00 × 2) mol × 22.4 L/mol = 89.6 L"],"strike":["89.6 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 44.8 L."}],"trap":"Using 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C, gives 48.9 L. The 22.4 L/mol shortcut belongs to 0 °C only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it stands for.","marks":[{"t":"2.00 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"STP","note":"0 °C and 1 atm"},{"t":"ideal behavior","note":"use the molar volume"}]},{"text":"**STP** means standard temperature and pressure: **0 °C (273 K) and 1 atm**. There, one mole of any ideal gas fills **22.4 L**.","calc":["at STP: 1 mol ↔ 22.4 L"]},{"text":"**Temperature matters.** Volume grows with T, so at 25 °C a mole fills more.","calc":["0 °C: 22.4 L/mol","25 °C: 22.4 × 298/273 ≈ 24.5 L/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** a gas is most ideal at high T and low P, where particle volume and intermolecular forces are both negligible."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n × 22.4 L/mol only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm); at any other temperature use PV = nRT."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-30a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:30#1"],"q":"A deep-sea fish sees with a single visual pigment that absorbs most strongly at 490 nm. Which change would most directly give it color vision?","choices":["A pigment with another peak","Twice as much of its pigment","A peak shifted to 550 nm","A narrower absorption band"],"ans":0,"exp":"One pigment only counts absorbed photons, so a dim light at 490 nm and a brighter light elsewhere can give the **same signal**. A **second pigment with a different absorption peak** responds differently to each wavelength, and the brain **compares the two responses** to tell colors apart.","how":{"ask":"With one pigment, can the brain tell a change in color from a change in brightness? What would let it compare?","know":"• A photoreceptor reports only how many photons it absorbs, not their wavelength (**univariance**).\n• **Color vision** needs at least two pigments with **different absorption peaks**; the brain compares their responses (their ratio).\n• More pigment, a shifted peak or a narrower band still leaves one pigment, so still no comparison.","steps":[{"text":"With one pigment, 450-nm and 530-nm light are absorbed equally well, so after matching brightness they give identical signals.","hl":"a single visual pigment","calc":["one pigment → one number (photons absorbed)","color and brightness mixed together"]},{"text":"More pigment raises sensitivity; a shifted peak or a narrower band changes which light is absorbed best. Each still leaves one pigment and one signal.","strike":["Twice as much of its pigment","A peak shifted to 550 nm","A narrower absorption band"]},{"text":"Add a second pigment peaking elsewhere: the two pigments now absorb in a different ratio at each wavelength.","calc":["450 nm: first 0.7, second ≈ 0.0","530 nm: first 0.7, second 0.7","different ratios → different colors"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The fish pigment and a second pigment with another peak","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: a pigment with another peak."}],"trap":"Choosing more pigment: it makes the fish more sensitive in dim light, but one pigment, however much of it, cannot separate color from brightness."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"a single visual pigment","note":"only one kind"},{"t":"490 nm","note":"its absorption peak"},{"t":"color vision","note":"telling wavelengths apart"}]},{"text":"**One pigment sends one number:** photons absorbed. A dim light near the peak and a bright light off the peak can give the same number.","calc":["signal = brightness × absorbance"]},{"text":"**Two pigments with different peaks** absorb each wavelength in a different ratio; the brain compares them.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two pigments, two peaks","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** color vision comes from comparing the responses of pigments with different absorption peaks; one pigment alone cannot tell wavelength from brightness."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:31#1","pe1-cp:31#2","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"q":"An insect odorant-binding protein holds a small hydrocarbon odorant inside a buried cavity. Which residue would most likely line that cavity?","choices":["Valine","Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"],"ans":0,"exp":"A hydrocarbon odorant is **nonpolar**, so it binds best in a **hydrophobic pocket** whose side chains are also hydrocarbon. Valine's isopropyl group contains only C and H and packs against the odorant (like dissolves like). Glutamate (–COO⁻) and arginine (guanidinium, +) are charged, and threonine's –OH is polar; all three would rather face water.","how":{"ask":"Is the odorant polar or nonpolar, and which side chain is itself pure hydrocarbon?","know":"• **Nonpolar side chains** (hydrophobic): Ala, **Val, Leu, Ile**, Met, **Phe**, Trp, Pro.\n• **Polar uncharged**: Ser, **Thr**, Asn, Gln, Tyr, Cys. **Charged**: Asp, **Glu** (−); Lys, **Arg**, His (+).\n• Like dissolves like: a hydrocarbon ligand is buried among hydrocarbon side chains.","steps":[{"text":"The ligand is a hydrocarbon: only C and H, so it is nonpolar.","hl":"a small hydrocarbon odorant","calc":["C–H bonds share electrons almost evenly → **nonpolar**"]},{"text":"It sits in a buried cavity, away from water, so the lining must be hydrophobic too.","hl":"inside a buried cavity"},{"text":"Valine's side chain is all C and H: it fits.","calc":["Val: –CH(CH₃)₂ → nonpolar ✓"]},{"text":"Glutamate carries –COO⁻ and arginine a positive guanidinium at pH 7: charges want water. Threonine's –OH is polar.","strike":["Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: valine."}],"trap":"Threonine looks harmless because it is small and uncharged, but its –OH makes it polar; a pocket for a pure hydrocarbon is lined with Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrocarbon odorant","note":"only C and H → nonpolar"},{"t":"buried cavity","note":"away from water"},{"t":"line that cavity","note":"side chains facing the ligand"}]},{"text":"**Polar vs nonpolar:** O and N pull electrons and make polar or charged groups; C–H bonds are nonpolar. Hydrocarbons mix with hydrocarbons, not with water."},{"text":"**The hydrophobic four** to remember for pockets: side chains made only of carbon and hydrogen.","calc":["Leu: –CH₂CH(CH₃)₂","Ile: –CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃","Val: –CH(CH₃)₂","Phe: –CH₂C₆H₅"]},{"text":"**Also:** charged or polar side chains don't fit a pocket for a nonpolar ligand; swapping in Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, Ser or Thr weakens binding."},{"text":"**Also:** when a nonpolar part of a drug sits in a water-free pocket, its main interaction is hydrophobic contact (London dispersion) with nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe, not hydrogen bonds or salt bridges."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar ligand binds in a hydrophobic pocket lined with Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-32a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:32#1","pe1-cp:32#2"],"q":"ATP is synthesized with ³³P in its β-phosphate only. After hexokinase converts glucose to glucose 6-phosphate with this ATP, where is the label found?","choices":["In ADP","In glucose 6-phosphate","In free phosphate","In AMP"],"ans":0,"exp":"Hexokinase, like every kinase, transfers ATP's **terminal (γ) phosphate** to its substrate. The **α and β phosphates stay** behind as ADP, so a label on β ends up in **ADP**, not in glucose 6-phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Find which of ATP's three phosphates the kinase moves, then see where the β phosphate ends up.","know":"• ATP phosphates, from the ribose outward: **α, β, γ**.\n• Kinase: ATP + R–OH → **ADP + R–O–PO₃²⁻**; only **γ** moves.\n• ADP keeps α and β; AMP would keep only α.","steps":[{"text":"The label is on β, the middle phosphate.","hl":"³³P in its β-phosphate only"},{"text":"Write the kinase reaction.","calc":["glucose + ATP(α–β*–γ) → glucose–O–P(γ) + ADP(α–β*)","label (*) stays in **ADP**"]},{"text":"Only γ reaches the glucose, and no phosphate is released free.","strike":["In glucose 6-phosphate","In free phosphate"]},{"text":"AMP would form only if β and γ left together, which a kinase does not do.","strike":["In AMP"]},{"text":"Answer: in ADP."}],"trap":"Assuming any phosphorus label in ATP is passed on: a kinase moves only the γ phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"β-phosphate","note":"the middle one"},{"t":"hexokinase","note":"a kinase"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"gets one phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Name ATP's phosphates** from the sugar out: α, β, γ.","fig":"atp"},{"text":"**A kinase moves the end one.** The γ phosphate goes to the substrate; α and β stay as ADP.","calc":["ATP → ADP + γ-phosphate (to substrate)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a kinase is the catalyst; the phosphate it attaches comes from ATP's γ phosphate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** kinases transfer ATP's γ (terminal) phosphate; any label on α or β stays in ADP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-33a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:33#1","pe1-cp:33#2"],"q":"In 1,2-dichloroethene, ClHC=CHCl, each carbon of the double bond is bonded to three atoms and has no lone pairs. What are its hybridization and geometry?","choices":["sp²; trigonal planar","sp³; trigonal pyramidal","sp³; tetrahedral","sp; linear"],"ans":0,"exp":"Three bonded atoms and no lone pairs give **three domains**, so each alkene carbon is **sp²**: trigonal planar with 120° angles. The one p orbital left unmixed on each carbon forms the π bond of C=C. sp³ needs four domains (tetrahedral, or trigonal pyramidal with a lone pair); sp needs two (linear).","how":{"ask":"Count the electron domains on an alkene carbon and convert them to a hybridization and shape.","know":"• Electron domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double bond counts once).\n• **4 → sp³**, tetrahedral, 109.5° (with one lone pair: trigonal pyramidal); **3 → sp²**, trigonal planar, 120°; **2 → sp**, linear, 180°.","steps":[{"text":"Count the domains on one alkene carbon.","hl":"bonded to three atoms and has no lone pairs","calc":["domains = 3 atoms (Cl, H, C) + 0 lone pairs = **3**"]},{"text":"Three domains → three sp² hybrid orbitals, spread as far apart as possible in one plane.","calc":["3 domains → **sp²**","angles ≈ 120° → **trigonal planar**"]},{"text":"sp³ needs four domains: tetrahedral with four atoms, trigonal pyramidal with three atoms plus a lone pair. These carbons have neither.","strike":["sp³; trigonal pyramidal","sp³; tetrahedral"]},{"text":"sp needs only two domains, as in a C≡C or C≡N carbon.","strike":["sp; linear"]},{"text":"Answer: sp²; trigonal planar."}],"trap":"sp³; trigonal pyramidal also has three atoms around the center, but that shape needs a fourth domain, a lone pair, which these alkene carbons do not have."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ClHC=CHCl","note":"C=C double bond"},{"t":"three atoms","note":"3 bonded groups"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"nothing extra"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** are the regions of electrons around an atom: each bonded atom counts once (single, double or triple bond), and each lone pair counts once."},{"text":"**Domains → hybridization → shape.**","calc":["2 → sp → linear","3 → sp² → trigonal planar","4 → sp³ → tetrahedral"]},{"text":"**Also:** cis/trans isomers exist because a C=C cannot rotate without breaking its π bond."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each carbon of a C=C has 3 domains, so it is sp² and trigonal planar (120°)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-34b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:34#2","pe1-cp:34#1"],"q":"Pancreatic lipase digests a dietary triacylglycerol made of three oleic acid chains. Which final products does the enzyme release?","choices":["Two fatty acids; 2-monoacylglycerol","Three fatty acids; glycerol","One fatty acid; 1,2-diacylglycerol","Two fatty acids; 1-monoacylglycerol"],"ans":0,"exp":"Pancreatic lipase cuts only the esters at **positions 1 and 3** of glycerol. Two cuts free **two oleic acids**, and the acyl chain at **C2** stays on: a **2-monoacylglycerol**. Glycerol would need three cuts and a diacylglycerol only one, and a 1-monoacylglycerol would mean the middle ester had been cut.","how":{"ask":"Use the enzyme's position rule to count the cuts and see which chain stays on glycerol.","know":"• Glycerol carbons: C1 and C3 are the ends; C2 is the middle.\n• **Pancreatic lipase** hydrolyzes the **C1 and C3** esters only.\n• Products: **2 fatty acids + 2-monoacylglycerol** (named for the carbon still holding a chain).","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme is pancreatic lipase, which works at the end positions.","hl":"Pancreatic lipase"},{"text":"Apply the position rule.","calc":["cut C1 ester → 1 oleic acid","cut C3 ester → 1 oleic acid","C2 ester kept → **2-monoacylglycerol**","total: **2** fatty acids"]},{"text":"Three cuts or one cut does not match the two end positions.","strike":["Three fatty acids; glycerol","One fatty acid; 1,2-diacylglycerol"]},{"text":"A chain left on C1 would mean the middle ester was cut, which this enzyme does not do.","strike":["Two fatty acids; 1-monoacylglycerol"]},{"text":"Answer: two fatty acids and a 2-monoacylglycerol."}],"trap":"Assuming digestion goes all the way to glycerol: pancreatic lipase leaves the middle chain in place."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Pancreatic lipase","note":"cuts C1 and C3"},{"t":"three oleic acid chains","note":"three esters to start"}]},{"text":"**Number glycerol's carbons** 1, 2, 3; each holds one ester.","fig":"triacylglycerol"},{"text":"**Cut only the ends.** Two acids leave; the middle chain stays.","calc":["3 chains − 2 cut = 1 left, on C2"]},{"text":"**Also:** lipases hydrolyze the ester bonds of triacylglycerols, releasing free fatty acids."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes positions 1 and 3, giving two fatty acids and a 2-monoacylglycerol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-35b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:35#2","pe1-cp:35#1"],"q":"Four residues of a soluble protein are replaced: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His. What is the change in the protein's net charge at pH 7?","choices":["A decrease of 6","A decrease of 5","No change","An increase of 6"],"ans":0,"exp":"At pH 7, Lys and Arg are **+1**, Glu and Asp are **−1**, and Ala and His are **≈ 0**. Each basic-to-acidic swap goes from +1 to −1, a change of **−2**; there are three of them. Ala→His changes nothing. Total: −2 − 2 − 2 + 0 = **−6**.","how":{"ask":"For each substitution, subtract the old side-chain charge from the new one at pH 7, then add the four changes.","know":"• Charges at pH 7: Arg/Lys **+1**, Asp/Glu **−1**, His **≈ 0**, others **0**.\n• Change at each site = **new − old**.\n• Net change = sum of the site changes.","steps":[{"text":"List the swaps.","hl":"Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His"},{"text":"Compute new − old at each site.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc","glutamate","aspartate-sc","histidine-sc"],"calc":["Lys→Glu: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Arg→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Lys→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Ala→His: 0 − 0 = **0**"]},{"text":"Add them.","calc":["Δ = −2 + (−2) + (−2) + 0 = **−6**"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives −2 − 2 − 2 + 1 = −5, but at pH 7 His is ≈ 0.","strike":["A decrease of 5"]},{"text":"Seeing three charged residues swapped for three charged residues as no change ignores the sign: each swap trades +1 for −1, a change of −2.","strike":["No change"]},{"text":"Positive charges are replaced by negative ones, so the net charge falls; an increase has the sign flipped.","strike":["An increase of 6"]},{"text":"Answer: a decrease of 6 (Δ = −6)."}],"trap":"Counting His as +1 at pH 7: its side-chain pKa is about 6, so at pH 7 it is mostly neutral and Ala→His adds nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Four residues","note":"four sites to add"},{"t":"pH 7","note":"use pH 7 charges"},{"t":"net charge","note":"sum of changes"}]},{"text":"**Assign charges at pH 7.**","calc":["Lys, Arg: +1","Glu, Asp: −1","Ala, His: 0"]},{"text":"**Change per site = new − old.** A + → − swap is worth 2.","calc":["(−1) − (+1) = −2"]},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7, count Arg and Lys as +1, Asp and Glu as −1, and His and everything else as 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each substitution take new charge minus old charge at the stated pH, then add all the site changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-36a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:36#1","pe1-cp:36#2"],"q":"After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%. Which conclusion fits?","choices":["Only mutant A is more stable","Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable","Neither mutant is more stable"],"ans":0,"exp":"Retained activity is the measure of heat stability: more activity left means less damage. Mutant A kept **65% vs the wild type's 30%**, so it is more stable. Mutant B kept **25%**, slightly **less** than the wild type, so it is not more stable.","how":{"ask":"Line up each mutant's retained activity against the wild type's and decide which are higher.","know":"• **% activity kept after heating**: higher → more heat-stable.\n• Compare every variant with the **wild type**, the reference.\n• A conclusion about \"both\" fails if even one variant does not fit.","steps":[{"text":"Find the reference and each variant's value.","hl":"wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%"},{"text":"Compare each with the wild type.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Activity kept after heating","x":{"label":"Enzyme"},"y":{"label":"Activity kept (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Wild type","Mutant A","Mutant B"],"series":[{"name":"Kept","values":[30,65,25]}]},"calc":["A: 65% > 30% → **more stable**","B: 25% ≤ 30% → **not more stable**"]},{"text":"Mutant B kept less than the wild type, so it is neither more stable alone nor along with A.","strike":["Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable"]},{"text":"Mutant A clearly kept more, so it is wrong that neither is more stable.","strike":["Neither mutant is more stable"]},{"text":"Answer: only mutant A is more stable."}],"trap":"Assuming every mutant behaves alike: check each one against the wild type separately."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"55 °C","note":"heating step"},{"t":"30%","note":"wild type, the reference"},{"t":"65%","note":"mutant A"},{"t":"25%","note":"mutant B"}]},{"text":"**Heat, cool, assay.** The activity that survives measures how well the enzyme resists heat.","calc":["% kept ↑ → heat stability ↑"]},{"text":"**Subtract the reference.**","calc":["A: 65 − 30 = +35","B: 25 − 30 = −5"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep only the claim the numbers show; reject absolute words like all, fully or every when any activity remains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare each variant's retained activity with the wild type's; higher means more heat-stable, lower means less."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-37a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:37#1"],"q":"A urine sample from patient A has a pH of 5.8, and a sample from patient B has a pH of 7.8. How does [H⁺] in sample B compare with sample A?","choices":["100 times lower","100 times higher","2 times lower","20 times lower"],"ans":0,"exp":"Because **pH = −log[H⁺]**, one pH unit corresponds to a **tenfold** change in [H⁺]. Sample B is 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units higher, so its [H⁺] is 10² = **100 times lower**: about 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M versus 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M.","how":{"ask":"Find the pH difference, turn it into a power of ten, and decide whether a higher pH means more or less H⁺.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units = a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺].\n• **Higher pH = less H⁺**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the pH change from A to B.","hl":"a pH of 5.8","calc":["ΔpH = 7.8 − 5.8 = **2** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"a pH of 7.8","calc":["factor = 10² = **100**","pH 5.8: [H⁺] = 10^−5.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M","pH 7.8: [H⁺] = 10^−7.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ = **100**"],"strike":["2 times lower","20 times lower"]},{"text":"Direction: B has the higher pH, so it has less H⁺.","strike":["100 times higher"]},{"text":"Answer: 100 times lower."}],"trap":"\"2 times lower\" treats pH as linear; every pH unit is a tenfold change, so 2 units is 10 × 10 = 100."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what you must compare.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 5.8","note":"sample A"},{"t":"a pH of 7.8","note":"sample B"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).","calc":["pH 5 → 10⁻⁵ M","pH 6 → 10⁻⁶ M (10 times less)"]},{"text":"**Subtract, then raise 10 to that power.** Then set the direction: higher pH, less H⁺.","calc":["factor = 10^(ΔpH)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a change of Δ pH units changes [H⁺] by a factor of 10^Δ, and a lower pH means more H⁺."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-38b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:38#2","pe1-cp:38#1","pe1-cp:38#3"],"q":"A glucopyranose has a phosphate on the ring carbon that is bonded to C5 but not to the ring oxygen. What is the compound?","choices":["Glucose 4-phosphate","Glucose 6-phosphate","Glucose 3-phosphate","Glucose 1-phosphate"],"ans":0,"exp":"C5 is bonded to the ring O, to **C6** (the –CH₂OH outside the ring) and to **C4**. The only other **ring** carbon on C5 is C4, so the phosphate is on C4: **glucose 4-phosphate**. A sugar phosphate is named by the number of the carbon that holds the phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Which ring carbon is next to C5 on the side away from the ring O, and what number is it?","know":"• Ring order: O – C1 – C2 – C3 – C4 – C5 – O.\n• C5's neighbors: ring O, C4 (ring), C6 (outside).\n• Name = glucose **n**-phosphate, where n is the carbon carrying the phosphate.","steps":[{"text":"The clue locates the phosphate relative to C5.","hl":"bonded to C5 but not to the ring oxygen"},{"text":"List C5's neighbors.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["C5 → ring O, **C4**, C6","ring carbon among them: **C4**"]},{"text":"C6 is bonded to C5 but sits outside the ring, so it is not a ring carbon.","strike":["Glucose 6-phosphate"]},{"text":"C3 is two carbons from C5, and C1 is bonded to the ring O.","strike":["Glucose 3-phosphate","Glucose 1-phosphate"]},{"text":"Answer: glucose 4-phosphate."}],"trap":"Reaching for glucose 6-phosphate because it is the familiar one: C6 is bonded to C5 but is the CH₂OH outside the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ring carbon","note":"C1–C5 only"},{"t":"bonded to C5","note":"C4 or C6"},{"t":"not to the ring oxygen","note":"rules out C1"}]},{"text":"**Walk the ring.** Every carbon number follows from the ring order.","calc":["O – C1 – C2 – C3 – C4 – C5 – O","C6 hangs off C5"]},{"text":"**Name by carbon number.** The number in a sugar phosphate's name is the carbon bearing the phosphate.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"**Also:** in glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 is bonded to the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH."},{"text":"**Also:** always put the phosphate where the passage says, then number the ring from C1; do not default to C6."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the ring from C1, find the carbon that carries the phosphate, and name it glucose n-phosphate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#2","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:43#1"],"q":"Glycogen's branching enzyme cuts a short block of glucose residues from a chain end and reattaches it to an interior residue. Which bond attaches the moved block to the interior residue?","choices":["α-1,6","α-1,4","β-1,4","β-1,6"],"ans":0,"exp":"The branching enzyme breaks an α-1,4 bond near the chain end and joins the released block's C1 to the **C6 –OH** of an interior glucose. That **α-1,6** bond is a new branch point. α-1,4 bonds make the linear chain, and glycogen has no β links.","how":{"ask":"When a block is moved to an interior residue to start a branch, which carbon of that residue does it bond to?","know":"• **Branching enzyme**: cuts an α-1,4 bond and forms an **α-1,6** bond (a branch point).\n• **Glycogen synthase**: forms α-1,4 bonds (chain growth).\n• β links appear in cellulose, not glycogen.","steps":[{"text":"The block is moved onto an interior residue: a new branch.","hl":"reattaches it to an interior residue"},{"text":"A branch attaches through C6.","calc":["block C1 → interior C6","→ **α-1,6** branch point"]},{"text":"α-1,4 is the bond that was cut, the linear chain bond made by glycogen synthase.","strike":["α-1,4"]},{"text":"Glycogen is built only from α links.","strike":["β-1,4","β-1,6"]},{"text":"Answer: α-1,6."}],"trap":"Choosing α-1,4 because the block is a piece of chain: inside the block the bonds are α-1,4, but the new bond to the interior residue is α-1,6."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"branching enzyme","note":"makes branch points"},{"t":"interior residue","note":"bonds through C6"}]},{"text":"**Two bonds in glycogen.** α-1,4 runs along a chain; α-1,6 starts a branch.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["chain: C1–O–C4","branch: C1–O–C6"]},{"text":"**The branching enzyme cuts one and makes the other.**","calc":["break α-1,4 → form α-1,6"]},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase forms α-1,4 bonds; the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** branch points are α-1,6 bonds, so fewer branches means fewer α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds, one at each branch point."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39c","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#3"],"q":"On a Haworth drawing of a D-glucose residue, its CH₂OH points up while C1's link to the next residue points down. How is this glycosidic link classified?","choices":["α, as in glycogen","β, as in glycogen","α, as in cellulose","β, as in cellulose"],"ans":0,"exp":"For a D-sugar in a Haworth drawing, compare the anomeric (C1) bond with the CH₂OH: **opposite faces → α**, same face → β. Here CH₂OH is up and the C1 bond is down, so the link is **α**, the type found in glycogen and starch. Cellulose uses β links.","how":{"ask":"Is the C1 bond on the same face as the CH₂OH or on the opposite face?","know":"• **D-sugar, Haworth**: C1 substituent **opposite** CH₂OH → **α**; **same** face → **β**.\n• Glycogen and starch: α-1,4 (+ α-1,6). Cellulose: β-1,4.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two directions.","hl":"its CH₂OH points up while C1's link to the next residue points down"},{"text":"Opposite faces → α.","fig":["t1-maltose","t1-cello-beta"],"calc":["CH₂OH up, C1 bond down → opposite → **α**"]},{"text":"β would need the C1 bond on the same face as CH₂OH.","strike":["β, as in glycogen","β, as in cellulose"]},{"text":"Cellulose is built from β links, so an α link is not cellulose's.","strike":["α, as in cellulose"]},{"text":"Answer: α, as in glycogen."}],"trap":"Matching α with cellulose: α links make glycogen and starch; cellulose is β-1,4."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂OH points up","note":"the reference"},{"t":"C1's link to the next residue points down","note":"the anomeric bond"}]},{"text":"**Compare C1 with CH₂OH.** Opposite faces = α; same face = β.","fig":["alpha-glucopyranose","beta-glucopyranose"]},{"text":"**Match to the polymer.** Glycogen and starch are α-linked; cellulose is β-linked.","calc":["α-1,4: glycogen, starch","β-1,4: cellulose"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Haworth projection, a C1 bond opposite the CH₂OH is α and one on the same face is β."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-40a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:40#1","pe1-cp:40#2"],"q":"In lactose synthesis, UDP-galactose donates its galactose. Apart from galactose, which sugar ring is part of UDP-galactose?","choices":["Ribofuranose","Deoxyribofuranose","Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"],"ans":0,"exp":"UDP is **uridine diphosphate**. Uridine is uracil bonded to **ribose**, a five-membered (furanose) ring with –OH groups on C2′ and C3′. UDP is an RNA-type nucleotide, so its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose; glucose and fructose are not part of it.","how":{"ask":"What does UDP stand for, and which sugar is in that nucleoside?","know":"• **UDP** = uridine diphosphate; **uridine** = uracil + **ribose**.\n• Nucleotides without a \"d\" (ATP, UTP, UDP, CTP) carry **ribose**.\n• Deoxyribose appears in DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…).","steps":[{"text":"The molecule is galactose attached to UDP.","hl":"UDP-galactose"},{"text":"Expand the name.","fig":["uracil","ribose"],"calc":["UDP = uridine diphosphate","uridine = uracil + **ribose** (furanose)"]},{"text":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose.","strike":["Deoxyribofuranose"]},{"text":"Glucose and fructose are not part of a uridine nucleotide.","strike":["Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"]},{"text":"Answer: ribofuranose."}],"trap":"Picking glucose because UDP-sugars are usually UDP-glucose: the attached sugar varies, but the nucleotide's own sugar is always ribose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"UDP-galactose","note":"galactose + UDP"},{"t":"Apart from galactose","note":"the nucleotide's sugar"}]},{"text":"**Read UDP.** Uridine diphosphate: uracil + ribose + two phosphates.","fig":"ribose","calc":["U = uridine","D = di (2 phosphates)","P = phosphate"]},{"text":"**Ribose vs deoxyribose.** Ribose has –OH on C2′ and C3′; deoxyribose lacks the 2′ –OH.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Also:** deoxyribose is found in DNA nucleotides such as dTTP; nucleotides without the \"d\" carry ribose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UDP is uridine diphosphate, and uridine contains ribose."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:41#2"],"q":"A phosphorylated enzyme carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines. It is incubated with a protein phosphatase. Which label is lost from the enzyme?","choices":["³²P only","³H only","Both labels","Neither label"],"ans":0,"exp":"A phosphatase **hydrolyzes phosphate esters**, releasing the phosphate as free Pᵢ. The ³²P rides on that phosphate, so it leaves the enzyme. The ³H is built into the leucine side chains, which the phosphatase does not touch, so it stays.","how":{"ask":"Place each label on its chemical group, then ask which group the phosphatase removes.","know":"• **Phosphatase**: R–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → R–OH + Pᵢ (removes phosphate).\n• A radioactive label goes wherever its atom goes.\n• Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase takes it off.","steps":[{"text":"Locate each label.","hl":"³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines","calc":["³²P → phosphate groups","³H → leucine side chains"]},{"text":"The phosphatase cuts off the phosphate.","calc":["Ser–O–PO₃²⁻(³²P) + H₂O → Ser–OH + Pᵢ(³²P)","³²P **leaves**; ³H **stays**"]},{"text":"Leucine is untouched, so ³H cannot be lost; and removing phosphate must take ³²P with it.","strike":["³H only","Both labels","Neither label"]},{"text":"Answer: ³²P only."}],"trap":"Thinking the phosphatase degrades the whole protein: it removes only phosphate groups."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"³²P in its phosphoserines","note":"label on phosphate"},{"t":"³H in its leucines","note":"label on the protein"},{"t":"protein phosphatase","note":"removes phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Each isotope tags one part.** Phosphorus is only in the phosphate; hydrogen here is in leucine.","fig":"leucine"},{"text":"**Phosphatase = phosphate off.** The phosphate leaves as free Pᵢ.","calc":["phospho-protein + H₂O → protein + Pᵢ"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase removes only phosphate, so a ¹⁴C label in the sugar backbone stays with the sugar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphatase removes phosphate groups, so a ³²P label on them leaves while labels elsewhere stay."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-42b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:42#2","pe1-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"q":"Strontium-90 and iodine-131, two fission products in reactor waste, are both β⁻ emitters. Which daughter nuclides do they form?","choices":["Yttrium-90 and xenon-131","Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"],"ans":0,"exp":"β⁻ decay keeps **A** and raises **Z by 1**. Sr (Z = 38) → **Y (39)**, A stays 90; I (Z = 53) → **Xe (54)**, A stays 131. Rubidium and tellurium lower Z (β⁺), and krypton-86 with antimony-127 lose 4 mass units (α).","how":{"ask":"Apply A unchanged, Z + 1 to each parent, then name the element with the new Z.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: A unchanged, **Z + 1**.\n• Z sets the element: Kr 36, Rb 37, Sr 38, Y 39; Sb 51, Te 52, I 53, Xe 54.","steps":[{"text":"Both parents are β⁻ emitters: keep A, add 1 to Z.","hl":"both β⁻ emitters","calc":["A → A, Z → Z + 1"]},{"text":"Strontium-90 (Z = 38).","hl":"Strontium-90","calc":["⁹⁰₃₈Sr → ⁹⁰₃₉Y + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 38 = 39 + (−1) ✓   A: 90 = 90 ✓"]},{"text":"Iodine-131 (Z = 53).","hl":"iodine-131","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 53 = 54 + (−1) ✓   A: 131 = 131 ✓"]},{"text":"Rubidium and tellurium have Z − 1 (positron emission); one correct daughter paired with tellurium is still wrong; krypton-86 and antimony-127 are alpha products.","strike":["Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"]},{"text":"Answer: yttrium-90 and xenon-131."}],"trap":"Moving one step left on the periodic table (rubidium, tellurium): that is β⁺ decay. β⁻ moves one step right."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the parents and the decay type**.","marks":[{"t":"Strontium-90","note":"Z = 38, A = 90"},{"t":"iodine-131","note":"Z = 53, A = 131"},{"t":"β⁻ emitters","note":"A same, Z + 1"}]},{"text":"**Keep the mass number.** The emitted electron carries no nucleons.","calc":["daughters: A = 90 and A = 131"]},{"text":"**Step one element to the right.** Z + 1 is the next element in the periodic table.","calc":["Sr (38) → Y (39)","I (53) → Xe (54)"]},{"text":"**Also:** in β⁻ decay one neutron turns into a proton, emitting the electron and an antineutrino, so the daughter has one more proton and one fewer neutron.","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino","Sr-90: 38 p, 52 n → Y-90: 39 p, 51 n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for β⁻ decay, keep A and move one element right (Z + 1) to name the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-44c","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:44#3","pe1-cp:44#1","pe1-cp:44#2"],"q":"Arsenic acid, H₃AsO₄, is triprotic. Which of these acids dissociates to the smallest extent when dissolved in water?","choices":["HAsO₄²⁻","H₂AsO₄⁻","H₃AsO₄","HCl"],"ans":0,"exp":"HCl is a strong acid. Arsenic acid's three Ka values fall steeply (pKa ≈ 2.2, 7.0, 11.5) because each H⁺ comes off a more negative ion. **HAsO₄²⁻** must lose H⁺ from a 2− ion, so it has the smallest Ka and dissociates least, just as HPO₄²⁻ does in the phosphate series.","how":{"ask":"Rank the four by Ka: the strong acid first, then each successive arsenic-acid step.","know":"• A strong acid (HCl) ionizes completely.\n• Triprotic acid: H₃A → H₂A⁻ → HA²⁻ → A³⁻, with Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃.\n• The most negative species that still has an H⁺ is the weakest acid.","steps":[{"text":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely.","strike":["HCl"]},{"text":"Write the three arsenic-acid steps.","hl":"is triprotic","calc":["H₃AsO₄ ⇌ H⁺ + H₂AsO₄⁻   pKa₁ ≈ 2.2","H₂AsO₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + HAsO₄²⁻   pKa₂ ≈ 7.0","HAsO₄²⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + AsO₄³⁻   pKa₃ ≈ 11.5"]},{"text":"Each step is about 10⁴–10⁵ times weaker than the one before, because H⁺ leaves an ever more negative ion.","calc":["Ka₁ ≈ 6 × 10⁻³ > Ka₂ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁷ > Ka₃ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹²"],"strike":["H₃AsO₄","H₂AsO₄⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HAsO₄²⁻."}],"trap":"H₃AsO₄ is the weak acid named in the stem, but it is the strongest of the three arsenic species; its ions are weaker still."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"triprotic","note":"three H⁺ to lose"},{"t":"smallest extent","note":"weakest acid, smallest Ka"}]},{"text":"**Strong acids are out:** HCl ionizes completely, so it dissociates most."},{"text":"**Line up the arsenic species by charge:** neutral H₃AsO₄, then 1− H₂AsO₄⁻, then 2− HAsO₄²⁻. The more negative the ion, the more tightly it holds H⁺.","calc":["0 → 1− → 2−","Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** HCl and H₂SO₄ (first H⁺) are strong acids; HF, H₃PO₄ and H₂CO₃ are weak."},{"text":"**Also:** each successive Ka of a polyprotic acid is much smaller, because H⁺ is harder to remove from a more negative ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a polyprotic series, the most negative ion that still carries an H⁺ (HPO₄²⁻, HAsO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻) is the weakest acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-46a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:46#1"],"q":"Which emission leaves a nucleus with the same mass number A and the same atomic number Z as before?","choices":["A gamma photon","An alpha particle","A beta-minus electron","A positron"],"ans":0,"exp":"A **gamma ray is a photon**: no mass number and no charge, so A and Z stay put. **α** (a ⁴He nucleus) lowers A by 4 and Z by 2; **β⁻** (an electron) raises Z by 1; **β⁺** (a positron) lowers Z by 1.","how":{"ask":"For each emission, write what it carries away in A and Z; find the one that carries away zero of both.","know":"• **α** = ⁴₂He: A − 4, Z − 2.\n• **β⁻** = electron (⁰₋₁e): Z + 1, A same.\n• **β⁺** = positron (⁰₊₁e): Z − 1, A same.\n• **γ** = high-energy photon (⁰₀γ): A and Z unchanged; the nucleus just drops from an excited state.","steps":[{"text":"Alpha decay removes two protons and two neutrons.","calc":["²²²₈₆Rn → ²¹⁸₈₄Po + ⁴₂He","A: 222 → 218, Z: 86 → 84"],"strike":["An alpha particle"]},{"text":"Beta decays keep A but change Z by one.","calc":["β⁻: ³₁H → ³₂He + ⁰₋₁e (Z + 1)","β⁺: ¹⁸₉F → ¹⁸₈O + ⁰₊₁e (Z − 1)"],"strike":["A beta-minus electron","A positron"]},{"text":"Gamma emission carries away energy only.","hl":"the same mass number A and the same atomic number Z","calc":["¹³⁷ᵐ₅₆Ba → ¹³⁷₅₆Ba + ⁰₀γ","A: 137 → 137, Z: 56 → 56"]},{"text":"Answer: a gamma photon."}],"trap":"Choosing the positron or electron because A is unchanged. Beta particles carry charge, so Z changes by one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what must stay the same**.","marks":[{"t":"mass number A","note":"protons + neutrons"},{"t":"atomic number Z","note":"protons: the element"}]},{"text":"**Track A and Z for each emission.**","calc":["α (⁴He): A − 4, Z − 2","β⁻ (electron): Z + 1","β⁺ (positron): Z − 1","γ (photon): no change"]},{"text":"**A gamma ray is light.** An excited nucleus releases its extra energy as a photon, like an excited atom emitting light.","calc":["X* → X + γ"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** gamma decay emits a photon and changes neither A nor Z; α is ⁴He, β⁻ is an electron, β⁺ is a positron."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-47c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:47#3","pe1-cp:47#1","pe1-cp:47#2","pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"q":"A reaction that releases a gas is spontaneous only above a certain temperature. Which signs of ΔH and ΔS fit ΔG = ΔH − TΔS?","choices":["ΔH positive, ΔS positive","ΔH negative, ΔS negative","ΔH negative, ΔS positive","ΔH positive, ΔS negative"],"ans":0,"exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, with **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**. Releasing a gas raises entropy, so **ΔS > 0** and the −TΔS term grows more negative as T rises. Being nonspontaneous at low T, where ΔG ≈ ΔH, requires **ΔH > 0**. ΔH < 0 with ΔS > 0 would be spontaneous at every temperature; ΔH > 0 with ΔS < 0 at none; both negative only at low T.","how":{"ask":"Which sign pair makes ΔG positive at low T but negative at high T?","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous when ΔG < 0.\n• At low T the ΔH term decides; at high T the −TΔS term decides.\n• A gas formed → **ΔS > 0**.\n• ΔH −, ΔS + → always; ΔH +, ΔS − → never; both + → high T only; both − → low T only.","steps":[{"text":"A gas is released, so entropy rises.","hl":"releases a gas","calc":["gas formed → ΔS > 0","−TΔS < 0, larger as T rises"]},{"text":"Not spontaneous at low T means ΔG > 0 there, where ΔG ≈ ΔH; at high T the −TΔS term wins.","hl":"spontaneous only above a certain temperature","calc":["low T: ΔG ≈ ΔH > 0 → ΔH > 0","high T: TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"A negative ΔH with a positive ΔS gives ΔG < 0 at every temperature, so there is no threshold.","calc":["ΔG = (−) − T(+) < 0 at all T"],"strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS positive"]},{"text":"A negative ΔS contradicts the gas release: with ΔH positive it is never spontaneous, and with ΔH negative it is spontaneous only below some temperature.","calc":["(+) − T(−) > 0 at all T","(−) − T(−) < 0 only at low T"],"strike":["ΔH positive, ΔS negative","ΔH negative, ΔS negative"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH positive, ΔS positive."}],"trap":"Choosing ΔH negative with ΔS positive because both terms favor spontaneity: that pair is spontaneous at every temperature, with no threshold to cross."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"releases a gas","note":"ΔS > 0"},{"t":"only above a certain temperature","note":"ΔG < 0 at high T only"},{"t":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS","note":"the sign test"}]},{"text":"**ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.** At low T the ΔH term dominates; as T rises, the −TΔS term takes over."},{"text":"**Four sign pairs.**","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS +: always spontaneous","ΔH +, ΔS −: never spontaneous","ΔH +, ΔS +: spontaneous above some T","ΔH −, ΔS −: spontaneous below some T"]},{"text":"**Also:** spontaneous means ΔG < 0, however slow the reaction."},{"text":"**Also:** a reaction that makes gas from a solid or solution has ΔS > 0."},{"text":"**Also:** for an endothermic reaction (ΔH > 0) with ΔS > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays positive, so the reaction is non-spontaneous, as long as ΔH is greater than TΔS. It becomes spontaneous once TΔS > ΔH, i.e. above T = ΔH/ΔS = 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K.","calc":["ΔH = +90 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.20 kJ/(mol·K), T = 300 K","TΔS = 300 × 0.20 = 60 kJ/mol < ΔH","ΔG = 90 − 60 = +30 kJ/mol → non-spontaneous","T > 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K → TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** by ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, a reaction that is spontaneous only at high temperature has both ΔH and ΔS positive."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#1","pe1-cp:48#2","pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"q":"Chlorine atoms destroy ozone in two steps: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂. What is the overall reaction?","choices":["O₃ + O → 2O₂","Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂","O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂","2O₃ → 3O₂"],"ans":0,"exp":"Add the two steps and cross out anything on both sides: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂ becomes **O₃ + O → 2O₂**. Cl is used and then regenerated (a catalyst), and ClO is made and then used (an intermediate), so neither appears overall. Leaving Cl on both sides is an unfinished sum; O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled; 2O₃ → 3O₂ is a different net reaction.","how":{"ask":"Add the steps, then cancel each species that appears on both sides.","know":"• **Overall reaction** = sum of the elementary steps (multiply a step if needed so shared species cancel).\n• Cancel species found on both sides: **catalysts** (used, then regenerated; a regenerated cofactor such as enzyme-bound FAD → FADH₂ → FAD cancels like a catalyst) and **intermediates** (made, then used).\n• What's left holds only the true reactants and products.","steps":[{"text":"Write both steps and add them.","hl":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂","calc":["Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","ClO + O → Cl + O₂","sum: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂"]},{"text":"Cancel Cl and ClO, each found on both sides, and combine the O₂.","calc":["cancel Cl (left and right)","cancel ClO (left and right)","**O₃ + O → 2O₂**","check: O atoms 3 + 1 = 4 = 2 × 2; charge 0 = 0 ✓"]},{"text":"Leaving Cl on both sides is a sum that was never simplified.","strike":["Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled.","strike":["O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"2O₃ → 3O₂ is balanced, but it uses no free O atom, so it is not the sum of these steps.","strike":["2O₃ → 3O₂"]},{"text":"Answer: O₃ + O → 2O₂."}],"trap":"Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂ is balanced and looks complete, but a species on both sides must be cancelled; an overall equation never shows a catalyst."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two steps","note":"a mechanism"},{"t":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","note":"step one"},{"t":"ClO + O → Cl + O₂","note":"step two"},{"t":"overall reaction","note":"sum and cancel"}]},{"text":"**A mechanism** is the list of elementary steps a reaction actually goes through. Adding them gives the overall equation."},{"text":"**Cancel like terms**, just as in algebra: a species on both sides drops out.","calc":["A + B → C","C + D → A + E","sum: B + D → E"]},{"text":"**Also:** after summing a mechanism, confirm that atoms and total charge (coefficient × charge) balance on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** add the steps, then cancel every species on both sides (catalysts and intermediates) to get the overall reaction."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#3"],"q":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) speeds up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. Which species is the catalyst?","choices":["Fe²⁺","Fe³⁺","I⁻","SO₄²⁻"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Fe²⁺ is used in the first step and regenerated in the second**, so it is the catalyst: present at the start and given back at the end. **Fe³⁺ is made in the first step and used up in the second**: an intermediate. I⁻ is a reactant and SO₄²⁻ a product; neither cancels. Overall: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂.","how":{"ask":"For each iron ion, is it consumed first and returned later, or made first and consumed later?","know":"• **Catalyst**: consumed in an early step, **regenerated** later; it is present before the reaction starts.\n• **Intermediate**: **produced** in an early step, consumed later; it is never added at the start.\n• Both cancel from the overall equation; reactants and products do not.","steps":[{"text":"Track Fe²⁺: a reactant in step one, a product in step two.","hl":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","calc":["step one: 2Fe²⁺ used","step two: 2Fe²⁺ made → **regenerated** → catalyst"]},{"text":"Track Fe³⁺: made in step one, used up in step two, so it is an intermediate.","hl":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","strike":["Fe³⁺"]},{"text":"I⁻ is only consumed and SO₄²⁻ only produced: a reactant and a product, which stay in the overall equation.","calc":["overall: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"],"strike":["I⁻","SO₄²⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: Fe²⁺."}],"trap":"Fe³⁺ also cancels from the overall equation, but it is made first and used later: that is an intermediate. A catalyst enters first as a reactant and is given back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺)","note":"added at the start"},{"t":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺","note":"step one reactants"},{"t":"2Fe²⁺ + I₂","note":"step two products"}]},{"text":"**Catalyst: in, then out.** It enters an early step as a reactant and leaves a later step as a product, unchanged overall."},{"text":"**Intermediate: out, then in.** It is made in an early step and used up in a later one.","calc":["catalyst: reactant (step one) → product (step two)","intermediate: product (step one) → reactant (step two)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst is used and then regenerated; an intermediate is made and then used up. Both cancel from the overall equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-49b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:49#1","pe1-cp:49#3"],"q":"A catalytic converter treats exhaust containing carbon monoxide and unburned propane. Taken together, which products does it form from these pollutants?","choices":["CO₂ and H₂O","CO and H₂","CH₃OH and H₂","C and H₂O"],"ans":0,"exp":"The converter **oxidizes** with O₂: carbon monoxide goes to **CO₂** (carbon +2 → +4), and a hydrocarbon such as propane burns completely to **CO₂ and H₂O**. Soot, CO, methanol or H₂ would be incomplete or reductive products.","how":{"ask":"What do CO and a hydrocarbon become when fully oxidized by O₂ over the catalyst?","know":"• The converter's oxidation job: **CO → CO₂** and **hydrocarbons → CO₂ + H₂O**.\n• Oxidation of carbon raises its oxidation number; full oxidation of carbon ends at +4 (CO₂).","steps":[{"text":"Note the two pollutants.","hl":"carbon monoxide and unburned propane","calc":["CO and C₃H₈"]},{"text":"Oxidize each with O₂ and balance.","calc":["2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂   (C: +2 → +4)","C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O"]},{"text":"CO and H₂, or soot and water, are incomplete products: carbon not taken all the way to +4 and H₂ not oxidized; the converter oxidizes fully to CO₂ and H₂O.","strike":["CO and H₂","C and H₂O"]},{"text":"Methanol plus H₂ is a partial, not complete, oxidation and is not what leaves the tailpipe.","strike":["CH₃OH and H₂"]},{"text":"Answer: CO₂ and H₂O."}],"trap":"Forgetting the hydrogen in a hydrocarbon: complete oxidation gives water as well as carbon dioxide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the pollutants**.","marks":[{"t":"carbon monoxide","note":"CO, C = +2"},{"t":"unburned propane","note":"hydrocarbon C₃H₈"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation side of the converter:** O₂ in the exhaust finishes burning anything carbon-based.","calc":["CO → CO₂","CₓHᵧ → CO₂ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Check by oxidation number:** carbon in CO is +2 and in CO₂ is +4, so carbon loses electrons: oxidation."},{"text":"**Also:** a solid catalyst acting on gases (a catalytic converter) is a heterogeneous catalyst."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalytic converter reduces nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) to N₂ while it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalytic converter oxidizes CO to CO₂ and unburned hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-51a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:51#1","pe1-cp:51#2","pe1-cp:51#3"],"q":"Equal masses of manganese dioxide catalyst, one as a single pellet and one as fine powder, are added to identical hydrogen peroxide solutions. Why does the powder release O₂ faster?","choices":["More exposed surface","Greater catalyst mass","Higher activation energy","Larger equilibrium constant"],"ans":0,"exp":"A solid catalyst works only at its **surface**, where H₂O₂ molecules meet it. Powder exposes far **more surface area** for the same mass, so more reaction sites act at once and the **rate rises**. The mass is equal, a catalyst lowers (not raises) Ea, and catalysts don't change K.","how":{"ask":"What differs between the pellet and the powder, given that their masses match?","know":"• A **heterogeneous catalyst reacts at its surface**.\n• Breaking a solid into small pieces greatly **raises surface area** without changing mass.\n• More surface → more active sites → **faster rate**.","steps":[{"text":"Note what is the same and what differs.","hl":"Equal masses of manganese dioxide catalyst","calc":["mass: same","form: pellet vs fine powder"]},{"text":"The masses are equal, so mass can't explain the difference.","strike":["Greater catalyst mass"]},{"text":"Powder exposes more surface where H₂O₂ meets the catalyst, so more molecules react per second.","calc":["surface area ↑ → active sites ↑ → rate ↑"]},{"text":"A catalyst lowers Ea, and a higher Ea would slow the reaction; neither form changes K.","strike":["Higher activation energy","Larger equilibrium constant"]},{"text":"Answer: more exposed surface."}],"trap":"Thinking grinding adds catalyst: the amount is unchanged; only the surface the reactants can reach grows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what was changed**.","marks":[{"t":"Equal masses","note":"mass held fixed"},{"t":"fine powder","note":"more surface"},{"t":"release O₂ faster","note":"rate"}]},{"text":"**Surface catalysis:** reactant molecules must touch the solid to react, so only surface atoms count."},{"text":"**Smaller pieces, more surface.** Cutting a solid exposes new faces that were inside before.","calc":["particle size ↓ → surface area ↑ → rate ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** grinding or cutting a catalyst raises its surface area but leaves its mass unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** cutting a cube into smaller cubes multiplies the total surface area by the ratio of the edge lengths."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** grinding a solid catalyst speeds the reaction because it exposes more surface area."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:52#1","pe1-cp:52#2","pe1-cp:52#3"],"q":"A 9.0-V battery powers a 30-Ω heater coil. Which replacement coil would draw the largest current from the battery?","choices":["A 12-Ω coil","A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"],"ans":0,"exp":"By **Ohm's law, I = V/R**, a fixed battery voltage drives more current through a smaller resistance. The 12-Ω coil draws 9.0 V ÷ 12 Ω = **0.75 A**, more than the 0.30 A now.","how":{"ask":"With the battery voltage fixed, which resistance gives the biggest I = V/R?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**.\n• Fixed V → current is **inversely proportional** to resistance: lower R, more current.","steps":[{"text":"The battery sets V; only R changes.","hl":"A 9.0-V battery","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Current now.","hl":"30-Ω heater coil","calc":["I = 9.0 V ÷ 30 Ω = **0.30 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the replacements.","calc":["12 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 12 = **0.75 A**","45 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 45 = 0.20 A","60 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 60 = 0.15 A","90 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 90 = 0.10 A"]},{"text":"The larger coils all lower the current below 0.30 A.","strike":["A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"]},{"text":"Answer: the 12-Ω coil."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger resistor \"uses more\" current. At fixed voltage, more resistance means less current."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"9.0-V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"30-Ω heater coil","note":"R now"},{"t":"largest current","note":"goal"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** the battery's push divided by the resistance gives the current.","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**Smaller R, bigger I.** Halve R and the current doubles.","calc":["R ↓ → I ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** a longer, thinner or hotter metal wire has more resistance; shorter, thicker or cooler has less."},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the ion concentrations a cell runs on lowers its voltage, and at fixed R that lowers the current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise the current from a fixed voltage, lower the resistance (I = V/R)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-53a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:53#1"],"q":"Standard reduction potentials: Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V. Strips of magnesium and silver are each dropped into 1 M HCl. Which produces H₂ gas?","choices":["Magnesium only","Silver only","Both magnesium and silver","Neither metal"],"ans":0,"exp":"H⁺/H₂ is the **zero reference**. Magnesium's E° is **negative** (−2.37 V), so Mg is more easily oxidized than H₂: Mg → Mg²⁺ while **H⁺ gains electrons and becomes H₂**. Silver's E° is **positive**, so Ag holds its electrons and does not reduce H⁺; no gas forms on it.","how":{"ask":"For each metal, is it easier to oxidize than H₂ (E° below that of H⁺/H₂)?","know":"• Reference: **2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, E° = 0 V**.\n• Metal with **E° < 0** is oxidized by H⁺: M → Mⁿ⁺ and H⁺ → H₂.\n• Metal with **E° > 0** does not react with H⁺.","steps":[{"text":"Note both potentials.","hl":"Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V","calc":["Mg: −2.37 V","Ag: +0.80 V","H⁺/H₂: 0 V"]},{"text":"Magnesium: E° < 0, so Mg is easier to oxidize than H₂ and reduces H⁺.","calc":["Mg + 2H⁺ → Mg²⁺ + H₂"]},{"text":"E° > 0: silver is harder to oxidize than H₂, so it cannot reduce H⁺.","strike":["Silver only","Both magnesium and silver"]},{"text":"Magnesium does react, so it is not the case that neither metal gives gas.","strike":["Neither metal"]},{"text":"Answer: magnesium only."}],"trap":"Thinking any metal dissolves in acid: only metals with E° below 0 V (more active than H₂) reduce H⁺ to H₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the potentials**.","marks":[{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"Mg: below 0"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: above 0"},{"t":"1 M HCl","note":"source of H⁺"},{"t":"H₂ gas","note":"H⁺ must be reduced"}]},{"text":"**H⁺/H₂ sits at 0 V.** A metal below it gives electrons to H⁺; a metal above it doesn't.","calc":["E°(metal) < 0 → metal oxidized, H⁺ → H₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal more easily oxidized than H₂ (E° < 0) is oxidized by acid and H⁺ is reduced to H₂ gas."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-54a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:54#1","pe1-cp:54#2"],"q":"Standard reduction potentials: Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Ni²⁺/Ni, −0.25 V. An iron nail is placed in nickel(II) sulfate solution. Which species is oxidized?","choices":["Iron metal","Nickel metal","Iron(II) ions","Nickel(II) ions"],"ans":0,"exp":"The half-reaction with the **lower reduction potential runs backward** as oxidation. Iron's E° (−0.44 V) is lower than nickel's (−0.25 V), so **Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻**, and Ni²⁺ is reduced to nickel metal.","how":{"ask":"Which half-reaction has the lower E°, and so runs as an oxidation?","know":"• **Higher E°** → reduced (cathode). **Lower E°** → oxidized (anode).\n• Only the reduced form of a couple (the metal) can be oxidized; the ion is already oxidized.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two potentials.","hl":"Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V","calc":["Fe²⁺/Fe: −0.44 V (lower)","Ni²⁺/Ni: −0.25 V (higher)"]},{"text":"Lower E° is oxidized: iron metal gives up electrons.","calc":["Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻"]},{"text":"Nickel(II) ions are reduced, not oxidized; nickel metal is the product they form.","calc":["Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni"],"strike":["Nickel(II) ions","Nickel metal"]},{"text":"Fe²⁺ is the product of the oxidation, not the species being oxidized.","strike":["Iron(II) ions"]},{"text":"Answer: iron metal."}],"trap":"Picking the ion of the more negative couple: Fe²⁺ is what iron becomes, not what gets oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the potentials**.","marks":[{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"Fe: lower"},{"t":"−0.25 V","note":"Ni: higher"},{"t":"iron nail","note":"Fe metal"},{"t":"nickel(II) sulfate","note":"Ni²⁺ ions"}]},{"text":"**Higher E° wins the electrons.** Ni²⁺ has the higher E°, so it is reduced."},{"text":"**The other one is oxidized.** Iron's half-reaction runs in reverse.","calc":["Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻","Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni"]},{"text":"**Also:** balance a redox equation by multiplying the half-reactions so the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, then check the charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** of two half-reactions, the metal with the lower reduction potential is oxidized."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-55a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:55#1","pe1-cp:55#2","pe1-cp:55#3"],"q":"In a galvanic cell, copper is the cathode (E° = +0.34 V) and iron is the anode (E° = −0.44 V). What is E°cell?","choices":["+0.78 V","−0.10 V","+0.34 V","+0.44 V"],"ans":0,"exp":"Subtract the anode from the cathode, **E°cell = E°red(cathode) − E°red(anode)**, using both reduction potentials: +0.34 − (−0.44) = **+0.78 V**. Adding the two values gives −0.10 V, and either electrode alone (+0.34 V or +0.44 V) is only half the cell.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the anode's reduction potential from the cathode's.","know":"• **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** (both as reduction potentials from the table).\n• Don't flip a sign and then also subtract; do one or the other.","steps":[{"text":"Note which electrode is which.","hl":"copper is the cathode (E° = +0.34 V)","calc":["E°cathode = +0.34 V","E°anode = −0.44 V"]},{"text":"Apply the formula.","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode","E°cell = +0.34 − (−0.44)","E°cell = +0.34 + 0.44 = **+0.78 V**"]},{"text":"Adding the two tabulated potentials skips the anode's sign change.","calc":["+0.34 + (−0.44) = −0.10 V ✗"],"strike":["−0.10 V"]},{"text":"+0.34 V is the cathode alone, and +0.44 V is only the anode's oxidation potential; the cell needs both.","calc":["oxidation of Fe: +0.44 V","reduction of Cu²⁺: +0.34 V","sum = +0.78 V"],"strike":["+0.34 V","+0.44 V"]},{"text":"Answer: +0.78 V."}],"trap":"Adding the two tabulated reduction potentials: that skips the sign change for the anode's oxidation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the roles and values**.","marks":[{"t":"copper is the cathode","note":"reduction"},{"t":"+0.34 V","note":"E°cathode"},{"t":"iron is the anode","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"E°anode"}]},{"text":"**The formula** uses both reduction potentials as listed.","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode"]},{"text":"**Watch the double negative.** Subtracting a negative number adds it.","calc":["+0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.34 + 0.44"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a galvanic cell, the cathode is the half-cell with the higher standard reduction potential."},{"text":"**Also:** a positive E°cell means spontaneous as written; a negative E°cell means not spontaneous (the reverse reaction is)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, with both values taken as reduction potentials."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-57a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:57#1","pe1-cp:57#2","pe1-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:56#1"],"q":"Solid sodium chloride is dissolved in a saturated silver chloride solution that sits over undissolved AgCl. Immediately afterward, how does the ion product Q compare with Ksp?","choices":["Above Ksp","Below Ksp","Equal to Ksp","Exactly zero"],"ans":0,"exp":"Before the addition the solution is saturated, so **Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = Ksp**. NaCl adds **Cl⁻, a common ion**, raising [Cl⁻] while [Ag⁺] is momentarily unchanged, so the product **Q now exceeds Ksp**.","how":{"ask":"What was Q before the addition, and which factor in Q just grew?","know":"• **Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]** at any moment; **Ksp** is its value at saturation.\n• Saturated: Q = Ksp. Adding a **common ion** raises one factor of Q.\n• Q > Ksp → precipitate forms; Q < Ksp → more dissolves.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the saturated solution.","hl":"saturated silver chloride solution","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻","Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = Ksp = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰","[Ag⁺] = [Cl⁻] = √(1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34 × 10⁻⁵ M"]},{"text":"NaCl adds Cl⁻. Suppose [Cl⁻] becomes 0.050 M.","hl":"Solid sodium chloride","calc":["Q = (1.34 × 10⁻⁵)(0.050)","Q = 6.7 × 10⁻⁷ > 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰"]},{"text":"Raising one factor can't leave Q equal to or below Ksp.","strike":["Below Ksp","Equal to Ksp"]},{"text":"Ag⁺ is still present, so Q is not zero.","strike":["Exactly zero"]},{"text":"Answer: Q is above Ksp."}],"trap":"Thinking Q stays at Ksp because the solution was saturated: saturation describes the moment before the common ion arrived."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sodium chloride","note":"adds Cl⁻, a common ion"},{"t":"saturated","note":"Q = Ksp before"},{"t":"Immediately afterward","note":"before any shift"}]},{"text":"**Q has the same form as Ksp** but uses the concentrations right now.","calc":["Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]"]},{"text":"**One factor up → Q up.** From Q = Ksp, any extra common ion pushes Q above Ksp.","calc":["[Cl⁻] ↑ → Q ↑ → Q > Ksp"]},{"text":"**Also (Le Châtelier):** adding a product, here the common ion, shifts the equilibrium left, so the sparingly soluble salt precipitates."},{"text":"**Also:** adding a common ion lowers a salt's solubility, but Ksp itself doesn't change unless temperature does."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a common ion to a saturated solution raises Q above Ksp."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-58a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:58#1","pe3-cp:21#3"],"q":"A recipe needs 2.34 g of sodium chloride (molar mass 58.5 g/mol), measured out from a 0.400 M NaCl stock. What volume of stock is required?","choices":["100 mL","16 mL","40 mL","5.85 L"],"ans":0,"exp":"First grams to moles: 2.34 g ÷ 58.5 g/mol = **0.0400 mol**. Then **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0400 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L = 0.100 L = **100 mL**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, then divide moles by molarity to get litres, then convert to mL.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass**.\n• **M = n ÷ V**, so **V = n ÷ M** (V in litres).\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Note the mass, molar mass and molarity.","hl":"2.34 g of sodium chloride","calc":["m = 2.34 g","molar mass = 58.5 g/mol","M = 0.400 mol/L"]},{"text":"Grams to moles.","calc":["n = 2.34 g ÷ 58.5 g/mol","n = 0.0400 mol"]},{"text":"Volume from V = n ÷ M, then mL.","calc":["V = 0.0400 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L","V = 0.100 L","V = 0.100 × 1000 = **100 mL**"]},{"text":"Multiplying n × M instead of dividing gives 16 mL, and stopping at the moles (0.0400 read as litres) gives 40 mL.","calc":["0.0400 × 0.400 = 0.016 L = 16 mL ✗","0.0400 → 40 mL skips ÷ M ✗"],"strike":["16 mL","40 mL"]},{"text":"Skipping the grams-to-moles step divides grams by molarity.","calc":["2.34 ÷ 0.400 = 5.85 ✗"],"strike":["5.85 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 100 mL."}],"trap":"Dividing grams by molarity: molarity is moles per litre, so the mass must become moles first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"2.34 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"58.5 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"0.400 M","note":"mol per L"}]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.**","calc":["n = mass ÷ molar mass"]},{"text":"**Moles → litres → mL.** Rearrange M = n/V.","calc":["V = n ÷ M","mL = L × 1000"]},{"text":"**Also:** going the other way, mass = M × V(L) × molar mass."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n ÷ M gives litres; convert grams to moles first and litres to mL at the end."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-59c","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:59#3","pe1-cp:59#1","pe1-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"q":"A rehab patient pushes a weighted sled with a steady 120-N force through 15 m in 6.0 s. What average power does the patient deliver?","choices":["300 W","1800 W","48 W","720 W"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Power = force × distance ÷ time**: W = 120 N × 15 m = 1800 J, and P = 1800 J ÷ 6.0 s = **300 W**. N·m/s = J/s = W.","how":{"ask":"Find the work as force × distance, then divide by the time.","know":"• **Work W = F·d** (N·m = J) when force and motion are in the same direction.\n• **Power P = W/t = F·d/t** (J/s = W).\n• Equivalently P = F·v.","steps":[{"text":"Work done on the sled.","hl":"a steady 120-N force through 15 m","calc":["W = F × d = 120 N × 15 m","W = **1800 J**"]},{"text":"Divide by the time.","hl":"6.0 s","calc":["P = W ÷ t = 1800 J ÷ 6.0 s","P = **300 W**","check: v = 15 m ÷ 6.0 s = 2.5 m/s; F·v = 120 × 2.5 = 300 W"]},{"text":"1800 W is the work in joules, never divided by time. 48 W uses F × t ÷ d (120 × 6.0 ÷ 15). 720 W multiplies force by time and ignores the distance.","strike":["1800 W","48 W","720 W"]},{"text":"Answer: 300 W."}],"trap":"Stopping at force × distance: 1800 J is energy. Power needs the division by time."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"120-N force","note":"F"},{"t":"15 m","note":"distance d"},{"t":"6.0 s","note":"time t"}]},{"text":"**Force × distance is work (energy).**","calc":["N·m = J","120 × 15 = 1800 J"]},{"text":"**Work ÷ time is power.**","calc":["J ÷ s = W","1800 ÷ 6.0 = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Also:** W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³; one more s⁻¹ than a joule means power."},{"text":"**Also:** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule (energy), not power; power needs kg·m²·s⁻³."},{"text":"**Also:** at constant speed the net force is zero, so the driving force equals the resisting force; then P = F·v.","calc":["constant speed: driving force = resisting force = 120 N","P = F·v = 120 N × 2.5 m/s = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** force × distance ÷ time is power (N·m/s = J/s = W); force × distance alone is energy."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 3 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-01a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:1#2","pe3-cp:1#3"],"q":"With base and heat, 1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, cyclizes to 3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one. Which two starting carbons become the ring's C=C?","choices":["C1 and C5","C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"],"ans":0,"exp":"Only the CH₃ (C5) can reach a carbonyl and close a five-membered ring: its enolate carbon bonds to **C1**, the phenyl ketone carbon. Losing water then puts the **C=C between C5 and C1**, conjugated with the C4 ketone, which stays a C=O.","how":{"ask":"Find which α-carbon attacks which carbonyl, then place the C=C made by dehydration.","know":"• **Aldol bond**: the enolate's **α-carbon** (nucleophile) bonds to the **other carbonyl carbon** (electrophile).\n• **Dehydration**: the C=C forms between the **former α-carbon** and the **former carbonyl carbon**, conjugated with the remaining C=O.\n• Number the chain, then follow each carbon by its neighbors.","steps":[{"text":"Number the chain from the phenyl end: C1 is the phenyl ketone carbon, C4 the methyl ketone carbon, C5 the CH₃.","hl":"C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃","calc":["C₆H₅–C1(=O)–C2H₂–C3H₂–C4(=O)–C5H₃"]},{"text":"Test each α-carbon. C2 or C3 attacking a carbonyl would make a three-membered ring; C5 attacking C1 makes a five-membered ring.","calc":["C5 → C1: ring = C5, C4, C3, C2, C1 = **5 atoms** ✓","C3 → C1 or C2 → C4: **3 atoms** ✗"]},{"text":"The new C5–C1 bond puts an OH on C1 (β to the C4 ketone). Dehydration removes that OH and an H from C5.","calc":["C=C: **C5 = C1**, conjugated with C4=O"]},{"text":"Check by neighbors: the phenyl is bonded to C1 at the start, and in the product it sits on ring C3, an alkene carbon.","hl":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one"},{"text":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; C4 stays the ketone, so it is never part of the C=C.","strike":["C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"]},{"text":"Answer: C1 and C5."}],"trap":"Putting the surviving C=O carbon (C4) into the C=C: that carbon keeps its oxygen, and the alkene forms at C1, the carbon that lost its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione","note":"two ketones, 1,4 apart"},{"t":"With base and heat","note":"aldol, then dehydration"},{"t":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one","note":"five-membered enone"}]},{"text":"**Number and label.** Write C1–C5 under the chain; mark which carbons are carbonyls (C1, C4) and which are α (C2, C3, C5)."},{"text":"**Make the aldol bond.** An α-carbon (as its enolate) bonds to the other carbonyl carbon. Pick the pair that closes a five- or six-membered ring.","calc":["C5 + C1 → 5-membered ring"]},{"text":"**Dehydrate.** The OH on the former carbonyl carbon leaves with an H from the former α-carbon, so the C=C joins those two and is conjugated with the C=O.","calc":["former α-C = former C=O carbon → α,β-unsaturated ketone"]},{"text":"**Also:** track carbons by their neighbors: a carbon bonded to a phenyl, or one next to a quaternary carbon (no H), keeps that neighbor in the product, so you can find it in the ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an intramolecular aldol condensation, the α-carbon bonds to the other carbonyl carbon, and the C=C forms between those same two carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-02a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:2#1"],"q":"Sulfonating naphthalene briefly at low temperature gives mainly the 1-isomer; heating for hours at high temperature, where the isomers interconvert, gives mainly the 2-isomer. Which conclusion follows?","choices":["The 2-isomer is lower in energy","The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea","Both isomers are equally low in energy"],"ans":0,"exp":"Only the long, hot run lets the isomers interconvert and reach **equilibrium**, where the **more stable** (lower-energy) product dominates: the 2-isomer. The brief, cold run shows **kinetic control**: the 1-isomer forms faster, so it has the lower Ea.","how":{"ask":"Decide which run reached equilibrium, then read stability from that run only.","know":"• **Thermodynamic control** (reversible, long time, higher T): the major product is the **more stable** one.\n• **Kinetic control** (irreversible, short time, low T): the major product is the one that **forms faster** (lower Ea).","steps":[{"text":"The hot, long run is reversible, so it reflects stability.","hl":"where the isomers interconvert","calc":["equilibrium → major = more stable = **2-isomer**"]},{"text":"The brief, cold run is irreversible, so it reflects rate.","hl":"briefly at low temperature","calc":["kinetic → major = faster = 1-isomer → lower Ea"]},{"text":"The 1-isomer wins only under kinetic control, so it is not the more stable; and since it forms faster, the 2-isomer has the higher Ea, not the lower.","strike":["The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea"]},{"text":"Equal stability would give a roughly even equilibrium mixture, not a dominant 2-isomer.","strike":["Both isomers are equally low in energy"]},{"text":"Answer: the 2-isomer is lower in energy."}],"trap":"Reading stability from the kinetic run: at low temperature and short times the faster-forming product wins, whatever its stability."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"briefly at low temperature","note":"kinetic conditions"},{"t":"heating for hours at high temperature","note":"thermodynamic conditions"},{"t":"interconvert","note":"equilibrium reached"}]},{"text":"**Sort the runs.** Interconversion means equilibrium; no interconversion means the first-formed product stays."},{"text":"**Equilibrium → stability.** The product favored at equilibrium has the lower free energy.","calc":["K = [2-isomer]/[1-isomer] > 1 → 2-isomer more stable"]},{"text":"**Kinetic → rate.** The product favored when nothing reverses is the one with the lower activation energy."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** under equilibrium (thermodynamic) conditions the more stable product is major; under kinetic conditions the faster-forming product is major."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:3#1","pe3-cp:3#2"],"q":"Propanal is treated with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product is isolated before any water is eliminated. Which compound is the product?","choices":["3-Hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","2-Methylpent-2-enal","2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"],"ans":0,"exp":"The enolate carbon of one propanal (C2, which carries the CH₃) bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second molecule. Without dehydration this **aldol addition** leaves a **β-OH**, two carbons from the aldehyde C=O: CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO, **3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal**. Losing water would give 2-methylpent-2-enal.","how":{"ask":"Join an α-carbon of one aldehyde to the carbonyl carbon of the other, and keep the OH.","know":"• **Aldol addition** gives a **β-hydroxy carbonyl**: the old C=O becomes C–OH, two carbons from the remaining C=O.\n• **Dehydration** (heat or more base) removes that OH and an α-H, giving the **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**.","steps":[{"text":"No water is lost, so the product keeps every atom of both molecules: an addition, not a condensation.","hl":"isolated before any water is eliminated","calc":["2 C₃H₆O → C₆H₁₂O₂"]},{"text":"Base removes an α-H from C2 of propanal; that carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second propanal.","calc":["CH₃CH₂–CH(O⁻)–CH(CH₃)–CHO → protonate → CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO"]},{"text":"Name it: five-carbon chain with CHO as C1, CH₃ on C2, OH on C3.","calc":["3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (C₆H₁₂O₂ ✓)"]},{"text":"2-Methylpent-2-enal is the dehydrated (condensation) product, which this question rules out.","strike":["2-Methylpent-2-enal"]},{"text":"The OH must be on the β-carbon and the methyl on the α-carbon, so the swapped name is wrong; a straight six-carbon chain would need the terminal CH₃ to be the nucleophile, but it is not α to the C=O.","strike":["2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."}],"trap":"Choosing the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde out of habit: that forms only after dehydration, which the stem excludes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cold, dilute NaOH","note":"mild: aldol addition"},{"t":"before any water is eliminated","note":"no dehydration"},{"t":"Propanal","note":"CH₃CH₂CHO"}]},{"text":"**Find the α-carbon.** In CH₃CH₂CHO the α-carbon is the CH₂ next to C=O; its enolate is the nucleophile."},{"text":"**Join and protonate.** α-Carbon to the other C=O carbon; the O⁻ picks up H⁺, giving an OH on the β-carbon.","calc":["α-C–C(OH) next to CHO → β-hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the mixture is heated, the β-OH and an α-H leave as water, giving the conjugated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.","calc":["β-hydroxy carbonyl − H₂O → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aldol addition gives a β-hydroxy carbonyl; only dehydration turns it into the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-04a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:4#1","pe3-cp:4#2","pe3-cp:4#3"],"q":"In a Michael addition, cyclohexanone's enolate adds to pent-3-en-2-one, CH₃–C(=O)–CH=CH–CH₃, numbered C1–C5 from the left. Which enone carbon forms the new bond?","choices":["C2","C3","C4","C5"],"ans":2,"exp":"Conjugation pulls electron density from the C=C toward oxygen, leaving **C4, the β-carbon**, electron-poor. The soft enolate bonds there (1,4-addition). C2 is the carbonyl carbon (1,2-attack), C3 is the α-carbon, and C5 is just a methyl group.","how":{"ask":"Label the α- and β-carbons of the enone and add the enolate to the β-carbon.","know":"• **Michael (1,4-) addition**: an enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.\n• **Robinson annulation**: the 1,5-diketone formed then undergoes an **intramolecular aldol + dehydration**, closing a **six-membered** enone ring.\n• Groups that do not react **ride along** on the carbon that carries them.","steps":[{"text":"Label the enone from the carbonyl: C2 is C=O, C3 is α, C4 is β.","hl":"CH₃–C(=O)–CH=CH–CH₃","calc":["C1H₃–C2(=O)–C3H=C4H–C5H₃","α = C3, β = C4"]},{"text":"Resonance puts δ+ on C4, so the enolate carbon bonds to C4.","calc":["O=C2–C3=C4 ↔ ⁻O–C2=C3–C4⁺"]},{"text":"C2 would be direct 1,2-attack, C3 is the α-carbon (it becomes the enolate carbon of the adduct), and C5 is an unactivated methyl.","strike":["C2","C3","C5"]},{"text":"Next the adduct, a 1,5-diketone, does an intramolecular aldol: the C1 methyl's enolate bonds to cyclohexanone's C=O carbon, closing a six-membered ring; dehydration gives the conjugated enone.","calc":["ring: C=O(cyclohexanone) – Cα(cyclohexanone) – C4 – C3 – C2 – C1 = **6 atoms**"]},{"text":"Where does C5 end up? It stays on C4, which in the new ring is bonded to the ring-fusion carbon and to the CH₂ next to the new ketone.","calc":["CH₃ on the ring carbon two bonds from the ketone carbon (via the CH₂), next to the ring-fusion carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: C4."}],"trap":"Attacking the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition): a soft, stabilized enolate adds to the β-carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michael addition","note":"1,4-addition"},{"t":"pent-3-en-2-one","note":"α,β-unsaturated ketone"},{"t":"C1–C5 from the left","note":"numbering to use"}]},{"text":"**Find α and β.** α is the carbon next to C=O; β is the far carbon of the C=C."},{"text":"**Add at β.** The enolate's carbon bonds to the β-carbon; the enone's C=C becomes a single bond and its enolate is protonated."},{"text":"**Also:** the resulting 1,5-dicarbonyl closes by intramolecular aldol and loses water, building a new six-membered ring with a conjugated C=O (Robinson annulation)."},{"text":"**Also:** a substituent that takes no part (here the C5 methyl) stays on its original carbon; follow that carbon into the ring to place it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Michael addition puts the enolate on the β-carbon; an aldol and dehydration then close the six-membered ring, carrying spectator groups along."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:5#3","pe3-cp:5#4"],"q":"A 40 mF capacitor is fully charged by a 15 V battery with 0.5 Ω internal resistance. What charge and energy does it store?","choices":["0.60 C; 4.5 J","0.60 C; 9.0 J","600 C; 4500 J","0.60 C; 0.30 J"],"ans":0,"exp":"Convert first: **40 mF = 0.040 F**. **Q = CV** = 0.040 F × 15 V = **0.60 C**. The stored energy is **U = ½CV²**, here 0.020 × 225 = **4.5 J** (or ½QV = ½ × 0.60 × 15 = 4.5 J). Once charged, no current flows, so the 0.5 Ω internal resistance drops no voltage and changes neither value.","how":{"ask":"Put the capacitance in farads, find the voltage across the capacitor, then apply Q = CV and U = ½CV².","know":"• **Q = CV** (coulombs = farads × volts).\n• **U = ½CV² = ½QV** (joules).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mF = 0.001 F.\n• A **fully charged** capacitor passes no current, so a resistor in series with it drops I·r = 0 V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milli-farads to farads.","hl":"40 mF","calc":["40 mF = 40 × 10⁻³ F = **0.040 F**"]},{"text":"Fully charged means the current has stopped. With I = 0, the internal resistance drops no voltage, so the capacitor holds the full battery voltage.","hl":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","calc":["V across r = I·r = 0 × 0.5 Ω = 0 V","V across C = **15 V**"]},{"text":"Charge from Q = CV. 600 C comes from leaving 40 mF as 40 F.","hl":"15 V battery","calc":["Q = CV","Q = 0.040 F × 15 V","Q = **0.60 C**"],"strike":["600 C; 4500 J"]},{"text":"Energy from U = ½CV², checked with ½QV. 9.0 J drops the ½, and 0.30 J forgets to square V (½CV).","calc":["U = ½CV²","U = ½ × 0.040 F × (15 V)²","U = ½ × 0.040 × 225 = **4.5 J**","check: U = ½QV = ½ × 0.60 C × 15 V = **4.5 J**"],"strike":["0.60 C; 9.0 J","0.60 C; 0.30 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.60 C; 4.5 J."}],"trap":"Reading mF as farads (600 C) or dropping the ½ in U = ½CV² (9.0 J). The internal resistance only slows the charging; it does not change the final charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"40 mF","note":"capacitance, in millifarads"},{"t":"fully charged","note":"no current now"},{"t":"15 V battery","note":"voltage across C"},{"t":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","note":"drops 0 V once I = 0"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before multiplying.","calc":["40 mF = 40 ÷ 1000 F = 0.040 F"]},{"text":"**Charge:** Q = CV.","calc":["Q = 0.040 F × 15 V = 0.60 C"]},{"text":"**Energy:** U = ½CV², or the same thing written ½QV.","calc":["½CV² = ½ × 0.040 × 15² = 4.5 J","½QV = ½ × 0.60 × 15 = 4.5 J"]},{"text":"**Also:** once a capacitor is fully charged, no current flows, so a series or internal resistance carries no voltage (V = IR = 0). It sets how fast the capacitor charges, never the final Q or U."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = CV and U = ½CV² = ½QV, with C in farads (1 mF = 10⁻³ F) and V the full source voltage once charging has stopped."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-06a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:6#1"],"q":"A jogger runs at 7 m/s past a parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren. Compared with 700 Hz, what does she hear approaching, then receding?","choices":["Higher, then lower","Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher","Equal, then equal"],"ans":0,"exp":"A listener moving **toward** a still source meets more crests each second, so the heard **frequency is higher**; moving **away**, she meets fewer, so it is **lower**. Taking sound at 350 m/s: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz approaching and 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz receding.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the listener is closing on or leaving the source in each half of the run, and how that changes the crests she meets per second.","know":"• Moving listener, still source: **f′ = f(v ± v_L)/v**, with v the speed of sound.\n• Use **+** when the listener moves **toward** the source (higher f′), **−** when moving **away** (lower f′).\n• Only motion along the line joining them shifts the frequency.","steps":[{"text":"The source is parked; only the listener moves. While approaching she runs into the crests, so she meets more per second. Take sound at 350 m/s.","hl":"runs at 7 m/s","calc":["f′ = f × (v + v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 + 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 1.02 = **714 Hz** > 700 Hz"]},{"text":"After passing she runs away from the crests, so fewer reach her each second. The pitch drops below 700 Hz, so neither the reversed order nor staying high fits.","calc":["f′ = f × (v − v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 − 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 0.98 = **686 Hz** < 700 Hz"],"strike":["Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher"]},{"text":"She hears exactly 700 Hz only when she has no velocity toward or away from the siren; while running along the road she hears a shift.","strike":["Equal, then equal"]},{"text":"Answer: higher, then lower."}],"trap":"Thinking only a moving source causes a Doppler shift. A moving listener shifts the heard frequency too: up while closing in, down while leaving."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find who moves**.","marks":[{"t":"runs at 7 m/s","note":"the listener moves"},{"t":"parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren","note":"the source is still"},{"t":"approaching, then receding","note":"two directions"}]},{"text":"**Count crests.** Running toward the siren, she meets the crests sooner than if she stood still, so more arrive each second: higher pitch."},{"text":"**Put numbers on it** (sound at 350 m/s).","calc":["toward: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz","away: 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moving toward a source raises the frequency you hear, and moving away lowers it, whether the source or the listener is the one moving."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:8#1","pe3-cp:8#2"],"q":"A trolley enters a magnetic brake zone at 12 m/s, and the braking force is proportional to its speed. Which describes its speed-versus-time graph?","choices":["Steep, then flattening","Straight downward line","Gentle, then steepening","Horizontal line"],"ans":0,"exp":"With **F ∝ v**, the deceleration a = F/m is largest at entry and shrinks as the trolley slows, so the speed drops steeply at first and then levels off: an **exponential** decay toward zero. A **constant** force would give a constant slope, a straight line.","how":{"ask":"Link the slope of a speed–time graph to the force, then ask how the force changes as the speed falls.","know":"• Slope of a v–t graph = acceleration = F/m.\n• **F ∝ v** (F = −bv): the slope is steep when v is large and gentle when v is small, giving exponential decay.\n• **Constant F**: constant slope, a straight line.","steps":[{"text":"The force, and so the deceleration, scales with speed.","hl":"the braking force is proportional to its speed","calc":["F = −bv","a = F ÷ m = −(b/m)v","large v → large deceleration; small v → small deceleration"]},{"text":"So the slope is steepest at entry and fades: the speed halves in equal times, dropping by less each interval. A graph that starts gentle and steepens would need the force to grow as the trolley slows.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Trolley speed in the brake zone","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":12,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"F ∝ v (magnetic brake)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,12],[2,6],[4,3],[6,1.5],[8,0.75]]},{"name":"Constant force","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,12],[2,9],[4,6],[6,3],[8,0]]}]},"calc":["12 → 6 → 3 → 1.5 m/s every 2 s","drops: 6, 3, 1.5 m/s: the slope shrinks"],"strike":["Gentle, then steepening"]},{"text":"A straight line needs a constant slope, which means a constant force; here the force fades with the speed.","strike":["Straight downward line"]},{"text":"A horizontal line means no change in speed, but the force is nonzero while the trolley moves.","strike":["Horizontal line"]},{"text":"Answer: steep, then flattening."}],"trap":"Picking the straight line out of habit: that is the graph for a constant force, like friction, not for a force that weakens as the object slows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"proportional to its speed","note":"F ∝ v"},{"t":"12 m/s","note":"starting speed"},{"t":"speed-versus-time graph","note":"slope = acceleration"}]},{"text":"**Slope is acceleration.** On a speed–time graph, a steeper drop means a bigger braking force.","calc":["a = F/m","F ∝ v → a ∝ v"]},{"text":"**Compare the two forces.** A speed-dependent brake gives a curve that flattens; a constant force gives a straight line.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two kinds of braking from 12 m/s","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":12,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"F ∝ v","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,12],[2,6],[4,3],[6,1.5],[8,0.75]]},{"name":"Constant F","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,12],[2,9],[4,6],[6,3],[8,0]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** a constant braking force gives a constant deceleration, so the speed falls by equal amounts in equal times: a straight line."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a force proportional to speed gives exponential slowing, steep at first and then flattening; a constant force gives a straight-line drop."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:9#1","pe3-cp:9#2"],"q":"Four C₄H₈ isomers, W–Z, each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Their heats of combustion are W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol. Which isomer is most stable?","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":3,"exp":"All four isomers end at the **same products**, so the difference in heat released is the difference in their starting enthalpies. Z releases the **least heat**, so it began lowest in energy and is the most stable; W, releasing the most, is the least stable.","how":{"ask":"Compare the heats of combustion; the smallest one marks the most stable isomer.","know":"• Isomers that burn to the **same products** can be ranked by heat of combustion: **less heat released = lower starting enthalpy = more stable**.\n• Boiling points, UV spectra and mass spectra do **not** measure thermodynamic stability.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula, same products, so all four reactions end at the same enthalpy.","hl":"each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O"},{"text":"Rank by heat released; the gap from the products is the starting enthalpy.","hl":"W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol","calc":["Z 2701 < Y 2707 < X 2710 < W 2717 kJ/mol","most stable: **Z**; Z is 2717 − 2701 = 16 kJ/mol below W"]},{"text":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest and is least stable; X and Y lie in between.","strike":["W","X","Y"]},{"text":"Answer: Z."}],"trap":"Thinking more heat released means a 'stronger' or more stable molecule: more heat out means it started higher in energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈ isomers","note":"same formula"},{"t":"4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O","note":"same products"},{"t":"W 2717","note":"heat released, kJ/mol"},{"t":"Z 2701 kJ/mol","note":"smallest heat"}]},{"text":"**Common endpoint.** Products are identical, so each heat of combustion measures how far its isomer sits above the same floor.","calc":["ΔH_comb = H(products) − H(isomer)"]},{"text":"**Smaller drop, lower start.** The isomer that releases the least heat is lowest in enthalpy: most stable.","calc":["2701 kJ/mol → lowest start → Z"]},{"text":"**Also:** boiling points reflect intermolecular forces, UV absorption reflects conjugation, and mass-spec patterns reflect mass and fragmentation; none of them measures stability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for isomers, the one that releases the least heat on combustion is the most stable."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-11a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:11#1","pe3-cp:11#2","pe3-cp:11#3"],"q":"A 0.82 L flask holds an ideal gas at 1.5 atm and 27 °C. Using R = 0.082 L·atm/(mol·K), about how many gas molecules does it contain?","choices":["3.0 × 10²²","3.3 × 10²³","0.050","1.2 × 10²⁵"],"ans":0,"exp":"**n = PV/RT** with T in kelvin (27 + 273 = 300 K): n = (1.5)(0.82) ÷ (0.082 × 300) = 0.050 mol. Molecules = **n × N_A** = 0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = 3.0 × 10²².","how":{"ask":"Find moles with the ideal gas law, then convert moles to molecules.","know":"• **PV = nRT**, so **n = PV/RT**.\n• **T must be in kelvin**: K = °C + 273.\n• Particles = **n × N_A** (N_A = 6.0 × 10²³ /mol).","steps":[{"text":"Convert the temperature to kelvin.","hl":"27 °C","calc":["T = 27 + 273 = **300 K**"]},{"text":"Solve for moles.","calc":["n = PV / RT","n = (1.5 atm)(0.82 L) ÷ (0.082 × 300)","n = 1.23 ÷ 24.6 = **0.050 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert moles to molecules.","calc":["N = 0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = **3.0 × 10²²**"]},{"text":"3.3 × 10²³ uses 27 instead of 300 K; 0.050 stops at moles; 1.2 × 10²⁵ inverts the gas law (RT/PV).","strike":["3.3 × 10²³","0.050","1.2 × 10²⁵"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.0 × 10²²."}],"trap":"Plugging in degrees Celsius: the gas law needs absolute temperature, so 27 °C is 300 K."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.82 L","note":"V"},{"t":"1.5 atm","note":"P"},{"t":"27 °C","note":"T, convert to K"},{"t":"molecules","note":"multiply by N_A"}]},{"text":"**Kelvin first.**","calc":["T = 27 + 273 = 300 K"]},{"text":"**Moles from PV = nRT.**","calc":["n = PV/RT = (1.5 × 0.82) ÷ (0.082 × 300) = 0.050 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** moles × Avogadro's number gives particles.","calc":["0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = 3.0 × 10²²"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number of gas particles = (PV/RT) × N_A, with T in kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-12a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:12#1","pe3-cp:12#2"],"q":"Sulfurous acid, H₂SO₃, has Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻² and Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸. What is the Kb of HSO₃⁻, and is a solution of NaHSO₃ acidic or basic?","choices":["7.1 × 10⁻¹³; acidic","7.1 × 10⁻¹³; basic","6.3 × 10⁻⁸; basic","1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶; acidic"],"ans":0,"exp":"HSO₃⁻ can lose H⁺ (its Ka is Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸) or gain H⁺ to become H₂SO₃. **Kb = Kw/Ka of the conjugate acid** = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³. **Ka > Kb**, so HSO₃⁻ acts mainly as an acid (sodium hydrogen sulfite solutions are acidic).","how":{"ask":"Find the ion's Kb from its conjugate acid, compare it with the ion's own Ka, and let the larger one decide.","know":"• An **amphoteric** ion can donate or accept H⁺.\n• Its **Ka** is the Ka for losing its own H⁺.\n• Its **Kb = Kw / Ka(conjugate acid)**, the acid it becomes when it gains H⁺.\n• **Larger constant wins**: Ka > Kb → acid; Kb > Ka → base.","steps":[{"text":"HSO₃⁻ losing H⁺ is the second ionization, so its Ka is Ka2.","hl":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","calc":["Ka(HSO₃⁻) = **6.3 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"Gaining H⁺ turns HSO₃⁻ into H₂SO₃, whose Ka is Ka1. Use Kb = Kw/Ka(conjugate acid).","hl":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","calc":["Kb = Kw / Ka1","Kb = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻²","Kb ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**"]},{"text":"Compare: 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is about 10⁵ times larger than 7.1 × 10⁻¹³, so the acid reaction dominates.","calc":["Ka ≫ Kb → acid"]},{"text":"The right Kb with the wrong behavior ignores the comparison; 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is the ion's Ka, not its Kb; 1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶ multiplies Kw by Ka1 instead of dividing.","strike":["7.1 × 10⁻¹³; basic","6.3 × 10⁻⁸; basic","1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶; acidic"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.1 × 10⁻¹³; acidic."}],"trap":"Using Kw/Ka2 for Kb: that is the Kb of SO₃²⁻. The Kb of HSO₃⁻ comes from the acid it forms, H₂SO₃ (Ka1)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","note":"H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻: the conjugate acid's Ka"},{"t":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"HSO₃⁻ → SO₃²⁻: the ion's own Ka"},{"t":"HSO₃⁻","note":"can gain or lose H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Get Kb from the conjugate acid.** The ion's Kb is Kw divided by the Ka of the species it becomes after taking H⁺.","calc":["Kb = Kw / Ka1 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³"]},{"text":"**Compare the two constants.** Whichever is larger names the main behavior.","calc":["Ka = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ > Kb = 7.1 × 10⁻¹³ → acid"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same test on HCO₃⁻ (Ka = 4.7 × 10⁻¹¹; Kb = Kw/4.3 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁸) gives Kb > Ka, so bicarbonate acts as a base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for an amphoteric ion, Kb = Kw/Ka(its conjugate acid); compare it with the ion's own Ka, and the larger constant wins."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-13a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:13#1","pe3-cp:13#2","pe3-cp:13#3"],"q":"For 2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃, a chemist mixes 5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol) and 14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol). Which reactant limits, and what remains?","choices":["Cl₂; 0.067 mol Al","Al; 0.067 mol Cl₂","Cl₂; 0.13 mol Al","Al; 0.10 mol Cl₂"],"ans":0,"exp":"Moles: Al = 5.4 ÷ 27 = 0.20 mol; Cl₂ = 14.2 ÷ 71 = 0.20 mol. Dividing by coefficients gives Al 0.10 and Cl₂ 0.067, so **Cl₂ limits**. It uses 0.20 × 2/3 ≈ 0.133 mol Al, leaving **0.067 mol Al** in excess.","how":{"ask":"Turn each mass into moles, divide by its coefficient, and the smallest result names the limiting reagent.","know":"• **Moles = mass ÷ molar mass.**\n• **Divide each reactant's moles by its coefficient**; the smallest quotient is the **limiting reagent**.\n• Whatever is **left over** when the reaction stops was **in excess**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the masses, molar masses and coefficients.","hl":"2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃"},{"text":"Convert each reactant to moles.","calc":["Al: 5.4 g ÷ 27 g/mol = **0.20 mol**","Cl₂: 14.2 g ÷ 71 g/mol = **0.20 mol**"]},{"text":"Divide by each coefficient. The smaller value limits.","calc":["Al: 0.20 ÷ 2 = 0.10","Cl₂: 0.20 ÷ 3 = **0.067** → Cl₂ limits"]},{"text":"Equal moles do not mean neither limits: Cl₂ needs 3 per 2 Al. Choices that make Al limiting fail.","strike":["Al; 0.067 mol Cl₂","Al; 0.10 mol Cl₂"]},{"text":"Find the Al used, then subtract. 0.13 mol is the Al consumed, not what remains.","calc":["Al used = 0.20 mol Cl₂ × 2/3 = 0.133 mol","Al left = 0.20 − 0.133 = **0.067 mol**"],"strike":["Cl₂; 0.13 mol Al"]},{"text":"**Also:** Al left over at the end confirms it was the reactant in excess."},{"text":"Answer: Cl₂; 0.067 mol Al."}],"trap":"Comparing raw moles or masses: the moles must first be divided by the coefficients in the balanced equation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol)","note":"mass and molar mass of Al"},{"t":"14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol)","note":"mass and molar mass of Cl₂"},{"t":"2 Al + 3 Cl₂","note":"the coefficients"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.** Reactions count particles, not grams.","calc":["Al: 5.4 ÷ 27 = 0.20 mol","Cl₂: 14.2 ÷ 71 = 0.20 mol"]},{"text":"**Divide by the coefficient.** The smallest quotient runs out first.","calc":["Al: 0.20/2 = 0.10","Cl₂: 0.20/3 = 0.067 → limiting"]},{"text":"**Leftover = start − used.**","calc":["Al used = 0.20 × 2/3 = 0.133 mol","Al left = 0.067 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** a reactant recovered unreacted at the end was in excess; the other one was limiting."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles ÷ coefficient for each reactant; the smallest is limiting, and the leftover reactant was in excess."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-15a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:15#1","pe3-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:15#3"],"q":"Cadmium, Z = 48, lies directly below zinc and forms colorless Cd²⁺ salts. Which configuration describes the ground state of the Cd²⁺ ion?","choices":["[Kr]4d¹⁰","[Kr]4d⁸5s²","[Kr]4d⁹5s¹","[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"],"ans":0,"exp":"Neutral Cd is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s² (36 + 10 + 2 = 48). Transition metals lose their **outer s electrons before d electrons**, so Cd²⁺ (46 electrons) is **[Kr]4d¹⁰**, a filled d subshell like Zn²⁺'s [Ar]3d¹⁰.","how":{"ask":"Write the neutral atom, then remove electrons from the highest-n subshell (5s) first.","know":"• Zn is **[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²**: a filled 3d and a filled 4s (18 + 10 + 2 = 30). Cd, below it, is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².\n• Cations of transition metals **lose ns electrons before (n−1)d**: Zn²⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰.\n• **Half-filled** means d⁵ or s¹; filled means d¹⁰ or s².","steps":[{"text":"Start from the neutral atom.","hl":"Z = 48","calc":["Kr core: 36 electrons","36 + 10 (4d) + 2 (5s) = 48 → [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"Remove two electrons from 5s, the highest-n subshell.","calc":["Cd²⁺: 46 electrons → **[Kr]4d¹⁰**"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d¹⁰5s² is the neutral atom; no electrons were removed.","strike":["[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d⁸5s² takes the electrons from 4d first, the wrong order.","strike":["[Kr]4d⁸5s²"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d⁹5s¹ takes one from each subshell; a half-filled s¹ is not what forms.","strike":["[Kr]4d⁹5s¹"]},{"text":"**Also, zinc:** Zn = [Ar]3d¹⁰4s² (filled 3d, filled 4s), and Zn²⁺ loses both 4s electrons first → [Ar]3d¹⁰."},{"text":"Answer: [Kr]4d¹⁰."}],"trap":"Removing d electrons first because d is filled last: on ionization, the ns electrons leave first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key facts**.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 48","note":"electron count of the neutral atom"},{"t":"directly below zinc","note":"same pattern as Zn, one row down"},{"t":"Cd²⁺","note":"two electrons removed"}]},{"text":"**Fill the neutral atom.** Zn: [Ar]3d¹⁰4s², filled 3d and filled 4s. Cd, one row down: [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","calc":["Zn: 18 + 10 + 2 = 30","Cd: 36 + 10 + 2 = 48"]},{"text":"**Remove ns first.** Zn²⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰; Cd²⁺ = [Kr]4d¹⁰. A filled d¹⁰ means all electrons are paired (diamagnetic, colorless salts)."},{"text":"**Also:** \"half-filled\" means d⁵ (e.g. Mn²⁺, [Ar]3d⁵) or s¹ (e.g. Cu, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹). Zn and its ion have only filled subshells, d¹⁰ and s²."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fill the neutral atom, then ionize from the highest-n s subshell first; d¹⁰ and s² are filled, d⁵ and s¹ are half-filled."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1","pe3-cp:32#1","pe3-cp:32#2","pe3-cp:32#3"],"q":"In aqueous AgNO₃, silver crystals grow on a copper wire as the solution turns blue. This reaction is best classified as:","choices":["redox","precipitation","acid–base","hydrolysis"],"ans":0,"exp":"Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2 Ag(s). Copper's oxidation state rises from 0 to +2 (oxidized) and silver's falls from +1 to 0 (reduced). **Any change in oxidation state makes a reaction redox**, even when a solid appears.","how":{"ask":"Assign oxidation states before and after, and check whether any of them change.","know":"• **Redox** = oxidation states change. **Oxidation** = loss of electrons (state rises); **reduction** = gain of electrons (state falls).\n• The species that **gains electrons is reduced**.\n• Elements (Cu, O₂) are 0; O is −1 in peroxides (H₂O₂) and −2 in H₂O.","steps":[{"text":"Write the change: silver metal forms and blue Cu²⁺ appears.","hl":"silver crystals grow on a copper wire","calc":["Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2 Ag(s)"]},{"text":"Assign oxidation states.","calc":["Cu: 0 → +2 (loses 2 e⁻, oxidized)","Ag: +1 → 0 (gains 1 e⁻, reduced)"]},{"text":"The solid is metal made from ions by electron transfer, not an ionic salt from two ions, so not precipitation. No proton moves (not acid–base), and water does not split a bond (not hydrolysis).","strike":["precipitation","acid–base","hydrolysis"]},{"text":"**Also, oxygen:** O₂ → H₂O₂ lowers O from 0 to −1; O₂ → H₂O lowers it to −2. O gains electrons, so O₂ is reduced.","calc":["O₂ (0) → H₂O₂ (−1) → H₂O (−2)"]},{"text":"**Also, cofactors:** a cofactor that is reduced and then reoxidized (e.g. FAD → FADH₂ → FAD) ends where it started, so it is not net changed; ask which species is reduced overall."},{"text":"Answer: redox."}],"trap":"Calling it precipitation because a solid forms: check oxidation states first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key observations**.","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"Cu metal, oxidation state 0"},{"t":"AgNO₃","note":"Ag⁺, oxidation state +1"},{"t":"turns blue","note":"Cu²⁺ forming"}]},{"text":"**Track oxidation states.** Cu 0 → +2 and Ag +1 → 0: states change, so the reaction is redox."},{"text":"**Gains electrons = reduced.** Ag⁺ gains an electron and is reduced; Cu loses two and is oxidized."},{"text":"**Also:** O₂ to H₂O₂ or H₂O lowers oxygen from 0 to −1 or −2, so O₂ is reduced (it is the oxidizing agent)."},{"text":"**Also:** in a multistep pathway, a cofactor that is reduced and then regenerated is not net changed; the overall reduced species is the one whose oxidation state ends lower."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** any change in oxidation state means redox; the species whose state falls gains electrons and is reduced."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-17a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:17#1","pe3-cp:17#2"],"q":"A feverish patient's temperature reads 122 °F. Expressed on the absolute scale used in gas-law calculations, this temperature is about:","choices":["323 K","50 K","395 K","341 K"],"ans":0,"exp":"**°F → °C: subtract 32, then × 5/9**: (122 − 32) × 5/9 = 90 × 5/9 = 50 °C. **K = °C + 273** = 323 K.","how":{"ask":"Convert °F to °C, then °C to kelvin.","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**.\n• **K = °C + 273**.\n• Never add 273 to a Fahrenheit value.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the Fahrenheit reading.","hl":"122 °F","calc":["°C = (122 − 32) × 5/9","= 90 × 5/9","= **50 °C**"]},{"text":"Add 273 to reach kelvin.","calc":["K = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"50 K stops at Celsius; 395 K adds 273 to the Fahrenheit value; 341 K skips subtracting 32 (122 × 5/9 + 273).","strike":["50 K","395 K","341 K"]},{"text":"Answer: 323 K."}],"trap":"Skipping the −32 (122 × 5/9 + 273 ≈ 341 K): subtract 32 first, then scale by 5/9."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"122 °F","note":"Fahrenheit: convert to °C first"},{"t":"absolute scale","note":"kelvin"}]},{"text":"**°F → °C.** Subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9.","calc":["(122 − 32) × 5/9 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** °C → K: add 273.","calc":["50 + 273 = 323 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and K = °C + 273; gas laws always use kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-18a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:18#2","pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"q":"A stimulator outputs 0.30 ms pulses separated by 3.7 ms of zero output, repeating steadily. What is the pulse frequency?","choices":["250 Hz","270 Hz","3333 Hz","294 Hz"],"ans":0,"exp":"**One period is one full repeat: on time plus off time** = 0.30 ms + 3.7 ms = 4.0 ms. Inverting that period in seconds (**f = 1/T**) gives 1 ÷ 0.0040 s = **250 Hz**.","how":{"ask":"Find the time for one complete on-plus-off cycle, then invert it.","know":"• **Period T** = time for one full repeat of the pattern = on time + off time.\n• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds for f in hertz.\n• 1 ms = 10⁻³ s.","steps":[{"text":"One repeat is a pulse plus the gap after it.","hl":"0.30 ms pulses","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Stimulator output","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Output (on = 1)","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Output","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[0.3,1],[0.3,0],[4,0],[4,1],[4.3,1],[4.3,0],[8,0]]}]},"calc":["T = t_on + t_off","T = 0.30 ms + 3.7 ms = **4.0 ms**"]},{"text":"Invert the period, in seconds.","calc":["4.0 ms = 4.0 × 10⁻³ s","f = 1/T = 1 ÷ 0.0040 s","f = **250 Hz**"]},{"text":"3333 Hz inverts the pulse alone (1 ÷ 0.30 ms) and 270 Hz the gap alone (1 ÷ 3.7 ms). 294 Hz subtracts the times (3.7 − 0.30 = 3.4 ms) instead of adding them.","strike":["3333 Hz","270 Hz","294 Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 250 Hz."}],"trap":"Using only the pulse width as the period: the pattern does not repeat until the off time has passed too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"0.30 ms pulses","note":"on time"},{"t":"3.7 ms of zero output","note":"off time"},{"t":"repeating steadily","note":"periodic"}]},{"text":"**One period is one full repeat.** Start at the rise of one pulse and stop at the rise of the next.","calc":["T = 0.30 + 3.7 = 4.0 ms"]},{"text":"**Also:** frequency is the number of repeats per second, f = 1/T.","calc":["f = 1 ÷ 0.0040 s = 250 Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** T runs from one pulse's start to the next pulse's start, however short each pulse is, and ms means 10⁻³ s, so convert before taking 1/T.","calc":["starts 40 ms apart: T = 40 × 10⁻³ s = 0.040 s","f = 1 ÷ 0.040 s = 25 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a pulsed signal, T = on time + off time, and f = 1/T with T in seconds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-19a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:19#1","pe3-cp:19#2","pe3-cp:19#3","pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3"],"q":"Taking hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m, what is the greatest photon energy from a lamp emitting wavelengths between 330 nm and 550 nm?","choices":["6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J","6.5 × 10⁻³² J"],"ans":0,"exp":"**E = hc/λ** puts λ in the denominator, so the most energetic photon has the **shortest** wavelength, 330 nm = 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m. E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m = **6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**. The 550 nm photons carry only 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","how":{"ask":"Pick the wavelength that gives the most energy, convert it to metres, and divide hc by it.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: photon energy is inversely proportional to wavelength.\n• **n (nano) = 10⁻⁹**: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• With hc in J·m, λ must be in metres to get joules.","steps":[{"text":"E = hc/λ, so the smallest λ gives the largest E. Use 330 nm, not 550 nm.","hl":"between 330 nm and 550 nm","calc":["E ∝ 1/λ","330 nm < 550 nm → 330 nm gives the most energy"]},{"text":"Convert nanometres to metres.","calc":["330 nm = 330 × 10⁻⁹ m = **3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"Divide hc by the wavelength.","hl":"hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","calc":["E = hc ÷ λ","E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m","E = **6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the 550 nm photon, the least energetic (1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 5.50 × 10⁻⁷). 6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J divides by 330 without converting nm to m. 6.5 × 10⁻³² J multiplies hc by λ instead of dividing.","strike":["3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J","6.5 × 10⁻³² J"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."}],"trap":"Using the longest wavelength because it is the bigger number: energy goes as 1/λ, so the shortest wavelength wins."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","note":"constant, in J·m"},{"t":"greatest photon energy","note":"want the maximum E"},{"t":"330 nm","note":"shortest λ"},{"t":"550 nm","note":"longest λ"}]},{"text":"**Photon energy: E = hc/λ.** Halve the wavelength and the energy doubles, so energy climbs toward the short-wavelength end.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy across the lamp's range","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":300,"max":600,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hc/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[330,6],[400,4.95],[450,4.4],[500,3.96],[550,3.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** nm means 10⁻⁹ m. Convert before dividing.","calc":["330 nm = 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ = 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"]},{"text":"**Also:** to get electronvolts, divide the energy in joules by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV; the shortcut E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm) skips both conversions.","calc":["450 nm: E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 4.5 × 10⁻⁷ = 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ 2.8 eV","shortcut: 1240 ÷ 450 ≈ 2.8 eV ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hc/λ with λ in metres (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m); the shortest wavelength in a range gives the most energetic photon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-20a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:20#1","pe3-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:14#1"],"q":"Two large parallel plates are held at 12 V, the left one positive. Which describes the field lines between them, away from the edges?","choices":["Evenly spaced; left to right","Evenly spaced; right to left","Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"],"ans":0,"exp":"Between large parallel plates the field has the **same strength and direction at every point**, so its lines are straight and **evenly spaced**. Field lines start on positive charge and end on negative charge, so they run **from the + plate to the − plate**: left to right.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a uniform field looks like, then which way its lines point.","know":"• **Line spacing shows strength**: closer lines mean a stronger field.\n• Between large parallel plates the field is **uniform**: E = V/d at every point, so lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced.\n• Field lines point **from + to −**, the way a positive test charge would be pushed.","steps":[{"text":"Large plates, away from the edges: the field is uniform. If the gap were 4.0 mm, E would be the same everywhere, so there is no crowding near a plate and no curving (lines bulge only at the edges).","hl":"Two large parallel plates","calc":["E = V ÷ d","E = 12 V ÷ 0.0040 m = **3000 V/m** at every point"],"strike":["Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"]},{"text":"A positive test charge is pushed away from the positive plate and toward the negative one, so the lines run from left to right.","hl":"the left one positive","strike":["Evenly spaced; right to left"]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced; left to right."}],"trap":"Drawing the lines from − to +: field lines leave positive charge and end on negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"large parallel plates","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"the left one positive","note":"sets the direction"},{"t":"away from the edges","note":"no fringing"}]},{"text":"**Uniform means the same everywhere.** Equal strength means equal line spacing; equal direction means straight, parallel lines.","calc":["E = V/d, the same at every point between the plates"]},{"text":"**Also:** field lines point from + to −: they start on the positive plate and end on the negative plate."},{"text":"**Also:** the field's size is E = V/d, with d in metres and 1 kV = 10³ V.","calc":["12 kV across 5.0 mm","E = 12 × 10³ V ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ m = 2.4 × 10⁶ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** between parallel plates the field lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced, pointing from the positive plate to the negative plate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:22#1","pe3-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"q":"Cholesterol oxidase converts cholesterol to a ketone, handing the two removed hydrogens to O₂. Which enzyme belongs to the same class?","choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase","Hexokinase","Triose phosphate isomerase","Pyruvate carboxylase"],"ans":0,"exp":"Removing hydrogens (electrons) from a substrate is an **oxidation**, so any oxidase is an **oxidoreductase**, like glucose oxidase. Lactate dehydrogenase also moves H between lactate and NAD⁺: an oxidoreductase. Hexokinase is a transferase (moves a phosphoryl), triose phosphate isomerase an isomerase, and pyruvate carboxylase a ligase (uses ATP to join CO₂).","how":{"ask":"Classify the oxidase's reaction, then classify each listed enzyme by what it does.","know":"The six classes:\n• **Oxidoreductase**: moves electrons/H (oxidases, dehydrogenases, reductases).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (phosphoryl, methyl, amino) between molecules (kinases, methyltransferases).\n• **Hydrolase**: breaks a bond by adding water (proteases, lipases).\n• **Lyase**: breaks or makes a bond without water or redox, often leaving a C=C (decarboxylases, aldolase).\n• **Isomerase**: rearranges one molecule into an isomer.\n• **Ligase**: joins two molecules using ATP (synthetases, carboxylases).","steps":[{"text":"Removing two H from a substrate and passing them to O₂ is an oxidation: an oxidoreductase.","hl":"handing the two removed hydrogens to O₂","calc":["cholesterol − 2 H → ketone (oxidized)","O₂ + 2 H → H₂O₂ (reduced)"]},{"text":"Classify the other enzymes by their reactions.","calc":["lactate dehydrogenase: lactate + NAD⁺ ⇌ pyruvate + NADH → **oxidoreductase**","hexokinase: ATP's phosphoryl → glucose → transferase","triose phosphate isomerase: DHAP ⇌ G3P → isomerase","pyruvate carboxylase: pyruvate + CO₂ + ATP → ligase"]},{"text":"Hexokinase only moves a phosphoryl group; no electrons change hands.","strike":["Hexokinase"]},{"text":"Triose phosphate isomerase rearranges one molecule; pyruvate carboxylase joins two using ATP.","strike":["Triose phosphate isomerase","Pyruvate carboxylase"]},{"text":"Answer: lactate dehydrogenase."}],"trap":"Thinking only enzymes named “oxidase” are oxidoreductases: dehydrogenases and reductases are in the same class."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"oxidase","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"two removed hydrogens","note":"electrons leave the substrate"},{"t":"same class","note":"match the reaction type"}]},{"text":"**Ask what moves.** Electrons or H → oxidoreductase; a group between molecules → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase; a bond breaks without water or redox → lyase; one molecule rearranges → isomerase; ATP joins two molecules → ligase."},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase oxidizes glucose (removes 2 H, which go to O₂), so it is an oxidoreductase.","calc":["glucose − 2 H → gluconolactone","O₂ + 2 H → H₂O₂"]},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme that moves a methyl group from a donor (such as SAM) onto a molecule is a transferase (a methyltransferase).","calc":["donor–CH₃ + R–H → donor–H + R–CH₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** turning a –CH₃ into –CH₂OH with O₂ adds a C–O bond, an oxidation, so that enzyme is an oxidoreductase.","calc":["R–CH₃ + [O] → R–CH₂OH (C gains a bond to O)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the class by what the enzyme moves: electrons → oxidoreductase, a group → transferase, water → hydrolase, no water → lyase, within one molecule → isomerase, ATP-driven joining → ligase."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-23a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:23#1","pe3-cp:23#2","pe3-cp:23#3"],"q":"A fuel-cell anode oxidizes hydrazine: N₂H₄ → N₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. If 193 C of charge flows, what mass of hydrazine (32 g/mol) was consumed? (F = 96,500 C/mol)","choices":["16 mg","32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"],"ans":0,"exp":"**mol e⁻ = Q/F** = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Each N₂H₄ gives 4 e⁻, so **mol N₂H₄ = mol e⁻ ÷ 4** = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol. **× molar mass**: 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 g/mol = 0.016 g = 16 mg.","how":{"ask":"Charge → moles of electrons → moles of fuel → grams.","know":"• **mol e⁻ = Q / F** (F = 96,500 C/mol).\n• **mol substance = mol e⁻ ÷ electrons per molecule** (from the half-reaction).\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert charge to moles of electrons.","hl":"193 C","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q / F","= 193 ÷ 96,500","= **2.00 × 10⁻³ mol**"]},{"text":"The half-reaction releases 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄.","hl":"4e⁻","calc":["mol N₂H₄ = 2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = **5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to mass.","calc":["m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 32 g/mol","= 0.016 g = **16 mg**"]},{"text":"32 mg divides by 2 instead of 4; 64 mg skips the ÷ 4; 256 mg multiplies by 4.","strike":["32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 16 mg."}],"trap":"Treating moles of electrons as moles of substance: divide by the electrons each molecule transfers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"193 C","note":"charge Q"},{"t":"96,500 C/mol","note":"Faraday's constant"},{"t":"4e⁻","note":"electrons per N₂H₄"},{"t":"32 g/mol","note":"molar mass"}]},{"text":"**Charge to electrons.**","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q/F = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"**Electrons to molecules.** Divide by the electrons each molecule gives up.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol N₂H₄"]},{"text":"**Also:** moles × molar mass gives the mass.","calc":["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 = 0.016 g = 16 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** a sensor that oxidizes glucose collects 2 e⁻ per glucose, so there mol glucose = mol e⁻ ÷ 2.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻ ÷ 2 = 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol glucose","1.00 × 10⁻³ mol × 180 g/mol = 0.180 g"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol e⁻ = Q/F; divide by electrons per molecule; multiply by molar mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-24a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:24#1","pe3-cp:24#2"],"q":"Succinate dehydrogenase strips two H atoms from succinate, making fumarate. In what form is its covalently bound flavin cofactor at the end of this step?","choices":["FADH₂","FAD","NADH","NADP⁺"],"ans":0,"exp":"The flavin starts as oxidized **FAD**, accepts the **2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)** taken from succinate, and leaves as reduced **FADH₂**. NADH and NADP⁺ are nicotinamide carriers, not flavins, and FAD is the form before the step.","how":{"ask":"Identify the flavin cofactor, then decide whether accepting two H leaves it oxidized or reduced.","know":"• **Flavin cofactor**: FAD (or FMN), often **tightly bound** to its enzyme.\n• **FAD + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → FADH₂**: accepting 2 H reduces it.\n• An acceptor must start oxidized (FAD, NAD⁺); it ends reduced (FADH₂, NADH).","steps":[{"text":"Two H atoms leave the substrate and go to the bound flavin.","hl":"strips two H atoms from succinate","calc":["succinate → fumarate + 2 H"]},{"text":"The flavin is FAD; adding 2 H gives FADH₂.","calc":["FAD + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → **FADH₂**"]},{"text":"FAD is the form that enters, not the one that leaves.","strike":["FAD"]},{"text":"NADH and NADP⁺ are nicotinamide cofactors, not flavins.","strike":["NADH","NADP⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: FADH₂."}],"trap":"Choosing FAD because it is the flavin's usual name: after accepting the two H it is FADH₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"strips two H atoms","note":"oxidation of succinate"},{"t":"covalently bound flavin cofactor","note":"FAD"},{"t":"at the end of this step","note":"after accepting H"}]},{"text":"**Two H in, reduced out.** The bound flavin accepts both hydrogens.","calc":["FAD (oxidized) + 2 H → FADH₂ (reduced)"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase works the same way with tightly bound FAD: glucose gives 2 H to FAD, and the FADH₂ then hands them to O₂, regenerating FAD.","calc":["glucose + E–FAD → gluconolactone + E–FADH₂","E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a bound flavin enzyme uses FAD, which accepts 2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻) and becomes FADH₂."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-25a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:25#1","pe3-cp:25#2"],"q":"A hormone enters its target cell and binds an intracellular steroid receptor. Which molecule has the ring skeleton of a steroid?","choices":["Estradiol","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Prostaglandin E₂"],"ans":0,"exp":"A steroid is defined by its **6-6-6-5 fused ring core** (rings A, B and C of six atoms, ring D of five). Estradiol has it. Epinephrine has one benzene ring, thyroxine has two separate rings joined by an ether O, and prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two chains.","how":{"ask":"Look for four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five, in each molecule.","know":"• **Steroid skeleton**: four **fused** rings, **6-6-6-5** (rings A, B, C, D), 17 ring carbons, derived from cholesterol.\n• Fused = neighboring rings share a bond.\n• Rings joined by a chain or an O are not fused, and a single ring is never a steroid.","steps":[{"text":"A steroid receptor binds molecules built on the steroid core.","hl":"steroid receptor"},{"text":"Draw the core to search for: three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring, all fused.","fig":["t3-steroid-abcd","cholesterol"],"calc":["rings: 6 + 6 + 6 + 5","fused: each pair shares one bond"]},{"text":"Estradiol has rings A–D (ring A aromatic): the 6-6-6-5 core."},{"text":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine with one benzene ring; thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not fused.","strike":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]},{"text":"Prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two side chains, a fatty-acid derivative.","strike":["Prostaglandin E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: estradiol."}],"trap":"Choosing any lipid-soluble or ring-containing hormone: only the fused 6-6-6-5 skeleton makes a steroid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"steroid receptor","note":"needs a steroid"},{"t":"ring skeleton","note":"count and fuse the rings"}]},{"text":"**Know the core.** Four fused rings: three six-membered (A, B, C) and one five-membered (D).","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd","calc":["6 + 6 + 6 + 5 = four rings, 17 ring C"]},{"text":"**Spot it in a drawing.** Find the five-membered ring, then check that it is fused to three six-membered rings in a row; extra groups (OH, C=O, a side chain) do not matter.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule out look-alikes.** One ring, rings joined by a linker, or no rings at all: not a steroid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steroid has four fused rings, three six-membered and one five-membered; look for that 6-6-6-5 skeleton and ignore the groups on it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-26a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:26#2","pe3-cp:26#3","pe4-cp:40#1"],"q":"A carbonate buffer (HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3) reads pH 10.8. Which compound, when added, moves it toward its greatest buffer capacity?","choices":["NaHCO₃","Na₂CO₃","NaOH","NaCl"],"ans":0,"exp":"**pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**. At pH 10.8 the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2. Capacity is greatest at **pH = pKa**, where the ratio is 1, so the pH must fall: add more of the **acid form**, NaHCO₃.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the pH must rise or fall to reach pKa, then add the form that moves it that way.","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]).\n• A buffer works best at **pH = pKa** ([base] = [acid]).\n• To **lower** pH, raise the share of the **acid** form; to raise pH, add the base form.","steps":[{"text":"Read the buffer pair and its pKa.","hl":"HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3","calc":["acid = HCO₃⁻, base = CO₃²⁻"]},{"text":"Find the current base:acid ratio.","hl":"pH 10.8","calc":["10.8 = 10.3 + log(ratio)","log(ratio) = 0.5","ratio = 10^0.5 ≈ **3.2**"]},{"text":"Best capacity is at pH = pKa = 10.3, ratio 1. The ratio must fall, so the pH must drop by 0.5: add the acid form.","calc":["pH 10.8 → 10.3: add HCO₃⁻ (as NaHCO₃)"]},{"text":"Na₂CO₃ and NaOH raise the base share and push the pH higher; NaCl adds neither buffer form.","strike":["Na₂CO₃","NaOH","NaCl"]},{"text":"Answer: NaHCO₃."}],"trap":"Adding the base form because the pH must \"reach\" pKa: here the buffer is above its pKa, so it needs more acid form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 10.3","note":"best buffering pH"},{"t":"pH 10.8","note":"current pH, above pKa"},{"t":"greatest buffer capacity","note":"pH = pKa"}]},{"text":"**Write Henderson–Hasselbalch.** It links pH to the base:acid ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])","10.8 − 10.3 = 0.5 → ratio ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Aim for pH = pKa.** There the ratio is 1, and the buffer resists added acid and base equally well."},{"text":"**Lower pH = more acid form.** The pH is too high, so raise [HCO₃⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** for a phosphate buffer, the acid form is H₂PO₄⁻ (the monosodium salt, NaH₂PO₄); adding it lowers the pH, while Na₂HPO₄ raises it."},{"text":"**Also:** a buffer works only within pKa ± 1, where the base:acid ratio stays between one-tenth and ten; check each pH against that window.","calc":["pKa 10.3 → useful from pH 9.3 to 11.3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]); to lower the pH add the acid form, to raise it add the base form, and capacity peaks at pH = pKa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-27a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2"],"q":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da) are coupled into a dipeptide by a single peptide bond. What is the dipeptide's mass?","choices":["170 Da","188 Da","206 Da","224 Da"],"ans":1,"exp":"Forming a peptide bond is a **condensation**: one H₂O (18 Da) leaves. Sum the amino acids, 75 + 131 = 206 Da, then subtract one water: 206 − 18 = **188 Da**.","how":{"ask":"Add the two amino-acid masses, then subtract one water for the one peptide bond.","know":"• Peptide bond formation releases **one H₂O, 18 amu**, per bond (condensation).\n• **Peptide mass = sum of amino-acid masses − 18 × (number of peptide bonds)**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the masses and the number of bonds formed.","hl":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da)"},{"text":"Add the free amino acids.","calc":["75 + 131 = 206 Da"]},{"text":"One peptide bond releases one water.","calc":["206 − 18 = **188 Da**"]},{"text":"206 Da forgets the lost water, 170 Da removes two waters, and 224 Da adds a water instead of removing it.","strike":["206 Da","170 Da","224 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 188 Da."}],"trap":"Reporting the simple sum (206 Da): the condensation removes H₂O, so the dipeptide is 18 Da lighter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"75 Da","note":"glycine"},{"t":"131 Da","note":"leucine"},{"t":"a single peptide bond","note":"one water lost"}]},{"text":"**Name the byproduct.** The carboxyl OH of one amino acid and an H from the other's amino group leave together as H₂O.","calc":["H₂O = 2(1) + 16 = 18 amu"]},{"text":"**Sum, then subtract.**","calc":["75 + 131 − 18 = 188 Da"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each peptide bond releases one H₂O (18 amu), so a dipeptide's mass is the sum of its amino acids minus 18."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-28a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:28#1","pe3-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:28#3","pe3-cp:28#4"],"q":"A student is planning Michaelis–Menten measurements on a purified enzyme. Which condition is NOT required for valid data?","choices":["Initial rates measured","Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate","Reaction run to equilibrium"],"ans":3,"exp":"Michaelis–Menten analysis needs **initial rates (v₀)** under **steady state**, a **constant pH**, and **[E] ≪ [S]** so [S] stays at its set value. Letting the reaction reach **equilibrium** is not wanted: by then [S] has fallen, product has built up and the net rate is zero.","how":{"ask":"Check each condition against the assumptions behind v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).","know":"• **v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** assumes **initial rates** (little product, [S] ≈ starting value) and a **steady state** of ES.\n• **Constant pH** (a buffer) keeps the enzyme's charges and activity fixed.\n• **[E] ≪ [S]** so binding barely depletes free substrate.\n• At **equilibrium** the net rate is zero, so it gives no v₀.","steps":[{"text":"This is a NOT question: find the condition the analysis does not want.","hl":"NOT required"},{"text":"Initial rates are the data Michaelis–Menten uses: the slope at the start of the product-versus-time curve.","strike":["Initial rates measured"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed over time in one enzyme run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Product (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,22],[5,46],[10,68],[15,80],[20,86],[25,88],[30,89]]}]}},{"text":"A buffered pH holds the enzyme's ionizable groups steady, and [E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value.","strike":["Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the curve is flat: net rate zero, [S] no longer the starting value. That is not wanted."},{"text":"Answer: reaction run to equilibrium."}],"trap":"Thinking a reaction must be “complete” to be measured: Michaelis–Menten uses only the first, straight part of the curve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten","note":"uses v₀"},{"t":"NOT required","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Initial rates, steady state.** Measure v₀ while [ES] is steady and little product has formed; later the curve bends over.","calc":["v₀ = slope at t ≈ 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep the pH constant with a buffer; a changing pH changes the enzyme's activity during the run."},{"text":"**Also:** keep [E] much less than [S], so the enzyme does not tie up a significant share of the substrate.","calc":["[E] ≪ [S] → free [S] ≈ total [S]"]},{"text":"**Also:** letting the reaction reach equilibrium is not wanted: the net rate there is zero and [S] has changed, so rates must be initial."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** valid Michaelis–Menten data use initial rates at steady state, constant pH and [E] ≪ [S]; never wait for equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-30a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:30#1"],"q":"Many metal-chelating drugs, such as vorinostat, contain a hydroxamic acid group. Which compound is a hydroxamic acid (Ph = phenyl)?","choices":["PhC(=O)NH–OH","PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"],"ans":0,"exp":"A hydroxamic acid is an **amide whose nitrogen carries an OH**: R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′, here with R = phenyl and R′ = H. Moving the acyl group to oxygen gives an O-acyl hydroxylamine, C=N–OH is an oxime, and an amide without the N–OH is a plain amide.","how":{"ask":"Look for a C=O bonded directly to an N that also bears an OH.","know":"• **Hydroxamic acid**: **R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′**, an amide whose N carries an OH (R′ may be H).\n• Contrast: oxime R–CH=N–OH; O-acyl hydroxylamine R–C(=O)–O–NH₂; amide R–C(=O)–NH₂.","steps":[{"text":"Write the target pattern.","calc":["R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"PhC(=O)NH–OH has C=O on N, and N bears OH: R = Ph, R′ = H.","hl":"PhC(=O)NH–OH"},{"text":"PhC(=O)O–NH₂ bonds the acyl group to O, not N; PhCH=N–OH has no C=O (an oxime); PhC(=O)NH₂ lacks the N–OH.","strike":["PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"]},{"text":"Answer: PhC(=O)NH–OH."}],"trap":"Picking the O-acyl isomer: both contain C=O, N and O, but in a hydroxamic acid the carbonyl carbon is bonded to nitrogen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydroxamic acid","note":"amide + N–OH"},{"t":"metal-chelating","note":"the C=O oxygen and the N–O oxygen bind the metal together"}]},{"text":"**Start from an amide.** R–C(=O)–NH–R′ is an amide."},{"text":"**Put OH on the nitrogen.** Replace the N–H with N–OH.","calc":["R–C(=O)–NH–R′ → R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydroxamic acid is R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′: an amide whose N carries an OH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-31a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:31#1"],"q":"At pH 7.0, one enzyme oxidizes substrate X with a rate constant of 5.6 s⁻¹ and substrate Y with one of 1.6 s⁻¹. If both substrates are equally available, X is oxidized how many times as fast as Y?","choices":["0.29","3.5","4.0","7.2"],"ans":1,"exp":"With the same availability, everything but k cancels: rate X ÷ rate Y = k_X ÷ k_Y = 5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**. 0.29 is the ratio upside down, 4.0 the difference, and 7.2 the sum.","how":{"ask":"Write each rate as k × availability, then divide the two rates.","know":"• **rate = k × [substrate]** (first order in each).\n• With **equal availability**, the concentrations cancel: **rate₁/rate₂ = k₁/k₂**.\n• “How many times faster” is a ratio, not a difference.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two rate constants.","hl":"5.6 s⁻¹","calc":["k_X = 5.6 s⁻¹","k_Y = 1.6 s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Write the ratio of rates; equal availability cancels.","calc":["rate X ÷ rate Y = (k_X·[S]) ÷ (k_Y·[S])","= k_X ÷ k_Y"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**"]},{"text":"0.29 is 1.6 ÷ 5.6 (upside down), 4.0 is 5.6 − 1.6, and 7.2 is 5.6 + 1.6.","strike":["0.29","4.0","7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.5."}],"trap":"Subtracting the rate constants (4.0): “times faster” asks for a ratio."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for X"},{"t":"1.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for Y"},{"t":"equally available","note":"concentrations cancel"}]},{"text":"**Rate = k × amount.** With the same amount of each substrate, only k differs.","calc":["rate X/rate Y = k_X/k_Y"]},{"text":"**Divide, larger over smaller** for “how many times faster”.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = 3.5"]},{"text":"**Also:** rate constants change with pH; from a table, take both k values at the stated pH before dividing."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with equal availability, the ratio of two rates equals the ratio of their rate constants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-33a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:33#1","pe3-cp:33#2","pe3-cp:33#3","pe3-cp:33#4"],"q":"A biochemist needs two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic to line a DNA-binding groove. Which pair should she choose?","choices":["Lysine and histidine","Aspartate and glutamate","Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Lysine, arginine and histidine** have nitrogen-containing side chains that accept protons: they are **basic and hydrophilic**. Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxylates (acidic); leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have hydrocarbon side chains (nonpolar).","how":{"ask":"Sort each amino acid by its side chain: basic, acidic or nonpolar.","know":"• **Basic, hydrophilic**: Lys, Arg, His (N-containing side chains that take H⁺).\n• **Acidic**: Asp, Glu (side-chain COOH/COO⁻).\n• **Nonpolar**: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe (hydrocarbon side chains).\n• Arg's guanidino side chain (pKa ≈ 12.5) is protonated at pH 7.4, so free Arg is **+1** overall.","steps":[{"text":"The groove needs basic, water-loving side chains.","hl":"basic and hydrophilic"},{"text":"Lysine's side chain ends in –NH₃⁺ and histidine's in an imidazole: both basic.","hl":"Lysine and histidine","fig":"lysine"},{"text":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxylates, so they are acidic.","strike":["Aspartate and glutamate"]},{"text":"Leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have only C and H in their side chains, so they are nonpolar.","strike":["Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"]},{"text":"Also check arginine's charge at pH 7.4: compare the pH with each pKa.","calc":["α-COOH (≈2): pH above → COO⁻, −1","α-NH₂ (≈9): pH below → NH₃⁺, +1","guanidino (≈12.5): pH below → protonated, +1","net = −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and histidine."}],"trap":"Calling histidine nonpolar because of its ring: its imidazole nitrogens take H⁺, so it is grouped with the basic amino acids."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"basic and hydrophilic","note":"N-containing side chains"},{"t":"DNA-binding groove","note":"DNA is negatively charged"}]},{"text":"**Basic three.** Lys, Arg and His have side chains that accept protons; they are basic and hydrophilic."},{"text":"**Acidic two.** Asp and Glu have side-chain carboxyl groups; they are acidic and negatively charged at pH 7.4."},{"text":"**Nonpolar group.** Leu, Ile, Val and Phe have hydrocarbon side chains; they are hydrophobic."},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7.4 arginine's side chain is fully protonated (pKa far above 7.4), so with COO⁻ and NH₃⁺ the molecule carries a net +1 charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lys, Arg and His are basic and hydrophilic; Asp and Glu are acidic; Leu, Ile, Val and Phe are nonpolar."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:34#1"],"q":"A diluted household ammonia cleaner measures pH 11.3 at room temperature. What is the hydroxide ion concentration, [OH⁻], in the cleaner?","choices":["2.0 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"],"ans":0,"exp":"At room temperature **pOH = 14 − pH** = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7, and **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH** = 10^−2.7 ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M. The value 5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is [H⁺], not [OH⁻].","how":{"ask":"Convert pH to pOH, then pOH to [OH⁻].","know":"• At 25 °C, **pH + pOH = 14** (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴).\n• **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH**; [H⁺] = 10^−pH.\n• 10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2 × 10⁻³.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the pH.","hl":"pH 11.3","calc":["pOH = 14 − 11.3 = **2.7**"]},{"text":"Undo the log.","calc":["[OH⁻] = 10^−2.7","= 10^0.3 × 10⁻³","≈ **2.0 × 10⁻³ M**"]},{"text":"5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is 10^−11.3, the [H⁺]; 2.7 × 10⁻³ M puts the pOH in as the digits; 5.0 × 10⁻³ M mixes the [H⁺] digits with the pOH exponent.","strike":["5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 × 10⁻³ M."}],"trap":"Computing 10^−pH and calling it [OH⁻]: that is [H⁺]. A basic solution has [OH⁻] larger than [H⁺]."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 11.3","note":"basic, so [OH⁻] > [H⁺]"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"pH + pOH = 14"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"use pOH, not pH"}]},{"text":"**Switch to pOH.**","calc":["pOH = 14 − pH = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.** Split the exponent into a whole number and a decimal.","calc":["10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"**Check.** [H⁺][OH⁻] should be about 10⁻¹⁴.","calc":["(5.0 × 10⁻¹²)(2.0 × 10⁻³) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pOH = 14 − pH, then [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-35a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:35#1","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"q":"A peroxidase pulls one proton and one electron off the O–H group of 4-methylphenol. Which species results?","choices":["4-Methylphenoxyl radical","4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation","4-Methylbenzyl cation"],"ans":0,"exp":"A proton plus an electron is one **H• atom**. Once H• is gone from Ar–OH, the O keeps a **single, unpaired electron**: the **phenoxyl radical Ar–O•**, neutral. Losing only H⁺ would give the phenoxide anion; losing only an electron gives a radical cation; a benzyl cation would need loss from the CH₃ group.","how":{"ask":"Add up what leaves (H⁺ + e⁻) and see what the oxygen keeps.","know":"• **H⁺ + e⁻ = H•** (one hydrogen atom).\n• **Ar–OH − H• → Ar–O•**, a **phenoxyl radical** (neutral, unpaired electron on O).\n• Ar–OH − H⁺ → Ar–O⁻ (anion); Ar–OH − e⁻ → Ar–OH•⁺ (radical cation).\n• **Peroxidases** oxidize **phenols** this way.","steps":[{"text":"Both a proton and an electron leave from the O–H.","hl":"one proton and one electron","calc":["H⁺ + e⁻ = H•"]},{"text":"Remove H• from the phenol: O keeps one electron of the old O–H bond.","fig":"phenol","calc":["Ar–OH → Ar–O• + (H⁺ + e⁻)","charge: 0 → 0, one unpaired electron"]},{"text":"Losing only H⁺ gives the anion; losing only e⁻ gives a radical cation.","strike":["4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation"]},{"text":"Nothing leaves the CH₃ group, so no benzyl cation forms.","strike":["4-Methylbenzyl cation"]},{"text":"Answer: the 4-methylphenoxyl radical."}],"trap":"Stopping at deprotonation (phenoxide): the electron leaves too, so the oxygen ends neutral with one unpaired electron."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"peroxidase","note":"oxidizes phenols"},{"t":"one proton and one electron","note":"= one H atom"},{"t":"4-methylphenol","note":"a phenol, like Tyr's side chain"}]},{"text":"**Count what leaves.** H⁺ + e⁻ = H•, so the O–H loses a whole hydrogen atom.","calc":["Ar–O–H → Ar–O• + H•"]},{"text":"**Also:** peroxidases act on phenols: they use H₂O₂ to pull H• from an aromatic O–H."},{"text":"**Also:** tyrosine is the amino acid with a phenol side chain, so it is the residue a peroxidase can oxidize (to a tyrosyl radical).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** the look-alikes are not phenols: lysine's side chain ends in an amine, leucine's is an alkyl group, and glutamine's ends in an amide.","fig":["lysine-sc","leucine-sc","glutamine-sc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** removing H• (H⁺ + e⁻) from a phenol gives a phenoxyl radical, Ar–O•; peroxidases do this to phenols such as Tyr's side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-37a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:37#1"],"q":"Four residues line a Ca²⁺-binding pocket in a protein. Which side-chain atom is best placed to bind the calcium ion directly?","choices":["Glu –COO⁻ oxygen","Lys –NH₃⁺ nitrogen","Leu –CH₃ carbon","Phe ring carbon"],"ans":0,"exp":"Ca²⁺ is a hard cation that binds **negatively charged or δ− oxygens with lone pairs**: the carboxylate oxygens of **Glu and Asp** and backbone or side-chain **carbonyl O**. Lys's –NH₃⁺ is positive and repels it; methyl and ring carbons have no lone pairs to donate.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom with a negative charge (or δ−) and free lone pairs to offer the positive ion.","know":"• **Ca²⁺ ligands**: oxygen atoms, especially **carboxylate O⁻** of **Asp and Glu**, plus **carbonyl O** (C=O of the backbone, Asn or Gln).\n• Positive groups (Lys –NH₃⁺, Arg) repel a cation.\n• Carbon atoms of alkyl or aromatic groups have no lone pair to donate.","steps":[{"text":"The atom must attract a doubly charged cation.","hl":"bind the calcium ion directly"},{"text":"Glu's carboxylate, like Asp's (drawn), carries a full negative charge spread over two oxygens, each with lone pairs.","fig":"aspartate-sc"},{"text":"Lys's ammonium nitrogen is positive and has no free lone pair: it repels Ca²⁺.","fig":"lysine-sc","strike":["Lys –NH₃⁺ nitrogen"]},{"text":"Methyl and ring carbons are nonpolar and have no lone pairs.","fig":["leucine-sc","phenylalanine-sc"],"strike":["Leu –CH₃ carbon","Phe ring carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: the Glu carboxylate oxygen."}],"trap":"Picking a nitrogen because N has a lone pair: in Lys it is protonated (–NH₃⁺) and positive, so it repels Ca²⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺-binding pocket","note":"a positive ion"},{"t":"side-chain atom","note":"which atom donates"}]},{"text":"**Opposites attract.** Ca²⁺ is a 2+ cation; it needs an electron-rich atom with lone pairs, best of all a negative one.","calc":["COO⁻ oxygen: −, lone pairs → binds","NH₃⁺: + → repels","C–H carbon: no lone pair → no bond"]},{"text":"**Also:** carbonyl oxygens (C=O of the peptide backbone, Asn, Gln) are δ− and also coordinate Ca²⁺, which is why Ca²⁺ loops are rich in Asp, Glu and backbone C=O."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ca²⁺ binds oxygen: the carboxylate O of Asp and Glu and carbonyl O; never a positive N or a carbon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-38a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:38#1","pe3-cp:38#2","pe3-cp:38#3"],"q":"Heme's porphyrin ring is built by joining four aromatic rings of the same type through one-carbon bridges. Which ring is that building block?","choices":["Five-membered, one N","Five-membered, two N","Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"],"ans":0,"exp":"A porphyrin is four **pyrrole** rings linked by =CH– bridges, and a pyrrole is **five-membered with one N**. The five-membered ring with two N is imidazole (histidine's side chain, which binds the heme iron from below); six-membered with one N is pyridine, and with two N a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"Name the unit heme is made of, then describe that ring by its size and its count of nitrogens.","know":"• **Heme** = a porphyrin ring (four **pyrrole** units joined by =CH– bridges) with Fe²⁺ held by the four inward-facing N.\n• **Pyrrole**: 5 ring atoms, **1 N** (an N–H).\n• **Imidazole**: 5 ring atoms, **2 N**; the side chain of **His**, whose free N binds the heme Fe.\n• **Pyridine**: 6 ring atoms, 1 N. **Pyrimidine**: 6 ring atoms, 2 N.","steps":[{"text":"The question asks for the repeating unit of the porphyrin ring.","hl":"four aromatic rings of the same type"},{"text":"That unit is pyrrole. Count its ring atoms and its nitrogens.","fig":"pyrrole","calc":["ring atoms: 4 C + 1 N = **5**","nitrogens: **1** (N–H)"]},{"text":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring. It is not part of the porphyrin; it is the residue whose free N lone pair binds the heme iron from below.","fig":["imidazole","t3-his-ring"],"strike":["Five-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Six-membered rings are pyridine (one N) and pyrimidine (two N); neither builds a porphyrin.","fig":"pyridine","strike":["Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Answer: five-membered, one N (pyrrole)."}],"trap":"Picking imidazole because histidine is drawn next to heme: His only binds the iron; the ring itself is made of pyrroles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porphyrin ring","note":"the ring of heme"},{"t":"four aromatic rings of the same type","note":"the repeating unit"}]},{"text":"**Heme = porphyrin + Fe.** The porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon bridges, with their four N pointing at the central iron."},{"text":"**Tell the rings apart by size and N count.**","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine"],"calc":["pyrrole: 5 atoms, 1 N","imidazole: 5 atoms, 2 N","pyridine: 6 atoms, 1 N"]},{"text":"**Also:** imidazole (two N) is the side chain of histidine; in heme proteins, His binds the heme iron through its N that carries a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**Also:** to name any ring, count its atoms first (5 or 6), then count the nitrogens (1 or 2)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heme's porphyrin is four pyrroles (5 atoms, 1 N); imidazole (5 atoms, 2 N) is His, which binds the iron; pyridine has 6 atoms and 1 N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-39a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:39#1"],"q":"At 37 °C, muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ and fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹. Which value is muscle's resistivity?","choices":["400 Ω·cm","2500 Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm","2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity: ρ = 1/σ.** For muscle, ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹) = **400 Ω·cm**; taking 1/σ also flips the (Ω·cm)⁻¹ unit to Ω·cm. 2500 Ω·cm belongs to fat.","how":{"ask":"Pick the muscle value and take its reciprocal, unit included.","know":"• **ρ = 1/σ**: resistivity and conductivity are reciprocals.\n• Units flip too: (Ω·cm)⁻¹ ↔ Ω·cm.\n• A good conductor (large σ) has a small ρ.","steps":[{"text":"Use the muscle value, not the fat value. 2500 Ω·cm is fat's resistivity (1 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴).","hl":"2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","strike":["2500 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Take the reciprocal.","calc":["ρ = 1 ÷ σ","ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹)","ρ = 0.4 × 10³ = **400 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm keeps σ's number and only swaps the unit. 40 Ω·cm misplaces the decimal (1 ÷ 0.025).","strike":["2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 400 Ω·cm."}],"trap":"Copying the conductivity value and writing Ω·cm after it: an inverted unit, (Ω·cm)⁻¹, means you must take the reciprocal of the number too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the right number**.","marks":[{"t":"muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"σ for muscle"},{"t":"fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"not needed"},{"t":"muscle's resistivity","note":"want ρ"}]},{"text":"**Reciprocals.** Conductivity measures how easily current flows; resistivity measures how hard it is. One is 1 over the other.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ","unit: 1 ÷ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ = Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["1 ÷ 0.0025 = 400","ρ = 400 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ρ = 1/σ, and the unit flips from (Ω·cm)⁻¹ to Ω·cm; read the value for the material asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-42a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:42#1","pe3-cp:42#2"],"q":"Variant R of a DNA fold unfolds between pH 6.3 and 6.7, with pK 6.5; variant S unfolds between pH 5.8 and 8.6, with pK 7.2. Which is more cooperative, and what is its pK?","choices":["R; pK 6.5","S; pK 7.2","R; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"],"ans":0,"exp":"Cooperativity is read from **steepness**: R goes from folded to unfolded within 0.4 pH units, S over 2.8 units. The narrow, steep transition (R) is the more cooperative, all-or-none one, and its midpoint, its pK, is 6.5. The pK of S (7.2) says where S unfolds, not how sharply.","how":{"ask":"Compare the width of each transition, then read the pK of the narrower one.","know":"• A **cooperative** transition is all-or-none: the structure unfolds over a **narrow** range, so the curve is **steep**.\n• A broad, shallow curve means a gradual, less cooperative change.\n• The **midpoint** (half folded) gives the pK (or Tm); it tells **where**, not **how sharply**.","steps":[{"text":"Read each variant's range.","hl":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","calc":["R: 6.7 − 6.3 = **0.4** pH units","S: 8.6 − 5.8 = **2.8** pH units"]},{"text":"Plot both: R drops like a cliff, S slopes gently.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction folded of two DNA variants versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"The narrower, steeper transition is the more cooperative one: R, not S.","strike":["S; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"]},{"text":"Match the curve to its own pK: R's midpoint is 6.5; 7.2 belongs to S.","strike":["R; pK 7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: R; pK 6.5."}],"trap":"Judging cooperativity by the midpoint (a higher pK or Tm): the midpoint gives position; only the steepness gives cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","note":"R's range"},{"t":"pK 6.5","note":"R's midpoint"},{"t":"between pH 5.8 and 8.6","note":"S's range"},{"t":"pK 7.2","note":"S's midpoint"}]},{"text":"**Width = cooperativity.** Subtract the ends of each range; the smaller width is the steeper, more cooperative curve.","calc":["R: 0.4 units","S: 2.8 units"]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pK.** On a curve, the pK is the pH where the fraction folded is one half.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Steep (cooperative) versus shallow transition","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** read each curve separately: its steepness and its own pK belong together."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steeper, narrower unfolding transition means more cooperative unfolding; the midpoint (pK or Tm) tells only where it happens."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:44#1","pe3-cp:44#2"],"q":"A single DNA strand is 28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T. Which nucleoside is absent from its complementary strand?","choices":["Deoxyadenosine","Deoxythymidine","Deoxyguanosine","Deoxycytidine"],"ans":0,"exp":"Pair each base: **A↔T, G↔C**. The template's A (28%) gives T in the complement, its G (35%) gives C, and its C (37%) gives G. With no T in the template, nothing calls for an A, so **deoxyadenosine** is absent from the new strand.","how":{"ask":"Write the partner of every base in the template, then see which partner never appears.","know":"• **Watson–Crick pairs**: A↔T (2 H-bonds), G↔C (3 H-bonds).\n• The complement's share of each base equals the template's share of its partner.\n• A base missing from the template means **its partner** is missing from the complement.","steps":[{"text":"List the template's bases.","hl":"28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T"},{"text":"Convert each to its partner in the complementary strand.","calc":["A 28% → **T 28%**","G 35% → **C 35%**","C 37% → **G 37%**","T 0% → **A 0%**"]},{"text":"The complement contains thymidine, cytidine and guanosine.","strike":["Deoxythymidine","Deoxycytidine","Deoxyguanosine"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxyadenosine."}],"trap":"Naming the base missing from the template (deoxythymidine): the complement lacks that base's partner, A."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"28% A","note":"pairs with T"},{"t":"35% G","note":"pairs with C"},{"t":"37% C","note":"pairs with G"},{"t":"no T","note":"its partner is missing"}]},{"text":"**Pair each base.** A↔T and G↔C, so each template percentage becomes its partner's percentage in the new strand.","calc":["A → T, T → A","G → C, C → G"]},{"text":"**Missing in, missing out.** A base absent from the template leaves its partner absent from the complement.","calc":["no T in template → no A in complement"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pair A↔T and G↔C; whatever base the template lacks, the complementary strand lacks its partner."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-45a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:45#1","pe3-cp:45#2","pe3-cp:45#3"],"q":"Proteins with pI values of 4.6, 5.9, 6.1 and 9.4 are loaded at pH 6.8 onto a cation-exchange column with negatively charged beads. How many bind?","choices":["0","1","3","4"],"ans":1,"exp":"A protein is **positive below its pI** and **negative above it**. At pH 6.8 only the pI 9.4 protein is below its pI, so only it is positive and binds the negative beads: **1**. The other three are negative; they would bind an anion exchanger instead.","how":{"ask":"Compare the buffer pH with each pI to get each protein's charge, then count the ones the beads attract.","know":"• **pH < pI → net positive**; **pH > pI → net negative**; pH = pI → no net charge.\n• **Cation exchanger** = negative beads → binds **positive** proteins.\n• **Anion exchanger** = positive beads → binds **negative** proteins.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pIs and the buffer pH.","hl":"4.6, 5.9, 6.1 and 9.4"},{"text":"Compare each pI with pH 6.8.","calc":["pI 4.6 < 6.8 → negative","pI 5.9 < 6.8 → negative","pI 6.1 < 6.8 → negative","pI 9.4 > 6.8 → **positive**"]},{"text":"Negative beads bind positive proteins: one.","calc":["positive proteins = **1**"]},{"text":"3 counts the negative proteins, which only an anion exchanger binds; 4 ignores charge; 0 misses that pI 9.4 lies above the buffer pH.","strike":["3","4","0"]},{"text":"Answer: 1."}],"trap":"Reversing the sign rule: a protein is positive when the pH is BELOW its pI, so only pI values above 6.8 bind a cation exchanger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.6, 5.9, 6.1 and 9.4","note":"the pIs"},{"t":"pH 6.8","note":"buffer"},{"t":"cation-exchange column","note":"negative beads"}]},{"text":"**Charge from pI.** Below its pI a protein holds extra H⁺ and is positive; above its pI it is negative.","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Also:** a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it binds the positive proteins: here only pI 9.4."},{"text":"**Also:** an anion exchanger has positive beads and binds the negative proteins: here pI 4.6, 5.9 and 6.1, three of them."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** proteins are positive below their pI and negative above it; a cation exchanger binds the positive ones, an anion exchanger the negative ones."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-47a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:47#1"],"q":"Of four nonmetals in groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4, which needs the most energy to lose its first electron?","choices":["sulfur","chlorine","selenium","bromine"],"ans":1,"exp":"First ionization energy **rises across a period** (more protons, same shell) and **rises up a group** (fewer shells, so the outer electrons sit nearer the protons). The group 17, period 3 atom, chlorine, is the smallest of the four and holds its electrons most tightly.","how":{"ask":"Place the four atoms on the table and apply both ionization-energy trends.","know":"• First ionization energy **increases left → right** across a period and **increases bottom → top** in a group.\n• Smaller atom → valence electrons closer to the nucleus → harder to remove.\n• Among halogens, **F** (smallest) is the hardest to ionize.","steps":[{"text":"Place the atoms: period 3 holds S (16) and Cl (17); period 4 holds Se (16) and Br (17).","hl":"groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4"},{"text":"Across a period, group 17 beats group 16: S and Se lose.","strike":["sulfur","selenium"]},{"text":"Up a group, period 3 beats period 4: Br, lower in group 17, loses.","strike":["bromine"]},{"text":"**Also:** extend the trend up group 17: F > Cl > Br > I, so fluorine, the smallest halogen, is the hardest of them all to ionize."},{"text":"Answer: chlorine."}],"trap":"Thinking a heavier atom with more protons holds its electrons more tightly: extra shells put them farther away and shield them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"lose its first electron","note":"first ionization energy"},{"t":"groups 16 and 17","note":"across a period"},{"t":"periods 3 and 4","note":"up and down a group"}]},{"text":"**Across a period:** more protons pull on the same shell, so ionization energy rises toward group 17 (and 18)."},{"text":"**Up a group:** fewer shells, a smaller atom, less shielding, so ionization energy rises toward the top."},{"text":"**Also:** of the halogens, fluorine is the hardest atom to ionize, because it is the smallest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** first ionization energy rises up a group and across a period; the smallest atom, top right, holds its electrons tightest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-48a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:48#1","pe3-cp:48#2"],"q":"A sled starts from rest and slides down an icy slope with constant acceleration of 3 m/s². Which describes its distance-versus-time graph?","choices":["Parabola (d ∝ t²)","Straight line (d ∝ t)","Horizontal line (d fixed)","Leveling curve (d ∝ √t)"],"ans":0,"exp":"Starting at zero velocity with constant a, **d = ½at²** = 1.5t² here, so **d ∝ t²**: doubling t quadruples d (1.5 m at 1 s, 6 m at 2 s), and the graph curves upward as a parabola. Only a **constant velocity** gives a straight line, d = vt.","how":{"ask":"Write the distance equation for a start at rest with constant acceleration and see how d scales with t.","know":"• **d = v₀t + ½at²**; from rest, v₀ = 0, so **d = ½at²** and **d ∝ t²** (a parabola).\n• **Constant velocity**: d = vt, a straight line.\n• Slope of a d–t graph = velocity.","steps":[{"text":"From rest, the v₀t term is zero.","hl":"starts from rest","calc":["v₀ = 0","d = v₀t + ½at² = ½at²"]},{"text":"Put in the acceleration and test a few times: doubling t gives four times the distance.","hl":"3 m/s²","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Distance of the sled versus a steady 6 m/s","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Distance (m)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Sled, a = 3 m/s²","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,1.5],[2,6],[3,13.5],[4,24]]},{"name":"Constant 6 m/s","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,6],[2,12],[3,18],[4,24]]}]},"calc":["d = ½ × 3 m/s² × t² = 1.5t²","t = 1 s → 1.5 m; t = 2 s → 6 m","t = 3 s → 13.5 m; t = 4 s → 24 m","2× the time → 4× the distance: **d ∝ t²**"]},{"text":"A straight line means equal distances each second: constant velocity, zero acceleration.","strike":["Straight line (d ∝ t)"]},{"text":"A flat line means the sled is not moving, and a leveling curve means it is slowing down; this sled speeds up.","strike":["Horizontal line (d fixed)","Leveling curve (d ∝ √t)"]},{"text":"Answer: parabola (d ∝ t²)."}],"trap":"Expecting a straight line because the acceleration is constant: constant acceleration makes the velocity graph straight, while the distance graph curves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"starts from rest","note":"v₀ = 0"},{"t":"constant acceleration of 3 m/s²","note":"a fixed"},{"t":"distance-versus-time graph","note":"shape of d(t)"}]},{"text":"**From rest:** d = ½at².","calc":["d = ½(3)t² = 1.5t²","1 s → 1.5 m, 2 s → 6 m"]},{"text":"**Compare shapes.** The accelerating sled curves upward; a steady speed draws a straight line.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speeding up versus steady speed","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Distance (m)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"a = 3 m/s² from rest","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,1.5],[2,6],[3,13.5],[4,24]]},{"name":"Constant 6 m/s","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,6],[2,12],[3,18],[4,24]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** a constant velocity gives d = vt, a straight line through the origin whose slope is the velocity."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest with constant acceleration, d = ½at², so d ∝ t² and the graph is a parabola; a constant velocity gives a straight line."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-49a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:49#1","pe3-cp:51#1"],"q":"A 4.0 MHz ultrasound beam passes from fat (sound speed 1440 m/s) into liver (1560 m/s). Which wave property is the same in both tissues?","choices":["Frequency","Wavelength","Speed","Amplitude"],"ans":0,"exp":"The **source sets the frequency**: every crest that reaches the boundary passes on, so the beam stays at 4.0 MHz in liver. The speed depends on the tissue, so **λ = v/f** changes too (0.36 mm in fat, 0.39 mm in liver), and the amplitude falls as some energy reflects or is absorbed.","how":{"ask":"Ask which property belongs to the source and which ones depend on the medium the wave is in.","know":"• The **source** fixes how many crests leave per second; a boundary cannot add or remove crests.\n• The **medium** sets the speed v.\n• **λ = v/f**, so if v changes at fixed f, λ changes in step.\n• **MHz = 10⁶ Hz**.\n• Reflection and absorption take energy, lowering the amplitude.","steps":[{"text":"The transducer sends out a fixed number of crests per second, and all of them cross into the liver. That count stays the same.","hl":"4.0 MHz","calc":["f in fat = f in liver = **4.0 MHz**"]},{"text":"Speed belongs to the medium, and the stem gives a different one for each tissue.","hl":"1560 m/s","strike":["Speed"]},{"text":"Wavelength follows from λ = v/f, with the MHz converted to Hz. Same f, different v, so different λ.","calc":["4.0 MHz = 4.0 × 10⁶ Hz","λ = v ÷ f","fat: 1440 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁶) = 3.6 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.36 mm**","liver: 1560 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁶) = 3.9 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.39 mm**"],"strike":["Wavelength"]},{"text":"Part of the beam reflects at the fat–liver boundary and tissue absorbs energy, so the transmitted amplitude is smaller.","strike":["Amplitude"]},{"text":"Answer: frequency."}],"trap":"Assuming the wavelength stays fixed and the frequency adjusts. It is the other way round: f is locked to the source, and λ = v/f takes up the change in speed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 MHz","note":"set by the source"},{"t":"passes from fat","note":"new medium"},{"t":"1560 m/s","note":"speed changes"},{"t":"the same in both tissues","note":"find the property that is kept"}]},{"text":"**Frequency belongs to the source.** Each crest that arrives at the boundary leaves it, so crests per second cannot change."},{"text":"**Also:** wavelength from speed and frequency is λ = v/f, with MHz = 10⁶ Hz.","calc":["λ(fat) = 1440 m/s ÷ 4.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 3.6 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.36 mm","λ(liver) = 1560 m/s ÷ 4.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 3.9 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.39 mm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at a boundary the frequency stays fixed by the source; speed, wavelength (λ = v/f, MHz = 10⁶ Hz) and amplitude can all change."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-53a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:53#1","pe3-cp:53#2","pe3-cp:53#3","pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"q":"Calcium phosphate, Ca₃(PO₄)₂, dissolves very slightly in water, giving Ca²⁺ and PO₄³⁻ ions. Which expression gives its solubility product, Ksp?","choices":["[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²","[Ca²⁺][PO₄³⁻]","[Ca²⁺]²[PO₄³⁻]³","[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²/[Ca₃(PO₄)₂]"],"ans":0,"exp":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ 3Ca²⁺(aq) + 2PO₄³⁻(aq). **The solid does not appear** in Ksp, and **each ion's concentration is raised to its coefficient**: Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]².","how":{"ask":"Write the dissolution equation, drop the solid, and use the coefficients as exponents.","know":"• Ksp = product of the **ion** concentrations at saturation.\n• **Solids (and pure liquids) are left out**: their activity is 1.\n• Each concentration is raised to the power of its **coefficient** in the balanced equation.","steps":[{"text":"Balance the dissolution: three Ca²⁺ and two PO₄³⁻ per formula unit.","hl":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂","calc":["Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ **3**Ca²⁺(aq) + **2**PO₄³⁻(aq)"]},{"text":"Leave out the solid; raise each ion to its coefficient.","calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²"]},{"text":"The solid never appears; omitting the exponents or swapping them misreads the coefficients.","strike":["[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²/[Ca₃(PO₄)₂]","[Ca²⁺][PO₄³⁻]","[Ca²⁺]²[PO₄³⁻]³"]},{"text":"Answer: [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²."}],"trap":"Writing the solid in the denominator, as in an ordinary K expression: a pure solid's activity is 1, so it is left out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂","note":"3 Ca, 2 PO₄ per unit"},{"t":"solubility product","note":"ions only"}]},{"text":"**Write the dissolution.** One formula unit releases 3 Ca²⁺ and 2 PO₄³⁻.","calc":["Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ 3Ca²⁺ + 2PO₄³⁻"]},{"text":"**Drop the solid.** A pure solid has activity 1, so it never appears in Ksp."},{"text":"**Coefficients become exponents.**","calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a 1:1 salt such as CaCO₃, every coefficient is 1, so Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** every K leaves out pure solids and liquids, so for a solid that gives off CO₂ only the gas remains: K = [CO₂] (or K_p = P_CO₂). Adding more of a solid changes nothing in K, so the equilibrium does not shift.","calc":["MgCO₃(s) ⇌ MgO(s) + CO₂(g)","K = [CO₂]; extra MgCO₃(s) or MgO(s) → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp is the product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, with the solid left out."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-54a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:54#1","pe3-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:52#1"],"q":"A metal's first three ionization energies are 600, 1150 and 4900 kJ/mol. To which family of the periodic table does the metal belong?","choices":["alkali metals","alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"],"ans":1,"exp":"Ionization energy leaps **once two electrons are gone** (1150 → 4900 kJ/mol), so the atom has two valence electrons and forms **2+ ions**: group 2, the **alkaline earth metals**.","how":{"ask":"Find where the big jump in ionization energy falls; that counts the valence electrons and names the group.","know":"• **Group names:** alkali metals = group 1; alkaline earth metals = group 2; halogens = group 17; noble gases = group 18; metalloids = the staircase elements (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te).\n• A huge jump in successive ionization energies comes once the valence shell is empty.\n• Group 2 loses 2 electrons → **2+ ions**; the jump comes after the 2nd electron.","steps":[{"text":"Compare each step with the one before it.","hl":"600, 1150 and 4900 kJ/mol","calc":["1150 ÷ 600 ≈ 1.9 (modest)","4900 ÷ 1150 ≈ 4.3 (**big jump**)"]},{"text":"The jump comes after electron 2: two valence electrons, group 2, 2+ ions."},{"text":"Alkali metals would jump after the 1st electron; inner transition metals typically form 3+ ions, so their big jump comes after the 3rd electron, not the 2nd; halogens are nonmetals that gain one electron.","strike":["alkali metals","inner transition metals","halogens"]},{"text":"**Also:** group 17 = halogens, group 18 = noble gases, and the metalloids sit on the staircase between metals and nonmetals."},{"text":"Answer: alkaline earth metals."}],"trap":"Confusing alkali (group 1, 1+) with alkaline earth (group 2, 2+)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"600","note":"1st electron"},{"t":"1150","note":"2nd electron"},{"t":"4900","note":"3rd electron: from the core"}]},{"text":"**Find the jump.** Divide each ionization energy by the one before it.","calc":["1150/600 ≈ 1.9","4900/1150 ≈ 4.3 → jump after the 2nd electron"]},{"text":"**Count valence electrons.** Two before the jump → group 2 → forms 2+ ions (Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, Sr²⁺)."},{"text":"**Also:** the group names to know: alkali metals (1), alkaline earth metals (2), halogens (17), noble gases (18), and metalloids along the staircase."},{"text":"**Also:** calcium is an alkaline earth metal (group 2), so its compounds, such as CaO, contain Ca²⁺. The transition metals fill the d block in the middle of the table. Sodium (group 1) forms only 1+ ions, while Mg and the transition metals Zn and Fe can form 2+, so Na cannot stand in for a divalent metal ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the big ionization-energy jump comes after the last valence electron; after the 2nd means group 2, the alkaline earth metals, which form 2+ ions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:55#1","pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"q":"At 360 K, a reaction has ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol and RT = 3.0 kJ/mol. What is its equilibrium constant K? (e² ≈ 7.4)","choices":["0.14","0.50","2.0","7.4"],"ans":3,"exp":"**ΔG° = −RT ln K**, so ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT = 6.0 ÷ 3.0 = 2.0, and K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**. A negative ΔG° always gives K > 1: products are favored once equilibrium is reached.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange ΔG° = −RT ln K for K, then undo the natural log.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K** (natural log). Rearranged: **K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)**.\n• **ΔG° < 0 ⇔ K > 1** (products favored); ΔG° > 0 ⇔ K < 1; ΔG° = 0 ⇔ K = 1.\n• K is always products over reactants as the reaction is written.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out ΔG° and RT. Both are in kJ/mol, so the units cancel.","hl":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol"},{"text":"Solve ΔG° = −RT ln K for ln K.","calc":["ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT","ln K = −(−6.0 kJ/mol) ÷ 3.0 kJ/mol","ln K = **2.0**"]},{"text":"Undo the natural log: raise e to that power.","calc":["K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**"]},{"text":"Check the sign: a negative ΔG° means K exceeds 1. That rules out 0.14 (sign of ΔG° flipped, e^−2) and 0.50 (RT ÷ ΔG°, with no log at all).","strike":["0.14","0.50"]},{"text":"2.0 is ln K, not K: the log was never undone.","strike":["2.0"]},{"text":"**Also, for unfolding:** for native ⇌ unfolded, products over reactants gives **K = [U]/[N]**, and **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** with the natural log, never e. With 10% unfolded: K = 10/90 ≈ 0.11, ln K < 0, so ΔG°′ > 0 (unfolding unfavorable).","calc":["K = [U]/[N] = 10 ÷ 90 ≈ 0.11","ΔG°′ = −RT ln(0.11) > 0"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.4."}],"trap":"Stopping at ln K = 2.0, or writing ΔG° = −RT e^K: the formula uses ln K, so K itself is e raised to −ΔG°/RT."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol","note":"negative: products favored"},{"t":"RT = 3.0 kJ/mol","note":"same units as ΔG°"},{"t":"e² ≈ 7.4","note":"the antilog you will need"}]},{"text":"**Link ΔG° and K.** The standard free energy and the equilibrium constant are tied by one formula with a natural log.","calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K"]},{"text":"**Solve for K.**","calc":["ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 6.0/3.0 = 2.0","K = e^2.0 ≈ 7.4"]},{"text":"**Sign check.** ΔG° < 0 ⇔ ln K > 0 ⇔ K > 1. A positive ΔG° gives K < 1; ΔG° = 0 gives K = 1."},{"text":"**Also:** for a native → unfolded change, K = [U]/[N] (product over reactant), so ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N]). If fewer than half the molecules are unfolded, K < 1 and ΔG°′ is positive."},{"text":"**Also:** the log is ln, not e: ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, while K = e^(−ΔG°′/RT) is the same law solved the other way."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K with K = products/reactants; ΔG° < 0 means K > 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-57a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:57#1","pe3-cp:57#2","pe3-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:57#4","pe3-cp:57#5"],"q":"In each species below, the central atom is bonded only to halogen atoms. Which one has an octahedral molecular geometry?","choices":["TeF₆","ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"],"ans":0,"exp":"Count electron domains on the central atom. **TeF₆**: 6 bonds, 0 lone pairs → **octahedral**. ICl₄⁻: 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs → square planar. ClF₃: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs → T-shaped. SiCl₄: 4 bonds → tetrahedral.","how":{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonds and lone pairs, then name the shape.","know":"• **Lone pairs** = (valence e⁻ − bonding e⁻) ÷ 2, after adding 1 e⁻ per negative charge and subtracting 1 per positive charge; each single bond uses 1 e⁻ of the central atom.\n• 6 bonds, 0 LP → **octahedral**.\n• 4 bonds + 2 LP → **square planar**.\n• 3 bonds + 2 LP → **T-shaped**.\n• 4 bonds, 0 LP → **tetrahedral**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the central atom's electron domains in each species.","hl":"octahedral molecular geometry","calc":["TeF₆: Te 6 e⁻, 6 bonds → 0 LP → 6 domains","ICl₄⁻: I 7 + 1 = 8 e⁻, 4 bonds → 2 LP → 6 domains","ClF₃: Cl 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 LP → 5 domains","SiCl₄: Si 4 e⁻, 4 bonds → 0 LP → 4 domains"]},{"text":"Only TeF₆ has all six domains as bonds, so atoms sit at all six octahedral corners.","calc":["TeF₆ → **octahedral**"]},{"text":"ICl₄⁻ is square planar (two lone pairs take opposite corners), ClF₃ is T-shaped, and SiCl₄ is tetrahedral.","strike":["ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"]},{"text":"Answer: TeF₆."}],"trap":"Picking ICl₄⁻ because it has six electron domains: its electron geometry is octahedral, but with two lone pairs the molecular shape is square planar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"central atom","note":"count its domains"},{"t":"octahedral molecular geometry","note":"6 bonds, no lone pairs"}]},{"text":"**Count electron domains.** Bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom set the electron geometry; only the bonded atoms set the molecular shape."},{"text":"**6 bonds, 0 lone pairs = octahedral.** TeF₆: Te's six valence electrons all go into six Te–F bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs is square planar: in ICl₄⁻ the lone pairs sit opposite each other."},{"text":"**Also:** 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs is T-shaped: ClF₃'s lone pairs take two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds and no lone pairs is tetrahedral, as in SiCl₄ (109.5° angles)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count domains on the central atom; 6 bonds/0 LP octahedral, 4 bonds/2 LP square planar, 3 bonds/2 LP T-shaped, 4 bonds/0 LP tetrahedral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:59#1","pe3-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:59#3"],"q":"A colorless crystal is extremely hard, does not conduct electricity, and has an extremely high melting point (above 3500 °C). Which substance is it?","choices":["diamond","solid CO₂","solid I₂","white phosphorus"],"ans":0,"exp":"Extreme hardness and a very high melting point mean **covalent bonds must break**, the mark of a **covalent network solid**. Diamond is carbon with each atom bonded to four others. CO₂, I₂ and P₄ are molecular solids held by weak forces.","how":{"ask":"Decide which kind of solid the properties fit, then find the substance of that kind.","know":"• **Covalent network solid**: every atom bonded covalently to several neighbors in one extended lattice → very hard, very high melting point. Period-2 example: **carbon** (diamond, graphite).\n• **Molecular solids** (N₂, O₂, CO₂, I₂, P₄): separate molecules held by weak forces → low melting points.\n• **P and I are not period 2** (P is period 3, I is period 5).","steps":[{"text":"Very hard, nonconducting and stable to thousands of degrees: covalent bonds hold the whole crystal.","hl":"extremely high melting point (above 3500 °C)"},{"text":"Solid CO₂ (dry ice) sublimes near −78 °C: separate molecules.","strike":["solid CO₂"]},{"text":"I₂ and P₄ are molecular solids with low melting points; neither element is in period 2 anyway.","strike":["solid I₂","white phosphorus"]},{"text":"**Also:** of the period-2 nonmetals C, N and O, only carbon forms a network; N₂ and O₂ are small diatomic molecules that are gases at room temperature."},{"text":"Answer: diamond."}],"trap":"Thinking strong bonds inside a molecule (C=O, N≡N) make a high melting point: melting a molecular solid breaks only the weak forces between molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key properties**.","marks":[{"t":"extremely hard","note":"strong bonds throughout"},{"t":"does not conduct electricity","note":"not a metal"},{"t":"above 3500 °C","note":"covalent bonds must break"}]},{"text":"**Name the solid type.** Hard + nonconducting + very high melting point = covalent network solid."},{"text":"**Period 2 example:** carbon. In diamond each C bonds to 4 others; in graphite, to 3 in sheets."},{"text":"**Also:** N₂ and O₂ are discrete molecules (gases at room temperature), and P and I are not in period 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom covalently bonded to several neighbors in an endless lattice makes a network solid; of the period-2 nonmetals C, N and O, only carbon forms one."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 4 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-03a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:3#1","pe4-cp:3#2"],"q":"Dihydrogen phosphate gives up a proton to water: H₂PO₄⁻ + H₂O → HPO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺. What happens to phosphorus's oxidation state, and what type of reaction is this?","choices":["Stays +5; acid–base","Stays +5; redox","Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"],"ans":0,"exp":"In H₂PO₄⁻: 2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1, so P = +5. In HPO₄²⁻: (+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2, so P = +5. **Only an H⁺ moves**, with its oxidation state of +1 unchanged, so no element changes oxidation state: it is **acid–base, not redox**.","how":{"ask":"Assign P's oxidation state before and after, then decide whether anything was oxidized or reduced.","know":"• Oxidation states: H = +1, O = −2; they add up to the species' charge.\n• **Redox** needs an element whose oxidation state changes. Moving H⁺ (still +1) changes no oxidation state: that is **acid–base**.","steps":[{"text":"Before: phosphorus in the acid.","hl":"H₂PO₄⁻","calc":["2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1","P = −1 − 2 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"After: phosphorus in the conjugate base.","hl":"HPO₄²⁻","calc":["(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2","P = −2 − 1 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"P stays +5, so the rising values are wrong.","strike":["Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"]},{"text":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is transferred, so the reaction is not redox.","strike":["Stays +5; redox"]},{"text":"Answer: Stays +5; acid–base."}],"trap":"Thinking that losing a bond to H oxidizes the central atom: the H leaves as H⁺, taking no electrons, so the central atom's oxidation state is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"gives up a proton","note":"H⁺ transfer"},{"t":"H₂PO₄⁻","note":"charge −1"},{"t":"HPO₄²⁻","note":"charge −2"}]},{"text":"**Assign oxidation states.** H = +1, O = −2, and the sum equals the charge.","calc":["H₂PO₄⁻: P = +5","HPO₄²⁻: P = +5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same holds for nitrogen: in NH₃, N + 3(+1) = 0 → N = −3; in NH₄⁺, N + 4(+1) = +1 → N = −3. Gaining H⁺ leaves N at −3.","calc":["NH₃: N = −3","NH₄⁺: N = −3"]},{"text":"**Classify.** No oxidation state changes and only H⁺ moves: acid–base, not redox."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reaction is redox only if some element's oxidation state changes; a pure H⁺ transfer is acid–base."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-04a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:4#1","pe4-cp:4#2"],"q":"Propane burns completely: C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 3 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. If 8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol) burns, what volume of CO₂ is released at STP?","choices":["4.5 L","13.4 L","14.7 L","17.9 L"],"ans":1,"exp":"8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = 0.20 mol propane. The equation gives 3 CO₂ per propane: 0.60 mol CO₂. **At STP one mole of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L**: 0.60 × 22.4 = 13.44 ≈ **13.4 L**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, use the coefficients to get moles of CO₂, then multiply by the STP molar volume.","know":"• moles = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• The coefficients give the **mole ratio** of product to reactant.\n• **V = n × 22.4 L/mol**, true only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm); at 25 °C the molar volume is about 24.5 L/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Moles of propane.","hl":"8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol)","calc":["n = 8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = **0.20 mol**"]},{"text":"Use the ratio from the equation.","hl":"3 CO₂","calc":["n(CO₂) = 0.20 mol × 3 = **0.60 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to volume at STP.","hl":"at STP","calc":["V = 0.60 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 13.44 L ≈ **13.4 L**"]},{"text":"4.5 L skips the 3 : 1 ratio (0.20 × 22.4); 17.9 L uses the water coefficient (0.80 × 22.4); 14.7 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C, not at STP.","strike":["4.5 L","17.9 L","14.7 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 13.4 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol without the mole ratio, or at a temperature other than STP."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.8 g","note":"mass burned"},{"t":"44 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"3 CO₂","note":"3 mol CO₂ per mol propane"},{"t":"at STP","note":"22.4 L/mol applies"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.**","calc":["8.8 ÷ 44 = 0.20 mol"]},{"text":"**Moles of reactant to moles of product** with the coefficients.","calc":["0.20 × 3 = 0.60 mol CO₂"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters at STP.** One mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 0 °C and 1 atm; at other conditions use PV = nRT instead.","calc":["0.60 × 22.4 = 13.4 L"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V(gas, STP) = (moles of reactant × product/reactant coefficient ratio) × 22.4 L/mol, and 22.4 L/mol holds at STP only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-07a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:7#1","pe4-cp:7#2","pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"q":"Glucose standards of 0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48. A serum sample read 0.84, so it was diluted 10-fold and reread at 0.28. What was its original concentration?","choices":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","14 mM","42 mM"],"ans":2,"exp":"The standards rise 0.20 absorbance unit per mM (**A ∝ c**). The first reading, 0.84, lies above the top standard, so it cannot be trusted; the diluted reading, 0.28, lies inside the range and gives 0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM. Undo the 10-fold dilution: 1.4 × 10 = **14 mM**.","how":{"ask":"Read the in-range reading off the standard line, then multiply by the dilution factor.","know":"• **Beer's law**: A = εbc, so at a fixed path length **A ∝ c**; the standards give the slope (absorbance per unit concentration).\n• Only **interpolate**: a reading above the highest standard must be diluted into the standard range, never read off an extended line.\n• Original concentration = **diluted concentration × dilution factor**.","steps":[{"text":"The standards set the line; find its slope.","hl":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","calc":["slope = 0.16 ÷ 0.8 = **0.20 per mM**","check: 0.48 ÷ 2.4 = 0.20 per mM"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Glucose standards: absorbance rises in step with concentration","x":{"label":"Glucose (mM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":0.6,"step":0.1},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.8,0.16],[1.6,0.32],[2.4,0.48]]}]}},{"text":"The first reading lies above the top standard (0.48), outside the proven straight range, so it is not used.","hl":"A serum sample read 0.84"},{"text":"Use the diluted reading, which falls inside the range.","hl":"reread at 0.28","calc":["c(diluted) = A ÷ slope","c(diluted) = 0.28 ÷ 0.20 per mM = **1.4 mM**"]},{"text":"Undo the dilution.","hl":"diluted 10-fold","calc":["c(original) = 1.4 mM × 10 = **14 mM**"]},{"text":"1.4 mM forgets to multiply back by the dilution factor. 4.2 mM extrapolates the line to the out-of-range 0.84 (0.84 ÷ 0.20) and 42 mM then multiplies that by 10 as well.","strike":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","42 mM"]},{"text":"Answer: 14 mM."}],"trap":"Reporting the concentration in the cuvette (1.4 mM): the sample was diluted, so multiply by 10."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM","note":"standard concentrations"},{"t":"0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","note":"their absorbances"},{"t":"read 0.84","note":"above the top standard"},{"t":"diluted 10-fold","note":"dilution factor"},{"t":"reread at 0.28","note":"the in-range reading"}]},{"text":"**The standards give the slope.** Absorbance is proportional to concentration, so divide any standard's A by its c.","calc":["0.32 ÷ 1.6 = 0.20 per mM"]},{"text":"**Interpolate, don't extrapolate.** The standards only show the line is straight up to A = 0.48. A reading above that is diluted until it lands between the standards."},{"text":"**Read, then multiply back.** The line gives the concentration in the diluted sample; the dilution factor returns it to the original.","calc":["0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM","1.4 mM × 10 = 14 mM"]},{"text":"**Also:** with no reading above range, the same two steps apply: a sample diluted 5-fold that reads A = 0.24 contains 0.24 ÷ 0.20 = 1.2 mM in the cuvette and 1.2 × 5 = 6.0 mM originally."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c(original) = (A ÷ slope of the standards) × dilution factor, using only a reading that falls inside the range of the standards."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:10#1","pe4-cp:10#2","pe4-cp:10#3"],"q":"Four alcohols are each heated with chromic acid, H₂CrO₄, a strong oxidizing agent. Which alcohol is NOT oxidized?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"exp":"Oxidizing an alcohol removes **the H on the carbon bearing the OH** and turns C–OH into C=O. In **2-methylbutan-2-ol** that carbon carries three carbon groups and no H, so it is a tertiary alcohol and resists oxidation. Pentan-1-ol (primary) goes to pentanal and then pentanoic acid; the two secondary alcohols become ketones.","how":{"ask":"Classify each alcohol as 1°, 2° or 3° by counting the carbons on the carbinol carbon, then find the one with no H there.","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon holding the OH. Oxidation needs an **H on that carbon**.\n• **1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid** (a strong oxidant like H₂CrO₄ goes all the way to the acid).\n• **2° alcohol → ketone** (stops there).\n• **3° alcohol → no reaction** (no H on the carbinol carbon).","steps":[{"text":"The reagent is a strong oxidant, so any alcohol that can be oxidized will be.","hl":"chromic acid, H₂CrO₄"},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol: the OH carbon is CH₂, bonded to one carbon, so it is primary. It is oxidized to an aldehyde and then on to the acid.","calc":["CH₃(CH₂)₃CH₂OH → CH₃(CH₂)₃CHO → CH₃(CH₂)₃COOH"],"strike":["Pentan-1-ol"]},{"text":"Pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon is CH, bonded to two carbons, so both are secondary and become ketones.","calc":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃ → pentan-2-one","CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂ → 3-methylbutan-2-one"],"strike":["Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"2-Methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon holds two CH₃ groups and an ethyl, three carbons and no H. It is tertiary and cannot form a C=O without breaking a C–C bond.","calc":["(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃: carbinol C has 0 H → no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-Methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Picking a branched secondary alcohol because it looks crowded: branching elsewhere does not matter; only the H on the carbinol carbon does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"chromic acid","note":"strong oxidant"},{"t":"NOT oxidized","note":"look for the alcohol that cannot lose an H"}]},{"text":"**Find the carbinol carbon** in each name: the number before \"-ol\" tells you which carbon holds the OH. Count how many carbons are bonded to it: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°."},{"text":"**Primary alcohols** have two H's on the carbinol carbon: they go to an **aldehyde**, and a strong oxidant carries them on to the **carboxylic acid**.","calc":["RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH"]},{"text":"**Secondary alcohols** have one H there: they become a **ketone** and stop.","calc":["R₂CHOH → R₂C=O"]},{"text":"**Tertiary alcohols** have no H there: nothing to remove, so **no oxidation**.","calc":["R₃COH → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon carries an H: 1° gives aldehyde then acid, 2° gives ketone, 3° does not react."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-11a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:11#1","pe4-cp:11#2","pe4-cp:11#3","pe4-cp:11#4"],"q":"In CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃, the bromine and the terminal CH₃ bonded to the CH carbon are cis to each other. What is the compound's name?","choices":["(Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"],"ans":0,"exp":"The five-carbon chain holds the C=C, and numbering from the CH₃–CH= end puts it at **C2**, with Br on **C3**: 3-bromopent-2-ene. On C2, **CH₃ beats H**; on C3, **Br (atomic number 35) beats the ethyl carbon (6)**. Those two winners are on the same side, so the alkene is **Z**, even though the methyl and the ethyl are on opposite sides.","how":{"ask":"Number the chain to give the C=C the lowest locant, name the substituent, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon.","know":"• **Parent chain**: the longest chain that contains the C=C, numbered from the end **nearer the C=C**.\n• **Substituents** get the locant of the carbon they sit on.\n• **CIP priority**: on each alkene carbon, the atom of higher atomic number wins.\n• **Z** = the two winners on the **same side**; **E** = opposite sides.","steps":[{"text":"The longest chain through the C=C has five carbons. From the left, the C=C starts at C2; from the right, at C3. Number from the left.","hl":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","calc":["left: C1H₃–C2H=C3(Br)–C4H₂–C5H₃ → C=C at 2 ✓","right: C=C at 3 ✗"]},{"text":"Numbering from the ethyl end gives the C=C a locant of 3, which is higher than needed.","strike":["(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"]},{"text":"The only substituent is Br, on C3.","calc":["3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank the groups on each alkene carbon by the atomic number of the first atom.","calc":["C2: CH₃ (C, 6) > H (1) → CH₃ wins","C3: Br (35) > CH₂CH₃ (C, 6) → Br wins"]},{"text":"The winners, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side, so the alkene is Z. Calling it E comes from comparing the two alkyl groups instead of Br.","hl":"are cis to each other","strike":["(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: (Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene."}],"trap":"Reading E/Z as cis/trans of the carbon chain: the ethyl and the methyl are on opposite sides, but Br outranks the ethyl, so the winners are cis and the name is Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","note":"five carbons, one Br on an alkene carbon"},{"t":"the bromine and the terminal CH₃","note":"the groups whose positions are given"},{"t":"cis to each other","note":"same side; compare with the priorities"}]},{"text":"**Pick and number the chain.** Take the longest chain that includes both alkene carbons, and number from the end that reaches the C=C first.","calc":["C=C at 2 from the left vs 3 from the right → left"]},{"text":"**Name the substituents** with their locants and put them in front of the parent, in alphabetical order if there are several.","calc":["Br on C3 → 3-bromo + pent-2-ene"]},{"text":"**Rank by CIP.** On each alkene carbon, compare the two attached atoms; higher atomic number wins. If they tie, move out to the next atoms.","calc":["Br (35) > C (6) > H (1)"]},{"text":"**Assign Z or E.** Winners on the same side = Z (zusammen); on opposite sides = E (entgegen)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the longest chain containing the C=C from its nearer end, name the substituents, then put Z when the higher-priority groups on the two alkene carbons share a side and E when they do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-12a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:12#1"],"q":"A hiker walks 11 km in a straight line, turns through any angle, then walks 7 km more. Which distance from the start is impossible?","choices":["4 km","10 km","18 km","19 km"],"ans":3,"exp":"Two displacements add as vectors: the resultant lies between **|11 − 7| = 4 km** (turning back) and **11 + 7 = 18 km** (going straight on). **19 km** is outside that range, so it can't happen.","how":{"ask":"Find the smallest and largest possible magnitudes of the vector sum.","know":"• For vectors of magnitudes A and B: **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Minimum when they point opposite (180°), maximum when parallel (0°); every value between is reachable at some angle.","steps":[{"text":"The two legs are vectors of 11 km and 7 km at an adjustable angle.","hl":"turns through any angle"},{"text":"Compute the two extremes.","calc":["min = |11 − 7| = **4 km** (walks back)","max = 11 + 7 = **18 km** (keeps straight)","at 90°: √(11² + 7²) = √170 ≈ 13 km"]},{"text":"4 km and 18 km are the ends of the range, and 10 km lies inside it.","strike":["4 km","18 km","10 km"]},{"text":"Answer: 19 km."}],"trap":"Thinking the endpoints are impossible: turning fully back or not at all gives exactly 4 km or 18 km."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"11 km","note":"vector A"},{"t":"7 km","note":"vector B"},{"t":"any angle","note":"the sum can vary"},{"t":"impossible","note":"look outside the range"}]},{"text":"**Smallest sum:** the vectors point opposite, so they subtract.","calc":["|A − B| = |11 − 7| = 4 km"]},{"text":"**Largest sum:** the vectors point the same way, so they add.","calc":["A + B = 11 + 7 = 18 km"]},{"text":"**Everything between is possible** at some angle; anything outside is not.","calc":["4 km ≤ R ≤ 18 km","19 km > 18 km → impossible"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** |A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B; a magnitude outside that range is impossible."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-13a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:13#1","pe4-cp:13#2"],"q":"Overnight contact lenses flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye so distant objects focus on the retina. What does flattening change?","choices":["Larger radius; lower power","Larger radius; higher power","Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"],"ans":0,"exp":"A **flatter** surface is part of a bigger sphere: its **radius of curvature increases**. A less curved surface bends light less, so the **focal length gets longer** (lower power, P = 1/f) and the focus moves back from in front of the retina onto it.","how":{"ask":"Link flatter shape → radius of curvature → focal length → where the image lands.","know":"• **Flatter = larger radius of curvature** (a flat surface has an infinite radius).\n• For a refracting surface or lens, **f grows with R** (lensmaker: 1/f ∝ 1/R); power P = 1/f falls.\n• **Myopia**: the eye is too converging, so the image forms in front of the retina; weakening it moves the focus back.","steps":[{"text":"Flattening means less curvature, so the radius of curvature grows.","hl":"flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye","calc":["flatter → R larger"]},{"text":"A larger radius bends light less: the cornea is less converging.","calc":["1/f ∝ 1/R → R up → f up","power P = 1/f → down"]},{"text":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening.","strike":["Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Higher power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen myopia.","strike":["Larger radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Answer: larger radius; lower power (longer focal length)."}],"trap":"Pairing a larger radius with higher power (shorter focal length): a flatter surface is weaker, not stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"myopic","note":"focus falls in front of the retina"},{"t":"flatten","note":"less curved surface"},{"t":"focus on the retina","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Flatter means a bigger circle.** A gently curved surface belongs to a sphere with a large radius.","calc":["more curved → small R","flatter → large R"]},{"text":"**Bigger radius, weaker focusing.** Focal length rises with R, so the cornea converges light less.","calc":["R up → f up → power down"]},{"text":"**Also:** a myopic eye focuses too strongly, in front of the retina; a longer focal length moves the image back onto the retina.","calc":["too strong → focus in front","weaker cornea → focus moves back onto retina"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** flattening the cornea raises its radius of curvature, which lengthens the focal length and pushes a nearsighted eye's focus back onto the retina."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-16a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:16#1"],"q":"A laser can fire continuously or in 4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power. Why is pulsing gentler on nearby tissue?","choices":["Gaps let the heat dissipate","Its mean output is larger","Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"],"ans":0,"exp":"The pulsed laser is on only 4 of every 20 ms, so it is **off 16 of every 20 ms**. During each 16-ms off-time, **deposited heat diffuses away**, so the surrounding tissue stays at a lower average temperature than under a beam that never stops.","how":{"ask":"What happens to the heat in the tissue while the pulsed laser is off?","know":"Heat flows from a warm spot into cooler surroundings over time, so any off-time lets the tissue cool before the next burst. The pulsed beam is on 4 ms and **off 16 ms** of every 20-ms cycle.","steps":[{"text":"Find the timing: same strength while on, but the pulsed beam is on only part of each cycle.","hl":"4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power"},{"text":"Find the off-time in each cycle.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = **16 ms** per cycle"]},{"text":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so it can't deliver more energy.","strike":["Its mean output is larger"]},{"text":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not its wavelength or how much the surface reflects.","strike":["Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"]},{"text":"During each 16-ms gap, heat diffuses out of the treated spot, so the nearby tissue's average temperature stays lower.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Nearby tissue temperature (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":35,"max":55,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[20,42],[40,47],[60,52]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[4,38],[20,37.3],[24,38.3],[40,37.6],[44,38.6],[60,37.9]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: gaps let the heat dissipate."}],"trap":"Equal peak power does not mean equal energy per second. The pulsed beam is off 16 of every 20 ms, so heat has time to leave between pulses."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"continuously","note":"always on"},{"t":"4-ms pulses","note":"on-time"},{"t":"every 20 ms","note":"one full cycle"},{"t":"equal peak power","note":"same strength while on"}]},{"text":"**Heat spreads** from a hot spot into cooler surroundings over time. Each off-time lets it leave before the next pulse arrives.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = 16 ms","continuous beam: off-time = 0 → heat piles up"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulsed light at the same peak power heats tissue less, because the gaps let heat dissipate and the average temperature stays lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-17a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:17#1","pe4-cp:17#2","pe4-cp:17#3","pe4-cp:17#4"],"q":"Which intermolecular forces act between molecules in a sample of pure liquid methanol, CH₃OH?","choices":["London forces only","Hydrogen bonds only","Dipole–dipole and London forces","Ion–dipole forces and London forces"],"ans":2,"exp":"**Every** molecule has London dispersion forces. Methanol is **polar** (bent C–O–H with a strongly electronegative O), so it also has dipole–dipole attractions, and because its H sits directly on oxygen those attractions are **hydrogen bonds**, the strongest kind of dipole–dipole force. Methanol has no ions, so there is no ion–dipole force.","how":{"ask":"List the forces in order: London (always), then dipole–dipole if the molecule is polar, then check whether that dipole force is hydrogen bonding.","know":"• **London dispersion**: present between **all** molecules.\n• **Dipole–dipole**: added when the molecule is **polar**.\n• **Hydrogen bonding**: an especially strong **dipole–dipole** force, when H is bonded to **N, O or F**.\n• **Ion–dipole**: needs an **ion** in the mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Every molecule has London forces, so the answer must include them. A list without them, or with only one polar force, is incomplete.","strike":["Hydrogen bonds only"]},{"text":"Methanol is polar: the O pulls electron density from C and H, and the bent C–O–H gives a net dipole. So dipole–dipole forces are present too.","hl":"methanol, CH₃OH","strike":["London forces only"]},{"text":"The H on O makes those dipole–dipole forces hydrogen bonds, which are a strong kind of dipole–dipole force, not a separate force outside it.","calc":["O–H present → H-bonding ⊂ dipole–dipole"]},{"text":"Ion–dipole forces need ions, and pure methanol is a molecular liquid.","hl":"pure liquid","strike":["Ion–dipole forces and London forces"]},{"text":"**Also:** ammonia works the same way: NH₃ is polar and has H on N, so its molecules attract by dipole–dipole forces (hydrogen bonds) plus London forces."},{"text":"Answer: Dipole–dipole and London forces."}],"trap":"Choosing hydrogen bonds alone: hydrogen bonding is a kind of dipole–dipole force, and London forces are always there as well."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"intermolecular forces","note":"between molecules, not bonds inside one"},{"t":"pure liquid methanol","note":"no ions present"},{"t":"CH₃OH","note":"H on O"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: London forces.** Every molecule has a temporary dipole from moving electrons, so London dispersion forces are always on the list."},{"text":"**Step 2: polar?** If the molecule has polar bonds that do not cancel, add dipole–dipole forces.","calc":["CH₃OH: bent C–O–H → polar → dipole–dipole ✓"]},{"text":"**Step 3: hydrogen bonding?** If H is bonded to N, O or F, the dipole–dipole force is a hydrogen bond, the strongest dipole–dipole type.","calc":["O–H → hydrogen bonds"]},{"text":"**Also:** for NH₃: London ✓, polar (pyramidal) ✓, H on N ✓ → dipole–dipole forces (hydrogen bonds) and London forces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** start with London forces (always), add dipole–dipole for a polar molecule, and call that dipole–dipole force hydrogen bonding when H sits on N, O or F."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-18a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:18#1","pe4-cp:18#2"],"q":"Hydroxylamine, NH₂OH, is a weak base with Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹. Approximately what is [OH⁻] in a 0.40 M hydroxylamine solution at equilibrium?","choices":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"],"ans":2,"exp":"For a weak base, **Kb = x²/C**, so **x = √(Kb·C)** = √(9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹). Rewrite with an even power: √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. So little base reacts that C barely changes.","how":{"ask":"Set up Kb with x for the ions formed, solve x = √(Kb·C), and take the root of the power of ten carefully.","know":"• B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻, so Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] = **x²/C** when x is small next to C.\n• **x = √(Kb·C)**; x is both [OH⁻] and [BH⁺].\n• To take a square root of 10ⁿ, first make n even (move a factor of 10 into the coefficient), then halve it.","steps":[{"text":"Collect the base's Kb and its starting concentration.","hl":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","calc":["C = 0.40 M"]},{"text":"Write the small-x expression and solve for x.","calc":["Kb = x² / C","x = √(Kb · C)","Kb · C = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40 = 3.6 × 10⁻⁹"]},{"text":"Make the exponent even before taking the root.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 = 6.0; √10⁻¹⁰ = 10⁻⁵","x = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M**"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: x is far below C.","calc":["6.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.40 ≈ 0.015 %"]},{"text":"3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M is Kb·C with no square root; 9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M is √Kb, which leaves out C; 1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M takes √3.6 without first making the exponent even.","strike":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M."}],"trap":"Halving an odd exponent as if it were even: rewrite 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ as 36 × 10⁻¹⁰ first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"weak base","note":"only a little reacts"},{"t":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","note":"base constant"},{"t":"0.40 M","note":"starting concentration C"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"x, the amount that reacts"}]},{"text":"**Write Kb with x.** Each base that reacts makes one BH⁺ and one OH⁻, and the base left is still about C.","calc":["Kb = x·x / (C − x) ≈ x² / C"]},{"text":"**Solve for x.**","calc":["x = √(Kb · C) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹)"]},{"text":"**Estimate the root of the power of ten.** Shift one factor of 10 so the exponent is even, then halve it; the coefficient's root is a nearby perfect square.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M"]},{"text":"**Also:** e.g. Kb = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵, C = 0.10 M: x = √(2.5 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻³ M (√2.5 ≈ 1.6, √10⁻⁶ = 10⁻³)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a weak base, [OH⁻] = [BH⁺] ≈ √(Kb·C); make the power of ten even, then take half of it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-19a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:19#1","pe4-cp:19#2","pe4-cp:47#1"],"q":"Four sealed flasks each hold a different gas above a small volume of water. After shaking, in which flask would the gas pressure fall the least?","choices":["Hydrogen chloride","Sulfur dioxide","Methylamine","Ethane"],"ans":3,"exp":"**Like dissolves like.** HCl, SO₂ and CH₃NH₂ are polar (and HCl and CH₃NH₂ also react with or hydrogen-bond to water), so they dissolve readily and leave the gas phase, lowering the pressure. Ethane is nonpolar, barely dissolves, and its pressure stays nearly the same.","how":{"ask":"Find the gas that stays in the gas phase: the one least soluble in water.","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: polar or hydrogen-bonding solutes dissolve in water; nonpolar ones barely do.\n• Gas pressure in a sealed flask falls only if gas molecules leave the gas phase, for example by dissolving.\n• Hydrocarbons (C–H and C–C bonds only) are nonpolar.","steps":[{"text":"The pressure falls only when gas dissolves, so find the least water-soluble gas.","hl":"gas pressure fall the least"},{"text":"Classify each gas by polarity.","calc":["HCl: polar, ionizes in water","SO₂: bent, polar","CH₃NH₂: polar, N–H hydrogen bonds","C₂H₆: only C–H and C–C bonds, nonpolar"]},{"text":"The polar gases dissolve readily, so their pressures drop sharply.","strike":["Hydrogen chloride","Sulfur dioxide","Methylamine"]},{"text":"Answer: Ethane."}],"trap":"Thinking only gases that ionize dissolve well: a polar gas like SO₂ dissolves readily, while a nonpolar gas barely dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sealed flasks","note":"gas can only leave by dissolving"},{"t":"small volume of water","note":"a polar solvent"},{"t":"fall the least","note":"find the least soluble gas"}]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water is polar and hydrogen-bonds, so it holds polar and hydrogen-bonding molecules and excludes nonpolar ones."},{"text":"**Link solubility to pressure.** Dissolved gas no longer pushes on the walls; a nonpolar gas stays in the gas phase, so its pressure (and the container) is unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** for saturated fatty acids, each has one polar –COOH head and a nonpolar tail. The **shortest chain** is the least hydrophobic, so it is the most soluble in water.","calc":["butanoic acid (4 C) > octanoic acid (8 C) > stearic acid (18 C)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** like dissolves like: the more nonpolar the molecule (a nonpolar gas, a longer hydrocarbon chain), the less it dissolves in water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-21a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:21#1","pe4-cp:21#2","pe4-cp:21#3","pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2","pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"q":"Drug Q's lethal dose is 60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells; in healthy cells it is 50 μM alone and 48 μM with R. Which conclusion do the data best support?","choices":["R sensitizes only the leukemia cells.","R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike.","R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."],"ans":0,"exp":"Compare each cell type with and without R. Leukemia cells need **10-fold less** Q once R is added (60 ÷ 6), while healthy cells barely shift (50 ÷ 48 ≈ 1.04). A dose that falls for one cell type only means R is a **selective** sensitizer. R was never tested by itself, and Q kills leukemia cells alone at 60 μM.","how":{"ask":"For each cell type, how much does the dose of Q drop when R is added, and does it drop for both types or one?","know":"• A **sensitizer** lowers the dose of a drug needed for the same effect.\n• Compare **with vs without** the sensitizer within each cell type, then compare the cell types.\n• **Selective** = the dose falls for the target (cancer) cells but not for normal cells.\n• **Enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer** (a fold change, not a difference).\n• Reject any claim about a condition the data never tested.","steps":[{"text":"Lay out the four doses as a small table: two cell types, each without and with R.","hl":"60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Lethal dose of drug Q, without and with compound R","x":{"label":"Cell type"},"y":{"label":"Lethal dose of Q (μM)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"categories":["Leukemia","Healthy"],"series":[{"name":"Q alone","values":[60,50]},{"name":"Q + R","values":[6,48]}]}},{"text":"Find the fold change for each cell type: dose alone ÷ dose with R.","calc":["leukemia: 60 μM ÷ 6 μM = **10-fold**","healthy: 50 μM ÷ 48 μM ≈ **1.04-fold** (essentially no change)"]},{"text":"R helps Q only in the leukemia cells, so it does not act on both types equally.","strike":["R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike."]},{"text":"No row gives R by itself, so its own toxicity is untested; and Q already kills leukemia cells at 60 μM without R.","strike":["R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."]},{"text":"Answer: R sensitizes only the leukemia cells."}],"trap":"Reading a claim the data never tested (R alone) as supported, or calling R a general sensitizer because some number moved slightly."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"60 μM alone","note":"leukemia, no R"},{"t":"6 μM with compound R","note":"leukemia, with R"},{"t":"50 μM alone","note":"healthy, no R"},{"t":"48 μM with R","note":"healthy, with R"}]},{"text":"**Compare within each cell type first.** Does adding R lower the dose?","calc":["leukemia: 60 → 6 μM (large drop)","healthy: 50 → 48 μM (tiny drop)"]},{"text":"**Selective means one type only.** The dose falls for the cancer cells and stays put for the normal cells, so the effect is selective."},{"text":"**Test only what was measured.** No condition gives R alone, so a claim about R killing cells by itself is unsupported either way."},{"text":"**Also:** enhancement is a **ratio**, dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer, never the difference. Compute it for every line and pick the largest.","calc":["line X: 90 → 30 μM: 90 ÷ 30 = 3-fold (drop 60 μM)","line Y: 8 → 0.5 μM: 8 ÷ 0.5 = 16-fold (drop 7.5 μM)","line Y is enhanced most, despite the smaller drop"]},{"text":"**Also:** order matters when a sensitizer blocks a pathway. Give the sensitizer **first**, long enough for it to act, then give the drug that works best once that pathway is blocked; giving the drug first or both together lets the pathway run while the drug is present."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare dose alone vs with the sensitizer in each cell type as a fold change (alone ÷ with); selective means only the cancer cells' dose falls, and claims about untested conditions are rejected."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-22a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:22#1","pe4-cp:22#2","pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2"],"q":"A blood test reports a peptide hormone at 640 nM, but the lab's software accepts only micromolar units. Which value should be entered?","choices":["0.064 μM","0.64 μM","64 μM","6.4 × 10⁵ μM"],"ans":1,"exp":"**nM = 10⁻⁹ M and μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, so 1 μM = 1000 nM. Going to the larger unit gives a smaller number: 640 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.64 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Write each prefix as a power of ten, then convert from nM to μM.","know":"• **Prefixes**: m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹, p = 10⁻¹².\n• 1 μM = 1000 nM, so **nM → μM: divide by 1000**.\n• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**; **M → μM: × 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the given value and unit.","hl":"640 nM"},{"text":"Convert through molar.","calc":["640 nM = 640 × 10⁻⁹ M = 6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M","6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M ÷ 10⁻⁶ M/μM = **0.64 μM**"]},{"text":"Shortcut: nM → μM is ÷ 1000.","calc":["640 ÷ 1000 = 0.64"]},{"text":"6.4 × 10⁵ μM multiplies by 1000 instead of dividing; 64 μM divides by only 10; 0.064 μM divides by 10⁴.","strike":["6.4 × 10⁵ μM","64 μM","0.064 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.64 μM."}],"trap":"Multiplying by 1000: a micromole is larger than a nanomole, so the number must get smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"640 nM","note":"value in nanomolar"},{"t":"micromolar","note":"target unit, μM"}]},{"text":"**Write the prefixes as powers of ten.** n = 10⁻⁹ and μ = 10⁻⁶: they differ by 10³."},{"text":"**Bigger unit, smaller number.** nM → μM: divide by 1000.","calc":["640 nM ÷ 1000 = 0.64 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** from pH to μM: [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM. At pH 6.3, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·³ M ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ M = 0.5 μM.","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M","5 × 10⁻⁷ M × 10⁶ μM/M = 0.5 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert through powers of ten: nM → μM ÷ 1000, M → μM × 10⁶, and [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-25a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:25#1","pe4-cp:25#2","pe4-cp:25#3","pe4-cp:37#1","pe4-cp:37#2","pe4-cp:37#3","pe4-cp:37#4"],"q":"A molecule contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups. How many π bonds does it contain?","choices":["5","7","9","11"],"ans":2,"exp":"Any pair of bonded atoms shares exactly one **σ bond**; every extra bond joining that pair is a **π bond**. So the C=O gives 1 π, the C=C 1 π, each C≡N 2 π, and the benzene ring (three C=C) 3 π: **3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**.","how":{"ask":"Give each multiple bond its π count, then add group by group.","know":"• **Single bond** = 1 σ, 0 π.\n• **Double bond** (C=O, C=C) = 1 σ + **1 π**.\n• **Triple bond** (C≡N, C≡C) = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• **Benzene ring** = three C=C = **3 π**.\n• More shared pairs pull atoms closer: a **double bond is shorter than a single bond**.","steps":[{"text":"List the groups with multiple bonds.","hl":"one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups"},{"text":"The ketone C=O is a double bond: one σ bond holds the atoms, and the second bond is π.","calc":["C=O = 1 σ + 1 π → 1 π"]},{"text":"Each C≡N is a triple bond: one σ and two π.","calc":["2 × C≡N = 2 × 2 π = 4 π"]},{"text":"The ring has three C=C (3 π), and the extra C=C has 1 π. Add everything.","calc":["3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 4 (C≡N) = 9 π"]},{"text":"Counting each C≡N as only 1 π gives 7; also counting the ring as a single π gives 5; counting all three lines of each triple bond as π gives 11.","calc":["3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7","1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5","3 + 1 + 1 + 6 = 11"],"strike":["7","5","11"]},{"text":"**Also:** the π bond adds a second shared pair, so C=O (about 120 pm) is shorter than a C–O single bond (about 140 pm)."},{"text":"Answer: 9."}],"trap":"Counting a triple bond as one π bond: only its first bond is σ, so the other two are both π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"one benzene ring","note":"three C=C"},{"t":"one ketone C=O","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"two C≡N groups","note":"two triple bonds"},{"t":"π bonds","note":"count only the extra bonds"}]},{"text":"**σ first, then π.** Between two atoms the first bond is always σ (head-on overlap); every extra bond is π (side-by-side p overlap).","calc":["double = 1 σ + 1 π","triple = 1 σ + 2 π"]},{"text":"**A carbonyl** C=O is one σ plus one π, not two σ bonds and not a lone π bond."},{"text":"**Count by group.** Benzene ring 3 π; each C=O or C=C 1 π; each C≡N or C≡C 2 π. Then add.","calc":["ring 3 + C=O 1 + C=C 1 + C≡N 2 + C≡N 2 = 9"]},{"text":"**Also:** more bonds between the same two atoms means a shorter, stronger link: triple < double < single in length."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** every double bond has one π and every triple bond two, a benzene ring has three; add them group by group, and remember a double bond is shorter than a single one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-26a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:26#1","pe4-cp:26#2"],"q":"Four neutral atoms have the ground-state configurations 1s²2s²2p⁶, [Ne]3s²3p⁵, [Ne]3s² and [Ar]4s¹. Which element releases the most energy on accepting an electron?","choices":["Neon","Chlorine","Magnesium","Potassium"],"ans":1,"exp":"Chlorine **needs just one more electron to fill its p subshell** (3p⁵ → 3p⁶), so the added electron completes a noble-gas configuration: halogens have the most negative electron affinities. Neon's shell is full, and the metals magnesium and potassium tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom whose configuration is completed, not disrupted, by one added electron.","know":"• **Electron affinity**: the energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron; more negative = gained more readily.\n• **Halogens** (ns²np⁵) need one electron to fill the p subshell, so they have the most favorable electron affinities.\n• **Noble gases** must start a new shell; **metals** (few valence electrons) tend to lose electrons.","steps":[{"text":"Look at each valence configuration.","hl":"[Ne]3s²3p⁵","calc":["Cl: 3p⁵ + e⁻ → 3p⁶, filled"]},{"text":"Neon is already full: an added electron would go into a new, higher shell.","hl":"1s²2s²2p⁶","strike":["Neon"]},{"text":"Magnesium (3s², filled s) and potassium (4s¹) are metals that lose valence electrons to reach a noble-gas core.","strike":["Magnesium","Potassium"]},{"text":"Answer: Chlorine."}],"trap":"Choosing potassium because its 4s orbital has room for one more: metals give up electrons far more readily than they take them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3p⁵","note":"one short of a filled p subshell"},{"t":"1s²2s²2p⁶","note":"already full"},{"t":"releases the most energy","note":"most negative electron affinity"}]},{"text":"**Ask what the new electron completes.** An electron that fills a subshell (np⁵ → np⁶) is strongly held, so energy is released."},{"text":"**Rule out the full and the metallic.** A noble gas has no room in its valence shell; Group 1 and 2 metals have small electron affinities and form cations."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** halogens (np⁵) gain an electron most readily; noble gases and metals do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-27a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:27#1","pe4-cp:27#2","pe4-cp:27#3","pe4-cp:27#4"],"q":"Compound Z's triple point is 0.40 atm and 15 °C. A sample sits on a phase boundary at 0.10 atm and −10 °C. Which process occurs there?","choices":["Sublimation","Melting","Vaporization","Condensation"],"ans":0,"exp":"The sample is colder and at lower pressure than the triple point, so the boundary it sits on is the **solid–gas line**. Solid and gas coexist there, and the solid turns straight to vapor: **sublimation**.","how":{"ask":"Which boundary line runs below and to the left of the triple point, and which two phases does it separate?","know":"• A **phase diagram** plots pressure (up) against temperature (across). Solid sits at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid in between.\n• Each line is where two phases coexist: **solid–gas = sublimation/deposition**, **solid–liquid = melting/freezing**, **liquid–gas = boiling/condensation**.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet; the **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the sample with the triple point.","hl":"0.10 atm and −10 °C","calc":["0.10 atm < 0.40 atm (lower pressure)","−10 °C < 15 °C (colder)"]},{"text":"Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so the only boundary there is the solid–gas line.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"Melting lies on the solid–liquid line and vaporization on the liquid–gas line; both start at the triple point and run above 0.40 atm.","strike":["Melting","Vaporization"]},{"text":"Condensation (gas → liquid) also needs the liquid–gas line, which the sample is not on.","strike":["Condensation"]},{"text":"Answer: sublimation."}],"trap":"Melting is what most solids do when warmed, but below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid region, so the solid goes straight to gas."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"triple point","note":"where all three lines meet"},{"t":"0.40 atm and 15 °C","note":"triple-point P and T"},{"t":"0.10 atm and −10 °C","note":"the sample"},{"t":"phase boundary","note":"on a line: two phases coexist"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** up = more pressure, right = hotter. Cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; liquid is the wedge in between.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"**Place the sample:** it is below and left of the triple point, so it sits on the solid–gas line. Crossing it = **sublimation** (solid → gas) or deposition (gas → solid).","calc":["0.10 < 0.40 atm and −10 < 15 °C → solid–gas line"]},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the solid–liquid line (nearly vertical, above the triple point) is **melting** or freezing."},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the liquid–gas line is **boiling** (vaporization) or condensation; at 1 atm it gives the normal boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** two points are special. The **triple point** is the one T and P where solid, liquid and gas all coexist. The **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find which line the point sits on and name that border: solid–gas = sublimation, solid–liquid = melting, liquid–gas = boiling; the triple and critical points are single special points, not lines."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-28a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:28#1"],"q":"Electron levels: E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV. Which downward jump emits the longest-wavelength photon?","choices":["E₄ → E₃","E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"],"ans":0,"exp":"**A photon carries the energy gap, and λ = hc/ΔE**, so the smallest gap gives the longest wavelength. The gaps are 0.5 eV (E₄ → E₃), 1.5 eV (E₃ → E₂), 5.0 eV (E₂ → E₁) and 7.0 eV (E₄ → E₁); the 0.5 eV drop emits light near 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm, the longest.","how":{"ask":"Find the energy gap of each transition and pick the smallest one.","know":"• An emitted photon's energy equals the gap: **ΔE = hc/λ**, so **λ = hc/ΔE**.\n• Wavelength is inversely proportional to the gap: **smallest ΔE → longest λ**.\n• Shortcut: λ(nm) ≈ 1240/ΔE(eV).","steps":[{"text":"List the levels.","hl":"E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV"},{"text":"Compute each choice's gap.","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = **0.5 eV**","E₃ → E₂: −5.5 − (−7.0) = 1.5 eV","E₂ → E₁: −7.0 − (−12.0) = 5.0 eV","E₄ → E₁: −5.0 − (−12.0) = 7.0 eV"]},{"text":"λ = hc/ΔE, so convert the smallest and largest gaps to wavelengths.","calc":["0.5 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm","7.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 7.0 ≈ 177 nm"]},{"text":"The three larger gaps all give shorter wavelengths; E₄ → E₁, the largest gap, gives the shortest.","strike":["E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: E₄ → E₃."}],"trap":"Picking the biggest drop: a large gap means a high-energy photon, which has the shortest wavelength, not the longest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₁ = −12.0 eV","note":"lowest level"},{"t":"E₄ = −5.0 eV","note":"highest level"},{"t":"longest-wavelength photon","note":"smallest gap"}]},{"text":"**The photon carries the gap.** A falling electron emits one photon with ΔE = E(upper) − E(lower).","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = 0.5 eV"]},{"text":"**Flip to wavelength:** λ = hc/ΔE, so wavelength and gap are inversely related.","calc":["λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ ΔE(eV)","1.5 eV → ≈ 830 nm; 0.5 eV → ≈ 2500 nm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = hc/ΔE, so the transition with the smallest energy gap emits the longest wavelength, and the largest gap the shortest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-29a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:29#1","pe4-cp:29#2","pe4-cp:29#3"],"q":"The ester CH₃CH₂–NH–CH₂CH₂–C(=O)–OCH₃ contains one nitrogen atom. Which functional group does that nitrogen belong to?","choices":["Primary amine","Secondary amine","Secondary amide","Carbamate"],"ans":1,"exp":"The N is bonded to two carbons (the ethyl CH₂ and a CH₂) and one H. **Neither carbon is a C=O carbon**, so it is an **amine**, and two attached carbons make it **secondary**. The carbonyl is two carbons away and belongs to the ester, not to the nitrogen.","how":{"ask":"List the atoms bonded directly to N, check whether any is a C=O carbon, then count the carbons.","know":"• **Amine**: N bonded only to C and H, with no C=O carbon on N; **1°, 2°, 3°** = one, two, three carbons on N.\n• **Amide**: N–C(=O). **Imine**: C=N. **Carbamate**: N–C(=O)–O.\n• Only the atoms directly on N count; a carbonyl elsewhere in the molecule does not.","steps":[{"text":"Find the nitrogen and its direct neighbors.","hl":"CH₃CH₂–NH–CH₂CH₂","calc":["N neighbors: CH₂ (ethyl), CH₂ (chain), H"]},{"text":"Is either carbon a carbonyl carbon? No: the C=O is two carbons away, so this is not an amide or a carbamate.","hl":"C(=O)–OCH₃","strike":["Secondary amide","Carbamate"]},{"text":"Count the carbons on N: two, so it is secondary, not primary.","calc":["carbons on N = 2 → 2° amine"],"strike":["Primary amine"]},{"text":"Answer: Secondary amine."}],"trap":"Seeing a C=O somewhere in the molecule and calling the N an amide: the C=O must be bonded to the N itself."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"–NH–","note":"the nitrogen: two bonds to C, one to H"},{"t":"C(=O)–OCH₃","note":"a carbonyl, but on the ester"},{"t":"Which functional group","note":"classify by N's neighbors"}]},{"text":"**Amine or not?** Look only at the atoms bonded to N. If they are all C (none a C=O carbon) and H, it is an amine."},{"text":"**Count the carbons on N.** One carbon: 1° (R–NH₂). Two: 2° (R₂NH). Three: 3° (R₃N)."},{"text":"**Also:** the other nitrogen groups: **amide** N–C(=O), as in CH₃C(=O)NHCH₃; **imine** C=N, as in (CH₃)₂C=NCH₃; **carbamate** N–C(=O)–O, as in CH₃NHC(=O)OCH₃."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** classify an N by its direct neighbors: C=O on N → amide (plus O on that carbon → carbamate), C=N → imine, only C and H → amine, 1°/2°/3° by the number of carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-30a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:30#1","pe4-cp:30#2"],"q":"Methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, is treated with NaBH₄ and then warmed with a trace of acid. Which organic product is isolated?","choices":["γ-Valerolactone","δ-Valerolactone","Pentane-1,4-diol","Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"],"ans":0,"exp":"NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor: it reduces the **ketone** at C4 to a secondary alcohol but leaves the **ester** alone. The new OH sits on the γ-carbon of the ester, so it attacks the ester carbonyl in the same molecule, expels **methanol** and closes a ring of C1–C4 plus the O: five atoms, **γ-valerolactone**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which carbonyl NaBH₄ reduces, then count the ring formed when the new OH attacks the ester.","know":"• **NaBH₄** reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols; it does **not** reduce esters (that needs LiAlH₄).\n• A **γ-hydroxy ester** (OH on the γ-carbon, C4, two carbons past the α-carbon) cyclizes to a **five-membered lactone**: count the ring as the carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O. The ester's alcohol part (here methanol) leaves.","steps":[{"text":"Number from the ester carbon: C1 is the CO₂CH₃ carbon, C4 the ketone carbon.","hl":"CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃","calc":["C5H₃–C4(=O)–C3H₂–C2H₂–C1(=O)OCH₃"]},{"text":"NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone, so the ester survives. A diol would need the ester reduced too, which takes LiAlH₄.","hl":"NaBH₄","calc":["C4=O → C4H–OH"],"strike":["Pentane-1,4-diol"]},{"text":"The C4 OH is γ to the ester carbonyl. With acid and warmth it attacks C1 and methanol leaves, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive.","hl":"warmed with a trace of acid","strike":["Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"]},{"text":"Count the ring atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4 and the O.","calc":["ring = 4 C + 1 O = 5 atoms → γ-lactone"]},{"text":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5.","strike":["δ-Valerolactone"]},{"text":"Answer: γ-Valerolactone."}],"trap":"Letting NaBH₄ reduce the ester as well: it is too mild, so the ester stays to be attacked by the new OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4-oxopentanoate","note":"ketone at C4 of an ester"},{"t":"NaBH₄","note":"reduces the ketone only"},{"t":"warmed with a trace of acid","note":"ring closure"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: what does the reducing agent touch?** NaBH₄ turns a C=O of an aldehyde or ketone into C–OH and leaves esters, acids and amides unchanged.","calc":["R₂C=O → R₂CH–OH; RCO₂R′ unchanged"]},{"text":"**Step 2: find the hydroxy ester.** Locate the new OH relative to the ester carbon: on the γ-carbon (C4) or the δ-carbon (C5)."},{"text":"**Step 3: cyclize.** The OH attacks the ester C=O inside the molecule, the OR′ group leaves as R′OH, and a lactone forms. Ring size = carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O.","calc":["γ: 4 C + O = 5-membered"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NaBH₄ reduces a ketone to an alcohol but leaves an ester; a γ- or δ-hydroxy ester then closes to a five- or six-membered lactone and releases the ester's alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-31a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:31#1"],"q":"A compound has two ethyl ester groups and one six-membered lactone ring. After heating with excess aqueous KOH, how many carboxylate groups does the product carry?","choices":["0","1","2","3"],"ans":3,"exp":"Hot excess hydroxide **saponifies** every ester. Each ethyl ester becomes a carboxylate and releases ethanol, and the lactone, being a cyclic ester, opens to a carboxylate with an alcohol (–OH) on the other end. That makes **2 + 1 = 3** carboxylate groups.","how":{"ask":"Count every ester linkage, the lactone included, because each one becomes a carboxylate in excess base.","know":"• **Saponification**: RCO₂R′ + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + R′OH, irreversible because the carboxylate is deprotonated.\n• A **lactone is a cyclic ester**: hydroxide opens it to a carboxylate plus an alcohol on the same chain.\n• **Excess** base means every ester group reacts.","steps":[{"text":"Excess hydroxide with heat hydrolyzes every ester group present, so the answer is not zero.","hl":"heating with excess aqueous KOH","strike":["0"]},{"text":"Each ethyl ester gives a carboxylate and ethanol.","calc":["2 × (RCO₂Et + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + EtOH) = 2 carboxylates"]},{"text":"The lactone is also an ester, just in a ring: it opens to one carboxylate and one alcohol on the same chain.","hl":"one six-membered lactone ring","calc":["lactone + OH⁻ → HO–(CH₂)…–CO₂⁻ = 1 carboxylate"]},{"text":"Add them. Stopping at 1 treats only the ring as reactive; stopping at 2 forgets that the lactone is an ester.","calc":["2 + 1 = 3"],"strike":["1","2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3."}],"trap":"Leaving the lactone intact: it looks like a ring, not an ester, but it is an ester and hydroxide opens it just as easily."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ethyl ester groups","note":"two esters"},{"t":"lactone","note":"a cyclic ester"},{"t":"excess aqueous KOH","note":"saponification of all"}]},{"text":"**Saponification**: hydroxide adds to the ester C=O, the alkoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate, so the reaction does not reverse.","calc":["RCO₂R′ + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + R′OH"]},{"text":"**Lactones count.** A lactone is an ester whose alcohol part is on the same chain; opening it gives a hydroxy carboxylate (one carboxylate per lactone)."},{"text":"**Count.** With excess base, carboxylates = number of ester groups + number of lactone rings.","calc":["2 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** excess hydroxide hydrolyzes every ester and every lactone, each one ending as a carboxylate plus its alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-32a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:32#1","pe4-cp:32#2"],"q":"Piperidine hydrochloride is stirred with aqueous NaOH before benzyl bromide is added for an SN2 reaction. Why is the base needed?","choices":["It removes H⁺ from the ammonium ion","It makes benzyl bromide more electrophilic","It turns piperidine into an amide anion","It converts bromide into a nucleophile"],"ans":0,"exp":"In the hydrochloride salt the nitrogen is a **piperidinium ion**: its lone pair already holds a proton, leaving nothing to attack carbon with. NaOH takes that H⁺ off, giving **neutral piperidine**, whose free lone pair attacks the benzylic carbon and displaces bromide. Hydroxide is far too weak to remove a second H from N.","how":{"ask":"Ask which form of the amine has a lone pair free to attack the C–Br carbon.","know":"• An **ammonium salt** (R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻) has **no lone pair** on N, so it is **not a nucleophile**.\n• A base stronger than the amine removes H⁺ and frees the **neutral amine**, whose lone pair is nucleophilic.\n• Removing H⁺ from a neutral amine (pKa about 36) needs a far stronger base than hydroxide.","steps":[{"text":"The starting material is a salt: the N carries two H's and a positive charge, with no lone pair.","hl":"Piperidine hydrochloride","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻: 4 bonds on N, 0 lone pairs"]},{"text":"Hydroxide removes one H⁺, freeing the amine.","hl":"aqueous NaOH","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻ → R₂NH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Only the neutral amine has a lone pair, so only it can attack the benzylic carbon and push out bromide.","calc":["R₂NH + PhCH₂Br → R₂N⁺H–CH₂Ph Br⁻"]},{"text":"The base does not touch the electrophile, and bromide is the leaving group, not the nucleophile.","strike":["It makes benzyl bromide more electrophilic","It converts bromide into a nucleophile"]},{"text":"An amide anion would need a base far stronger than hydroxide, since a neutral N–H has a pKa near 36.","strike":["It turns piperidine into an amide anion"]},{"text":"Answer: It removes H⁺ from the ammonium ion."}],"trap":"Thinking the salt is already a nucleophile because it contains nitrogen: until the H⁺ is removed, the nitrogen has no lone pair to donate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Piperidine hydrochloride","note":"an ammonium salt"},{"t":"aqueous NaOH","note":"base"},{"t":"SN2 reaction","note":"needs a nucleophile with a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Salt vs free amine.** An amine \"hydrochloride\" is R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻: the lone pair on N has been used to bond H⁺.","calc":["R₂NH + HCl → R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**The base frees it.** A base whose conjugate acid is weaker than the ammonium ion takes the H⁺ back.","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻ → R₂NH + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Only the free amine is a nucleophile.** Its lone pair attacks the carbon bearing the leaving group in the SN2 step."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a base removes H⁺ from an ammonium salt to free the amine, and only that free amine, with its lone pair, can act as the nucleophile."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-33a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:33#1","pe4-cp:33#2"],"q":"An amine protected as an ethyl carbamate, R–NH–CO₂Et, goes through four steps: (I) NaBH₄, EtOH, 0 °C; (II) TsCl, pyridine; (III) NaCN, DMSO; (IV) KOH, H₂O, reflux. In which step is the carbamate cleaved?","choices":["Step I","Step II","Step III","Step IV"],"ans":3,"exp":"A carbamate is a stable amide-like ester: breaking it means **hydrolysis**, which needs **strong aqueous base or acid and heat**. Only the KOH/water/reflux step supplies all three, so that is where the carbamate is cut. The other steps are a mild reduction, a tosylation and an SN2 substitution.","how":{"ask":"Find the step that has water, a strong acid or base, and heat: that is what carbamate hydrolysis needs.","know":"• A **carbamate** (R₂N–CO–OR′) is cleaved by **hydrolysis**: strong base (NaOH, KOH) or strong acid (HCl) in **water**, with **heat** (reflux).\n• Products: amine + CO₂ + R′OH.\n• Mild reagents (NaBH₄), dry conditions or room temperature leave it intact.","steps":[{"text":"Look for the hydrolysis conditions in each step.","hl":"(IV) KOH, H₂O, reflux"},{"text":"Step I: NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor at 0 °C; it does not reduce carbamates, esters or amides.","hl":"(I) NaBH₄, EtOH, 0 °C","strike":["Step I"]},{"text":"Step II: TsCl in pyridine converts an OH into a tosylate. There is no water and no strong base, so nothing is hydrolyzed.","strike":["Step II"]},{"text":"Step III: cyanide displaces the tosylate by SN2 in dry DMSO; with no water there is nothing to hydrolyze it.","strike":["Step III"]},{"text":"Step IV has strong base, water and heat: the carbamate is hydrolyzed.","calc":["R–NH–CO₂Et + KOH/H₂O, Δ → R–NH₂ + CO₃²⁻ + EtOH"]},{"text":"Answer: Step IV."}],"trap":"Choosing the reduction step because NaBH₄ attacks C=O groups: it reduces aldehydes and ketones, not carbamates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ethyl carbamate","note":"amide + ester on one C=O"},{"t":"KOH, H₂O, reflux","note":"strong base, water, heat"},{"t":"cleaved","note":"hydrolysis"}]},{"text":"**What breaks a carbamate?** Like an amide, it is resonance-stabilized and slow to react, so it needs **hydrolysis under forcing conditions**: strong acid or base, water and heat.","calc":["R₂N–CO₂R′ + H₂O (acid or base, Δ) → R₂NH + CO₂ + R′OH"]},{"text":"**Scan each step for all three**: water present? strong acid or base? heat? A step missing any one leaves the carbamate in place."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbamate is cleaved only by hydrolysis with strong base or acid and heat, so pick the step that supplies those conditions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-34a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:34#1","pe4-cp:34#2","pe4-cp:34#3","pe4-cp:34#4","pe4-cp:34#5"],"q":"With an inhibitor added, an enzyme's Lineweaver–Burk plot keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM, but its x-intercept moves from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹. Which type of inhibitor is it?","choices":["Competitive","Noncompetitive","Uncompetitive","Mixed"],"ans":0,"exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. The y-intercept stays at 0.25, so **Vmax stays 4 μM/s**. The x-intercept moves toward zero, so **KM rises from 2 to 8 μM**. Same Vmax with a larger apparent KM is the signature of competitive inhibition: the lines cross on the y-axis.","how":{"ask":"Translate each intercept into Vmax or KM, then ask which of the two changed.","know":"• Lineweaver–Burk: 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax.\n• **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**; **x-intercept = −1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: Vmax same, KM up (lines cross on the y-axis).\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same (lines cross on the x-axis).\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed (lines cross off both axes).\n• Uncompetitive: Vmax and KM both fall by the same factor (parallel lines).","steps":[{"text":"The y-intercept gives Vmax; it did not move.","hl":"keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","calc":["Vmax = 1 ÷ 0.25 s/μM = **4 μM/s** (with and without inhibitor)"]},{"text":"The x-intercept gives KM; it moved toward zero.","hl":"from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹","calc":["−1/KM = −0.50 μM⁻¹ → KM = **2 μM**","−1/KM = −0.125 μM⁻¹ → KM = **8 μM**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Lineweaver–Burk plot with and without the inhibitor","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.5,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.5,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,0.5],[1,0.75]]},{"name":"+ Inhibitor","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.125,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,1.25],[1,2.25]]}]}},{"text":"Noncompetitive and mixed inhibitors both lower Vmax, which would raise the y-intercept; here it is unchanged.","strike":["Noncompetitive","Mixed"]},{"text":"An uncompetitive inhibitor lowers both Vmax and KM, giving a parallel line with a higher y-intercept; here KM rose.","strike":["Uncompetitive"]},{"text":"Answer: competitive."}],"trap":"Mixing up the axes: the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so an x-intercept closer to zero means a larger KM, not a smaller one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which intercept it belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","note":"1/Vmax"},{"t":"x-intercept","note":"−1/KM"},{"t":"−0.50 to −0.125","note":"moves toward zero"}]},{"text":"**Read the intercepts.** y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM.","calc":["Vmax = 1/0.25 = 4 μM/s, both lines","KM: 1/0.50 = 2 μM → 1/0.125 = 8 μM"]},{"text":"**Competitive:** the inhibitor competes for the active site, so enough substrate wins it back: Vmax is unchanged and KM rises. The lines meet on the y-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** noncompetitive inhibition lowers Vmax with KM unchanged: the y-intercept rises and the lines meet on the x-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** mixed inhibition lowers Vmax and changes KM: the lines meet away from both axes."},{"text":"**Also:** uncompetitive inhibition lowers both Vmax and KM by the same factor: the lines are parallel (same slope, higher intercepts)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = −1/KM; competitive keeps Vmax and raises KM, noncompetitive lowers Vmax only, mixed lowers Vmax and shifts KM, uncompetitive lowers both."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-35a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:35#1","pe4-cp:35#2","pe4-cp:35#3"],"q":"Lactate dehydrogenase uses NADH and pyruvate as its two substrates. Which finding would show that it follows an ordered sequential mechanism?","choices":["Pyruvate binds only to the enzyme–NADH complex","Either substrate can bind the free enzyme first","A product is released prior to pyruvate binding","Pyruvate binds the free enzyme tightly"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Ordered sequential**: one substrate must bind first, and both are on the enzyme before any product forms. If pyruvate can bind only the enzyme–NADH complex, NADH is the obligatory first substrate. Either-first binding is **random**; a product leaving before the second substrate arrives is **ping-pong**; pyruvate binding the free enzyme doesn't show a required order; it is consistent with random binding or with pyruvate binding first.","how":{"ask":"What does ordered mean for which substrate can bind the free enzyme?","know":"• **Sequential** mechanisms: both substrates bind (a ternary complex) before any product leaves.\n• **Ordered**: one substrate must bind first; the second binds only to the enzyme–first-substrate complex.\n• **Random**: either substrate can bind first.\n• **Ping-pong**: the first substrate binds and a product leaves (leaving a modified enzyme) before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"Ordered means a required binding order: the second substrate cannot bind free enzyme.","hl":"ordered sequential mechanism"},{"text":"If pyruvate binds only once NADH is on, NADH must come first: that is the ordered pattern.","hl":"ordered sequential mechanism"},{"text":"Either substrate binding first describes a random mechanism.","strike":["Either substrate can bind the free enzyme first"]},{"text":"A product leaving before the second substrate binds describes ping-pong, not sequential.","strike":["A product is released prior to pyruvate binding"]},{"text":"Pyruvate binding the free enzyme doesn't show a required order; it is consistent with random binding or with pyruvate binding first.","strike":["Pyruvate binds the free enzyme tightly"]},{"text":"Answer: pyruvate binds only to the enzyme–NADH complex."}],"trap":"Thinking any two-step binding is ping-pong: ping-pong needs a product to leave between the two substrates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two substrates","note":"a bisubstrate enzyme"},{"t":"ordered sequential","note":"fixed binding order, no early product"}]},{"text":"**Ordered:** substrate 1 binds free enzyme; substrate 2 binds only the E·S1 complex; products leave after both are bound.","calc":["E + NADH → E·NADH","E·NADH + pyruvate → E·NADH·pyruvate → products"]},{"text":"**Also:** random sequential means either substrate can bind the free enzyme first; both still bind before any product leaves."},{"text":"**Also:** ping-pong (double displacement) means substrate 1 binds, a first product leaves and the enzyme is left modified, and only then does substrate 2 bind.","calc":["E + S1 → E′ + P1","E′ + S2 → E + P2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ordered = one substrate must bind first; random = either can bind first; ping-pong = a product leaves before the second substrate binds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-38a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:38#1","pe4-cp:38#2","pe4-cp:39#1","pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"q":"A dye solution in a 0.40 cm cell absorbs A = 0.63 at 430 nm. Its molar absorptivity is 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm and 7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm. What is its concentration?","choices":["12 μM","30 μM","75 μM","225 μM"],"ans":2,"exp":"**Beer's law, A = εbc, so c = A/(εb).** Use the ε at the measured wavelength (430 nm): c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40) = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = **75 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange Beer's law for c, using the ε that matches the wavelength of the reading.","know":"• **Beer's law: A = εbc** (ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹, b = path length in cm, c in M), so **c = A/(εb)**.\n• ε depends on wavelength: use the value at the wavelength where A was read.\n• A blank cancels everything in the beam only if the reference and sample cells are identical except for the analyte.","steps":[{"text":"The reading was taken at 430 nm, so use ε at 430 nm and ignore the 550 nm value.","hl":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm"},{"text":"Rearrange Beer's law and substitute, keeping the path length.","hl":"0.40 cm cell","calc":["A = εbc → c = A ÷ (εb)","εb = 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ × 0.40 cm = 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹","c = 0.63 ÷ 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹ = **7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M**","7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **75 μM**"]},{"text":"225 μM uses the 550 nm absorptivity (0.63 ÷ 2800). 30 μM drops the path length (0.63 ÷ 2.1 × 10⁴). 12 μM multiplies by b instead of dividing.","strike":["225 μM","30 μM","12 μM"]},{"text":"If A were too high for the detector, dilute the sample or use a shorter cell: A = εbc falls in proportion to c or b.","calc":["halve c → A halves","halve b → A halves"]},{"text":"Answer: 75 μM."}],"trap":"Grabbing the other wavelength's ε, or forgetting to divide by a path length that is not 1 cm."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 cm cell","note":"path length b"},{"t":"A = 0.63 at 430 nm","note":"absorbance, read at 430 nm"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm","note":"the ε to use"},{"t":"7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm","note":"wrong wavelength: a distractor"}]},{"text":"**Beer's law:** absorbance grows with ε, path and concentration. Solve for c.","calc":["A = εbc","c = A ÷ (εb)"]},{"text":"**Match the wavelength.** ε is a property of the compound at one wavelength; the reading was at 430 nm.","calc":["c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40)","c = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = 75 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** a reading above the detector's linear range is fixed by lowering A: dilute the sample (smaller c) or use a shorter path (smaller b). A longer cell makes A larger, not smaller.","calc":["A = εbc: dilute 3-fold → A ÷ 3","1.0 cm → 0.20 cm cell → A ÷ 5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the reference (blank) and sample cells must differ only in the analyte, so the two readings can be compared. A cell's own absorbance (a scratch, a different glass) would add to the sample reading.","calc":["A(read) = A(analyte) + A(cell difference)","cell difference 0.10 → reading too high by 0.10"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A/(εb) with ε at the measured wavelength; lower a too-high A by diluting or shortening the path, and blank with an identical cell."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:41#1","pe4-cp:41#2"],"q":"A phosphatase cleaves glucose 6-phosphate through a covalent intermediate formed by an active-site aspartate. Which substrate atom does the aspartate oxygen attack?","choices":["Phosphorus","Bridging ester oxygen","Glucose C-6 carbon","Terminal phosphoryl oxygen"],"ans":0,"exp":"The aspartate carboxylate oxygen is a **nucleophile**, so it bonds to the **electrophile**. Phosphorus carries four electronegative oxygens and is strongly δ+, so the Asp O attacks **P**. The P–O bond to glucose breaks, glucose leaves, and the phosphoryl group now sits on the enzyme as an **aspartyl phosphate**, later hydrolyzed by water.","how":{"ask":"The attacking oxygen has lone pairs; which atom of the phosphate ester is electron-poor enough to accept them?","know":"• A nucleophile (lone pair: Asp carboxylate O, His imidazole N, Ser/Cys) attacks an **electrophilic** atom.\n• In a phosphate ester R–O–PO₃²⁻, the **P** is bonded to four O atoms and is strongly δ+.\n• Oxygens are electron-rich: a nucleophile does not attack them.\n• Attack at P releases R–OH and leaves the **phosphoryl group on the enzyme** (phospho-Asp or phospho-His), which water then hydrolyzes.","steps":[{"text":"The aspartate oxygen is the nucleophile in this step.","hl":"aspartate oxygen attack","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Find the electron-poor atom: P, pulled on by four oxygens.","calc":["P bonded to 4 O → strongly δ+ → the electrophile","Asp–COO⁻ + Glc–O–PO₃²⁻ → Asp–C(=O)O–PO₃²⁻ + Glc–O⁻"]},{"text":"Oxygen atoms are electron-rich, so the nucleophile does not attack the bridging or the terminal oxygen.","strike":["Bridging ester oxygen","Terminal phosphoryl oxygen"]},{"text":"Attack at C-6 would break the C–O bond and put glucose, not phosphate, on the enzyme; phosphatases break the P–O bond.","strike":["Glucose C-6 carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: phosphorus."}],"trap":"Picking an oxygen because the bond that breaks is a P–O bond: the nucleophile forms its new bond to P, and the bridging O leaves with glucose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"covalent intermediate","note":"enzyme bonded to part of the substrate"},{"t":"aspartate","note":"carboxylate O nucleophile"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"a phosphate monoester"}]},{"text":"**Nucleophile meets electrophile.** Lone pairs go to the δ+ atom; in a phosphate ester, that is P."},{"text":"**Follow the phosphate.** The Asp O–P bond forms as the P–O(glucose) bond breaks, so the phosphoryl group is transferred to the enzyme.","calc":["E–Asp–O⁻ + Glc–OPO₃²⁻ → E–Asp–O–PO₃²⁻ + Glc–O⁻","E–Asp–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → E–Asp–O⁻ + HPO₄²⁻ + H⁺"]},{"text":"**Also:** when the nucleophile is a histidine N, the same attack on P gives a phosphohistidine intermediate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in phosphate-ester cleavage, the enzyme nucleophile (Asp O or His N) attacks the electrophilic phosphorus, never an oxygen, and the phosphate is transferred to the enzyme."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-43a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:43#1","pe4-cp:43#2","pe4-cp:43#3"],"q":"An enzyme (5.0 nM) gives: pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM. As pH rises from 6 to 8, how do kcat/KM and Vmax change?","choices":["kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**: 300/50 = 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 6 and 600/200 = 3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 8, so it **halves**. **Vmax = kcat[E]total** with the same enzyme amount: 1.5 μM/s rises to 3.0 μM/s, so it **doubles**. A faster kcat does not mean higher efficiency when KM grows even more.","how":{"ask":"Compute kcat/KM at each pH, then Vmax = kcat × [E]total at each pH.","know":"• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM** (units μM⁻¹s⁻¹ or M⁻¹s⁻¹); higher means better at low [S].\n• **Vmax = kcat[E]total**: with the same enzyme concentration, Vmax tracks kcat.\n• KM is the [S] at half Vmax; a larger KM means weaker apparent binding.\n• Read a table row by row: compute each quantity at each condition, then compare.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the values for each pH.","hl":"pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM","calc":["[E]total = 5.0 nM = 0.0050 μM"]},{"text":"Catalytic efficiency at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ ÷ 50 μM = **6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ ÷ 200 μM = **3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","6 → 3: **halves**"]},{"text":"Vmax at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **1.5 μM/s**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **3.0 μM/s**","1.5 → 3.0: **doubles**"]},{"text":"Efficiency falls, so it does not double or quadruple; Vmax follows kcat, so it neither stays the same nor halves.","strike":["kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"]},{"text":"Answer: kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles."}],"trap":"Judging efficiency from kcat alone: kcat doubled, but KM quadrupled, so kcat/KM fell."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 nM","note":"[E]total, same at both pH"},{"t":"kcat 300 s⁻¹","note":"pH 6 turnover"},{"t":"KM 50 μM","note":"pH 6"},{"t":"kcat 600 s⁻¹","note":"pH 8 turnover"},{"t":"KM 200 μM","note":"pH 8"}]},{"text":"**Efficiency is a ratio.** kcat/KM, so look at how both numbers change.","calc":["kcat × 2, KM × 4 → kcat/KM × 2/4 = × ½"]},{"text":"**Also:** Vmax = kcat[E]total, so at a fixed enzyme concentration Vmax changes by the same factor as kcat.","calc":["kcat × 2 → Vmax × 2"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a pH table, compute kcat/KM and Vmax in each row and state the direction of change as the pH rises; the two can move in opposite directions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM and Vmax = kcat[E]total; compute each at every condition in the table before comparing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-44a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:44#1"],"q":"A steel paper clip, far denser than water, can rest on top of still water if laid down gently. What mainly holds it up?","choices":["Surface tension","Buoyant force","Water viscosity","Atmospheric pressure"],"ans":0,"exp":"Water molecules at the surface are pulled inward and sideways by **cohesion** (hydrogen bonds), forming an elastic 'skin'. That **surface tension** resists being dented and supports light objects such as the clip or a water strider.","how":{"ask":"Find the property of the water surface that resists being stretched.","know":"• **Surface tension**: surface molecules have neighbors only below and beside them, so **cohesion** (hydrogen bonding) pulls them into a taut film.\n• The film can hold up small objects, such as a clip or an insect, that are too dense to float by buoyancy.","steps":[{"text":"Steel is far denser than water, so buoyancy alone would sink it.","hl":"far denser than water"},{"text":"It stays up only when laid down gently: the surface film dents but does not break.","hl":"laid down gently"},{"text":"Buoyancy can't hold a denser object; viscosity only resists flow; air presses on both sides equally.","strike":["Buoyant force","Water viscosity","Atmospheric pressure"]},{"text":"Answer: surface tension."}],"trap":"Choosing buoyancy: an object denser than water sinks unless the surface film holds it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"far denser than water","note":"buoyancy can't do it"},{"t":"rest on top of still water","note":"held at the surface"},{"t":"laid down gently","note":"the surface film must not break"}]},{"text":"**Cohesion makes a skin.** Hydrogen bonds pull surface water molecules toward each other and inward."},{"text":"**The skin resists stretching**, so a small light object dents it and is held up."},{"text":"**Also:** this is how water striders and other insects stand on water: their legs dimple the film without breaking it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** surface tension, from cohesion between water molecules at the surface, supports small objects and insects on water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-46a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:46#1"],"q":"In green laser light (532 nm), 1 μm dust specks cast shadows ringed by bright and dark circles. What causes the rings?","choices":["Diffraction","Refraction","Polarization","Dispersion"],"ans":0,"exp":"An obstacle **about the size of the wavelength** makes light **bend around its edges** into the shadow; where the bent waves overlap they reinforce or cancel, making rings of light and dark. That is **diffraction**.","how":{"ask":"Name the wave effect that makes fringes around an obstacle near the wavelength in size.","know":"• **Diffraction**: waves bend around an obstacle or through an opening; it is strongest when the size is comparable to the wavelength.\n• The bent waves overlap and **interfere**, making bright (constructive) and dark (destructive) fringes.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the speck to the wavelength: both are around a micrometer or less.","hl":"1 μm dust specks","calc":["532 nm = 0.532 μm","speck ≈ 1 μm ≈ 2 wavelengths"]},{"text":"Light bending around the speck's edge spreads into its shadow, and the waves interfere into rings.","hl":"bright and dark circles"},{"text":"Refraction needs light to pass into a new medium; polarization filters by orientation; dispersion splits colors, but a laser has one color.","strike":["Refraction","Polarization","Dispersion"]},{"text":"Answer: diffraction."}],"trap":"Choosing refraction because the light passes something small: fringes around a shadow are diffraction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"532 nm","note":"the wavelength"},{"t":"1 μm dust specks","note":"obstacle near the wavelength in size"},{"t":"bright and dark circles","note":"fringes"}]},{"text":"**Size versus wavelength.** When an obstacle is close to λ in size, light visibly bends around it.","calc":["1 μm ÷ 0.532 μm ≈ 2"]},{"text":"**Bending plus interference gives fringes.** Waves meeting in step make bright bands; out of step, dark bands."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bright and dark fringes around an obstacle (or slit) about the size of the wavelength mean diffraction: light bends around it and interferes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-48a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:48#1","pe4-cp:48#2","pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2","pe4-cp:51#3"],"q":"At physiological pH, which tautomer of guanine predominates, and how many hydrogen bonds does it form with cytosine in a Watson–Crick pair?","choices":["Keto; 3","Enol; 3","Keto; 2","Enol; 2"],"ans":0,"exp":"Under physiological conditions guanine, like thymine and uracil, is almost entirely in the **keto** form (C6=O with N1–H). Its pairing face is then acceptor, donor, donor (O6, N1–H, N2–H), matching cytosine's donor, acceptor, acceptor (N4–H, N3, O2): **three hydrogen bonds**. A·T pairs make only two.","how":{"ask":"Which tautomer has the C=O and ring N–H, and how many donor–acceptor matches line up across G and C?","know":"• **Tautomers** differ in where an H and a double bond sit: keto C=O / N–H versus enol C–OH / C=N.\n• At physiological pH, **G, T and U are mainly keto**; rare enol forms change the pairing face and cause mispairs.\n• A **hydrogen bond** forms where an H on N or O (donor) faces a lone-pair N or O (acceptor).\n• Normal pairs: **G·C has 3 H-bonds; A·T has 2.**","steps":[{"text":"The common form of guanine is the keto tautomer: C6=O and N1–H.","hl":"which tautomer of guanine predominates","fig":"guanine"},{"text":"Count donor–acceptor matches across the Watson–Crick face.","fig":"cytosine","calc":["G O6 (acceptor) ··· H–N4 of C (donor): 1","G N1–H (donor) ··· N3 of C (acceptor): 2","G N2–H (donor) ··· O2 of C (acceptor): 3"]},{"text":"The enol is the rare form, so both enol options fall.","strike":["Enol; 3","Enol; 2"]},{"text":"Two hydrogen bonds is the A·T count, not G·C.","strike":["Keto; 2"]},{"text":"Answer: keto; 3."}],"trap":"Thinking the enol is favored because it has an O–H: in water at pH 7, the keto (lactam) form dominates for G, T and U."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"physiological pH","note":"keto forms dominate"},{"t":"tautomer of guanine","note":"keto vs enol"},{"t":"Watson–Crick pair","note":"G·C"}]},{"text":"**Keto wins.** G, T and U sit mainly as keto (C=O, ring N–H) under physiological conditions; the enol forms are rare."},{"text":"**Count the H-bonds pair by pair.** An H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one hydrogen bond.","calc":["G·C: O6···H–N4, N1–H···N3, N2–H···O2 = 3","A·T: N6–H···O4, N1···H–N3 = 2"]},{"text":"**Also:** a rare enol tautomer mispairs. Enol guanine has O6–H (donor) and N1 (acceptor) in place of C6=O and N1–H, so its face reads donor, acceptor, donor and pairs with thymine instead of cytosine; against cytosine only one hydrogen bond lines up.","calc":["enol G O6–H ··· O4 of T","enol G N1 ··· H–N3 of T","enol G N2–H ··· O2 of T","O6–H faces H–N4: donor meets donor, no bond","N1 faces N3: acceptor meets acceptor, no bond","N2–H···O2: 1 bond, so 3 → 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** swapping a donor for an acceptor changes the count in a drawn pair: redo the count for the tautomer actually drawn, matching each donor H to an acceptor across the pair."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** G, T and U are mainly keto at physiological pH, and rare enols mispair; G·C has 3 H-bonds and A·T 2, counted as donor H facing acceptor N or O."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:50#1","pe4-cp:50#2"],"q":"An esterase's active-site aspartate uses its negatively charged side chain to hold a protonated histidine in place. Which mutation most likely preserves activity?","choices":["Asp → Glu","Asp → Ala","Asp → Lys","Asp → Phe"],"ans":0,"exp":"The job depends on the **negative carboxylate** pairing with the positive histidine. Glutamate keeps a –COO⁻, one CH₂ longer, so it is a **conservative substitution**. Alanine and phenylalanine are nonpolar and carry no charge, and lysine is positive, so it would repel the histidine.","how":{"ask":"What chemical feature of the aspartate side chain does the job, and which replacement keeps that feature?","know":"• **Conservative substitution**: replace a residue with one of similar chemistry (charge, polarity, size), usually keeping function.\n• At pH 7: Asp and Glu are −1 (carboxylates); Lys and Arg are +1; Ala and Phe are nonpolar and neutral.\n• Asp and Glu differ only by one CH₂ in the side chain.","steps":[{"text":"The key feature is the negative charge.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Glutamate also ends in a carboxylate, so the ion pair survives.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"glutamate"},{"text":"Alanine and phenylalanine have no charge, so the ion pair is lost.","strike":["Asp → Ala","Asp → Phe"]},{"text":"Lysine is positive at pH 7 and would repel the protonated histidine.","strike":["Asp → Lys"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp → Glu."}],"trap":"Choosing by size alone: the carboxylate charge, not the side-chain length, carries the function."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negatively charged side chain","note":"the carboxylate"},{"t":"protonated histidine","note":"positive partner"},{"t":"preserves activity","note":"conservative swap"}]},{"text":"**Name the job.** An Asp that pairs with a positive group needs a –COO⁻."},{"text":"**Keep the chemistry.** Glu is the only standard residue besides Asp with a side-chain carboxylate.","calc":["Asp: –CH₂–COO⁻","Glu: –CH₂–CH₂–COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Also:** swapping Asp for Ala, Lys or Phe loses the negative charge (Lys even reverses it), so activity is lost."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep an Asp's function, swap it for Glu, since both carry a carboxylate; Ala, Lys or Phe lose the negative charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-53a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:53#1","pe4-cp:53#2","pe4-cp:53#3"],"q":"An 8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV. How high must a 20 kg crate rise to match its energy (g = 9.8 m/s²)?","choices":["1.0 m","2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"],"ans":0,"exp":"**Set the capacitor's energy equal to mgh.** U = ½CV² = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F × (7.0 × 10³ V)² = 196 J. Then h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ (20 kg × 9.8 m/s²) = 196 ÷ 196 = **1.0 m**.","how":{"ask":"Find the capacitor's stored energy in joules, then find the height that gives the crate the same potential energy.","know":"• **Capacitor energy: U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts).\n• **Gravitational potential energy: U = mgh**, so h = U/(mg).\n• Prefixes: **μF = 10⁻⁶ F**, **kV = 10³ V**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert both prefixed values to SI units first.","hl":"8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV","calc":["C = 8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = 7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Compute the stored energy with U = ½CV².","calc":["V² = (7.0 × 10³)² = 4.9 × 10⁷ V²","U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = **196 J**"]},{"text":"Set U = mgh and solve for h.","hl":"20 kg crate","calc":["mg = 20 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 196 N","h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ 196 N = **1.0 m**"]},{"text":"2.0 m drops the ½ (CV² = 392 J). 1.0 × 10⁶ m leaves C in μF as if it were farads. 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m leaves V in kV as if it were volts.","strike":["2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 m."}],"trap":"Skipping a prefix: a missed 10⁻⁶ or a missed (10³)² moves the answer by a factor of a million."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 μF","note":"capacitance C"},{"t":"7.0 kV","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"20 kg","note":"mass m"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefixes.** Micro is 10⁻⁶ and kilo is 10³.","calc":["8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"**Capacitor energy:** U = ½CV². Square the voltage, exponent included.","calc":["U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = 196 J"]},{"text":"**Equal energies:** set ½CV² = mgh and solve for h.","calc":["h = ½CV²/(mg)","h = 196 ÷ (20 × 9.8) = 1.0 m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to match a capacitor's energy with a lifted mass, convert to F and V, compute U = ½CV², then h = U/(mg)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-54a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:54#1","pe4-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:54#3"],"q":"A 3.7 V drone battery is rated at 5.0 A·h. What total charge flows through it from full to empty?","choices":["5.0 C","300 C","1.8 × 10⁴ C","6.7 × 10⁴ C"],"ans":2,"exp":"Amp-hours measure **charge, since Q = I·t**. 1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C, so 5.0 A·h × 3600 C/(A·h) = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**. The 3.7 V is not needed; multiplying by it gives energy in joules.","how":{"ask":"Convert the amp-hour rating to coulombs.","know":"• **Q = I·t** (C = A × s).\n• **1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C**.\n• Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Amp-hours multiply current by time, which gives charge.","hl":"rated at 5.0 A·h","calc":["Q = I·t → A·h is a charge unit"]},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds and multiply.","calc":["1 h = 3600 s","Q = 5.0 A × 3600 s","Q = 18 000 C = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**"]},{"text":"The voltage plays no part in the charge.","hl":"3.7 V","calc":["3.7 V × 1.8 × 10⁴ C ≈ 6.7 × 10⁴ J: energy, not charge"]},{"text":"6.7 × 10⁴ C multiplies by the voltage, 300 C uses 60 s per hour, and 5.0 C skips the time conversion.","strike":["6.7 × 10⁴ C","300 C","5.0 C"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.8 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"Multiplying by the voltage: that gives energy in joules, not charge in coulombs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what they are.","marks":[{"t":"3.7 V","note":"voltage: not needed"},{"t":"5.0 A·h","note":"charge rating"},{"t":"What total charge","note":"answer in coulombs"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.** An amp-hour already is a charge.","calc":["Q = I·t"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert with 1 A·h = 3600 C.","calc":["1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C","5.0 A·h × 3600 C = 1.8 × 10⁴ C"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage doesn't matter for charge; it only enters if you want energy (E = QV)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = I·t, and 1 A·h = 3600 C; ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-55a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:55#1","pe4-cp:55#2"],"q":"A 75 g toy cart speeds up from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s in 0.40 s. Find the mean net force on the cart:","choices":["0.15 N","0.18 N","15 N","150 N"],"ans":0,"exp":"**F = mΔv/Δt** in SI units: m = 0.075 kg, Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = 0.80 m/s. F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s = **0.15 N**.","how":{"ask":"Convert to kg and m/s, then divide the momentum change by the time.","know":"• **F = ma = mΔv/Δt** (N = kg·m/s²).\n• **1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm/s = 10⁻² m/s**: convert before multiplying.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the mass and speeds to SI.","hl":"75 g","calc":["m = 75 g = **0.075 kg**","15 cm/s = 0.15 m/s; 95 cm/s = 0.95 m/s"]},{"text":"Find the change in velocity.","hl":"from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s","calc":["Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = **0.80 m/s**"]},{"text":"Apply F = mΔv/Δt.","hl":"0.40 s","calc":["F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s","F = 0.060 ÷ 0.40 = **0.15 N**"]},{"text":"0.18 N uses the final speed instead of the change; 15 N leaves speeds in cm/s; 150 N leaves mass in grams.","strike":["0.18 N","15 N","150 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.15 N."}],"trap":"Skipping a unit conversion: grams or cm/s each make the force off by a power of ten."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"75 g","note":"mass in grams"},{"t":"15 cm/s","note":"initial speed"},{"t":"95 cm/s","note":"final speed"},{"t":"0.40 s","note":"time interval"}]},{"text":"**Average force = mass × velocity change ÷ time.**","calc":["F = mΔv/Δt"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert to SI first: g → kg (÷ 1000) and cm/s → m/s (÷ 100).","calc":["75 g = 0.075 kg","Δv = 80 cm/s = 0.80 m/s"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["F = 0.075 × 0.80 ÷ 0.40 = 0.15 N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** F = mΔv/Δt with mass in kg and speeds in m/s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:56#1"],"q":"A solid aluminum block sits in a uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C. Once its charges stop moving, the field inside it is zero because:","choices":["mobile electrons rearrange.","the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."],"ans":0,"exp":"A metal's **free electrons** drift in the outside field and pile up on one face, leaving the other face positive. They keep moving until **their field exactly cancels** the outside one inside the metal: **electrostatic equilibrium**, E = 0 inside.","how":{"ask":"Explain what the free electrons do until they stop moving.","know":"• A conductor has **free electrons** that move whenever a field acts on them.\n• They collect on the **surface**, and their field opposes the applied one.\n• **Electrostatic equilibrium**: charges stopped, so E inside = 0 and any excess charge sits on the surface.","steps":[{"text":"The outside field pushes the block's free electrons toward one face.","hl":"uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C","calc":["electrons → one face (−)","other face left (+)"]},{"text":"Those surface charges make a field opposite to the applied one; motion stops only when the two cancel inside.","hl":"Once its charges stop moving","calc":["E(inside) = E(applied) − E(surface charges)","stop only when E(inside) = 0"]},{"text":"Metals don't absorb fields, aluminum has free electrons, and a neutral block still has free charges that separate.","strike":["the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."]},{"text":"Answer: mobile electrons rearrange, and their surface charge cancels the field inside."}],"trap":"Saying the metal blocks or absorbs the field: the zero is the result of induced surface charges cancelling it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"solid aluminum block","note":"a conductor: free electrons"},{"t":"Once its charges stop moving","note":"electrostatic equilibrium"},{"t":"field inside it is zero","note":"explain why"}]},{"text":"**Free electrons move in a field.** They drift until they reach the surface and pile up there."},{"text":"**Their field opposes the outside field.** If any field were left inside, electrons would keep moving.","calc":["E inside ≠ 0 → electrons move","electrons stop → E inside = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, free electrons move to the surface until their field cancels any field inside, so E = 0 there."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-58a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:58#2"],"q":"A motor neuron receives excitatory synaptic input. In the action potential that follows, which of these events occurs first?","choices":["Na⁺ influx","K⁺ efflux","Ca²⁺ influx","Hyperpolarization"],"ans":0,"exp":"Graded potentials first bring the **axon hillock to threshold**. Then the **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, letting Na⁺ rush in** (depolarization), K⁺ channels open and K⁺ leaves (repolarization), the membrane briefly hyperpolarizes, and only when the spike reaches the axon terminal does Ca²⁺ enter.","how":{"ask":"Put the four events in order, starting from the moment the hillock reaches threshold.","know":"Order of an action potential: (1) **graded potentials reach threshold at the axon hillock**; (2) **voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, Na⁺ in** (depolarization); (3) **voltage-gated K⁺ channels open, K⁺ out** (repolarization); (4) **hyperpolarization** (K⁺ channels slow to close). Later, at the **axon terminal**, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, Ca²⁺ enters, and neurotransmitter is released.","steps":[{"text":"The input is excitatory, so graded potentials spread to the axon hillock and bring it to threshold. That starts the spike.","hl":"excitatory synaptic input"},{"text":"Plot the spike at the hillock: rise, fall, dip, recover.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]},"calc":["−70 → −55 mV: graded potentials reach threshold","−55 → +35 mV: Na⁺ in (step 2)","+35 → −70 mV: K⁺ out (step 3)","below −70 mV: hyperpolarization (step 4)"]},{"text":"K⁺ leaves only after the Na⁺ upstroke, and the dip below rest comes after that.","strike":["K⁺ efflux","Hyperpolarization"]},{"text":"Ca²⁺ enters at the axon terminal, after the spike has traveled down the axon, to trigger neurotransmitter release.","strike":["Ca²⁺ influx"]},{"text":"Answer: Na⁺ influx."}],"trap":"Ca²⁺ influx sounds like an early trigger, but it happens last and far away, at the axon terminal, where it releases neurotransmitter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words.**","marks":[{"t":"excitatory synaptic input","note":"makes graded potentials"},{"t":"action potential","note":"the all-or-none spike"},{"t":"occurs first","note":"put the events in order"}]},{"text":"**Step 1, threshold:** small graded potentials add up at the axon hillock. At about −55 mV the spike fires.","calc":["rest ≈ −70 mV → threshold ≈ −55 mV"]},{"text":"**Step 2, depolarization:** voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open and Na⁺ rushes in (influx = moving in). **Step 3, repolarization:** voltage-gated K⁺ channels open and K⁺ flows out (efflux = moving out). **Step 4:** K⁺ channels close slowly, so the membrane dips below rest (hyperpolarization).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ influx and neurotransmitter release come later, at the axon terminal: the spike arrives, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, and Ca²⁺ triggers vesicle fusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** threshold at the hillock → Na⁺ in → K⁺ out → hyperpolarization; Ca²⁺ entry and transmitter release happen last, at the terminal."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-59a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:59#1","pe4-cp:59#2"],"q":"A 300 W heater warms 0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C. If no heat is lost, how long does this take?","choices":["0.20 s","72 s","200 s","272 s"],"ans":2,"exp":"**q = mcΔT** = 0.50 kg × 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C) × 50 °C = 60 kJ = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. **t = q/P** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J ÷ 300 J/s = **200 s**.","how":{"ask":"Find the heat the ethanol absorbs, convert it to joules, then divide by the heater's power.","know":"**q = mcΔT**, with m in the unit c uses (kJ/(kg·°C) takes kg; J/(g·°C) takes g) and **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. **P = q/t**, so **t = q/P**; 1 W = 1 J/s and 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Note the values; c is per kilogram, so the mass stays in kg.","hl":"0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C"},{"text":"Use the temperature change, not either reading: ΔT = 18 gives 72 s and ΔT = 68 gives 272 s.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["72 s","272 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","q = **60 kJ** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"Divide by the power, in joules per second.","hl":"300 W","calc":["t = q ÷ P","t = 60 000 J ÷ 300 J/s","t = **200 s**"]},{"text":"Dividing 60 kJ by 300 W without changing kJ to J gives a time 1000 times too short.","strike":["0.20 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 s."}],"trap":"Mixing kJ with W (J/s): 60 ÷ 300 = 0.20 s. Convert kJ to J first. Using 18 or 68 as ΔT gives 72 s or 272 s."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"300 W","note":"power: 300 J each second"},{"t":"0.50 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)","note":"per kg, so keep kg"},{"t":"from 18 °C to 68 °C","note":"start → end"}]},{"text":"**Match the units:** c is in kJ per kg per °C, so the mass goes in as 0.50 kg; no gram conversion.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","0.50 × 2.4 = 1.2","1.2 × 50 = **60 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Time from power: t = q/P.** Power is in W = J/s, so change kJ to J first.","calc":["60 kJ = 60 000 J","t = 60 000 ÷ 300 = **200 s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT with the mass in the unit c uses, then t = q ÷ P with q in joules (1 W = 1 J/s)."}]},
];
